ring operation. Training data provenance supports traceability which underpins these, but doesn't directly implement them.\n- Article 12(3): Specific to Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics). Not directly addressed here.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses the \"technical documentation kept up to date\" requirement from Recital 71\n- Supports traceability of how systems were developed (a core purpose stated in Recital 71)\n- Automation ensures consistency and reduces risk of human error/omission in record-keeping\n- Dataset hashes provide tamper-evidence for data provenance\n- Covers \"training, testing and validation processes\" as mentioned in Recital 71\n\nWeaknesses/Gaps:\n- The proposal is narrowly focused on TRAINING metadata. Article 12(1) specifically requires automatic recording of events over the LIFETIME of the system, which includes operational/runtime logs. This proposal doesn't address runtime event logging (which is the primary focus of Article 12).\n- It doesn't explicitly address the specific logging requirements in Article 12(2)(a)-(c) for risk identification and post-market monitoring.\n- \"Every epoch's parameters\" could be extremely data-intensive — for large models, this could create enormous storage challenges. But from a compliance perspective, more thorough records are better than fewer.\n- The proposal says \"for all high-risk models\" which is good scope.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This is a strong foundational component of the record-keeping obligation\n- It addresses the technical documentation requirement (which underpins Article 12's logging requirements)\n- However, it's NOT sufficient on its own to satisfy Article 12 — it doesn't cover operational/runtime event logging\n- But as a standalone proposal, it does address a real and important compliance gap: ensuring training records are maintained automatically\n\nThe question is whether this clears my bar. As a Compliance Officer concerned