ctured way — is sound and valuable for compliance. However:\n\n1. The \"automated verification\" framing is dangerous from a compliance perspective. I need human judgment loops, not full automation of legal interpretation.\n2. The EU AI Act's implementing regulations aren't finalized yet. Building this now on the bare text risks rework.\n3. As a compliance function, I would want this to be one tool in my toolkit, not the primary mechanism for ensuring compliance.\n4. The resource commitment is significant and the payoff timeline (high-risk obligations kick in 2026-2027) means we'd need this operational before then.\n\nHowever, the fundamental problem it solves is real: how do you systematically verify that your technical architecture actually meets a legal requirement that's phrased at a high level? A structured mapping does help with audit readiness, internal review, and demonstrating diligence to regulators.\n\nI think this clears my bar but with reservations. The core concept (structured mapping of legal requirements to technical controls) is something I would want in my compliance toolkit. The \"automated verification\" framing needs to be tempered to \"assisted verification with human review,\" but the proposal as stated still points in a direction I'd support.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The key question: does this clear MY bar from MY vantage point? \n\n- Institutional survival: YES, having systematic compliance verification reduces existential regulatory risk\n- Regulatory compliance: PARTIALLY - it helps operationalize requirements but the \"automated\" aspect could be a liability if over-relied upon\n- Market reputation: YES, being recognized for compliant architecture is valuable\n\nThe proposal as stated has the right direction. The risks (premature codification before implementing regulations, over-reliance on automation) are execution risks that can be managed in implementation. The core value proposition — structured mapping between law and techni