bly trigger on \"high-uncertainty or risk-prone operational regimes\" is an open research problem in interpretability/ML safety. This could consume enormous resources with uncertain timelines.\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) a practical engineering task of implementing logging infrastructure that meets Article 12, and (2) a deep research program into model interpretability and failure modes. The scientific ambition is high but the regulatory requirement doesn't actually demand this level of sophistication for most systems.\n- \"Advances the scientific understanding of failure modes\" - this is a nice co-benefit but it's not what Article 12 requires. The regulation asks for logs that enable traceability, post-market monitoring, and identifying risk situations. It doesn't require you to understand *why* a model fails at the activation level.\n- For most high-risk AI systems under Annex III (biometrics, critical infrastructure, employment, education, etc.), the logging requirements in Article 12(3) are quite specific and concrete (timestamps, reference databases, input data, natural person IDs). The \"semantic\" layer is an enhancement but not a requirement.\n- From resource allocation: if we commit to this research track, we're diverting engineers away from the concrete implementation tasks that actually satisfy Article 12(3) for our specific systems. The practical path to compliance is clear; this proposal adds a research layer on top that has uncertain payoff and timeline.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe idea is scientifically interesting and could eventually be valuable, but as framed it's a research exploration (\"research into defining,\" \"aims to identify,\" \"involves designing new triggers\"). The lab cannot pursue everything, and approving means committing scarce implementation resources. The regulatory requirement for Article 12 can be met with more straightforward engineering: structured event logging, timestamps, input/output capture, model version t