latory — we need post-market monitoring capability.\n- But the specific proposal as framed (\"build a dashboard,\" \"pre-defined risk matrix,\" custom anomaly detection) seems like over-engineering for what could be handled with existing infrastructure plus targeted logging fields.\n- From resource allocation: this competes with our core R&D work for engineering bandwidth. A Lab Director would want to minimize the engineering footprint of compliance while ensuring we're compliant.\n- The proposal doesn't leverage \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" — it builds new tooling.\n\nI think from my vantage point, I'd lean toward rejecting this as proposed because:\n1. It's a heavy custom build when lighter approaches (repurposing existing observability, adding structured log fields with simple alerting) could achieve the same compliance outcome.\n2. It consumes engineering resources that should go toward our scientific mission.\n3. The \"pre-defined risk matrix\" and anomaly detection layer adds complexity that may not be proportionate to what the regulation actually requires (the regulation requires logs to exist and enable traceability — it doesn't mandate a fancy anomaly-detection dashboard).\n\nHowever, I acknowledge there IS a legitimate need here. The promise score should reflect that while I reject this specific implementation, the underlying capability is needed. Maybe a 40-50? It addresses a real requirement but in an inefficient way from my resource-allocation perspective. Actually, let me reconsider — the proposal says \"The lab will build a dashboard\" which is the expensive part. If we could do this with existing tools at lower cost, I'd approve that version. As written, it's a net-new engineering project for compliance monitoring.\n\nI'll reject it but give it moderate promise since the underlying need is real and regulatory — just not in this implementation form from my resource-efficiency perspective. Maybe 45? It's something we'll need to do e