r than retrofitting, it's far more feasible. A pre-development gate ensures this.\n\n5. **The \"unnecessary application\" language**: The proposal frames it as preventing \"unnecessary application of restrictive record-keeping burdens to purely exploratory, low-risk frontier research.\" This is a double-edged sword from my perspective. On one hand, I don't want us over-complying on basic research (which would waste resources and potentially trigger unnecessary regulatory scrutiny). On the other hand, I need to be careful that this framework doesn't become a tool for under-classification — i.e., labeling something as \"exploratory\" to avoid Article 12 obligations when it's clearly heading toward a high-risk deployment. The peer-review committee structure with documented evaluation against Annex III criteria mitigates this risk somewhat, but I'd want the process to be robust.\n\n6. **The central registry**: This is excellent from a compliance perspective. It creates an auditable trail of classification decisions, which is exactly what a regulator would want to see if they investigate whether we correctly identified our obligations.\n\nOverall, this is a strong compliance control that directly supports Article 12 implementation by ensuring the right systems get the right logging capabilities built in at the right time. The main risk I'd flag is potential under-classification (gaming the \"exploratory\" label), but the structured peer-review and documented process mitigates that significantly.\n\nThis clears my bar decisively. It's a foundational governance mechanism without which Article 12 compliance would be ad hoc and reactive rather than systematic and preventive.\n\nPromise score: This is very high value for compliance. I'd say around 85-90. It's not a 100 because the proposal as stated could potentially be gamed (labeling things \"exploratory\" to avoid obligations), and it doesn't explicitly address what happens when a project transitions from exploratory to de