rs and enterprise customers who ask about compliance readiness.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. The proposal focuses on Recital 71 (technical documentation) but doesn't explicitly address Article 12's logging requirements (automatic recording of events/logs). These are related but distinct obligations. However, the proposal is specifically scoped to technical documentation, so this isn't necessarily a deficiency - it just means it addresses one part of the record-keeping regime.\n2. \"Mandatory update\" triggered by architecture/training set changes might miss other relevant changes (e.g., changes in deployment environment, new data sources not captured in training set changes, changes to risk management processes). The trigger mechanism could be too narrow.\n3. Git-based workflow is a practical implementation choice but the proposal doesn't specify how it handles the \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement from Recital 71 - just because something is version-controlled doesn't mean it's comprehensible to a regulator.\n4. The scope seems limited to high-risk systems (which is appropriate since the Act applies to those), but there's no mention of how this integrates with Article 12's logging requirements or Article 72's post-market monitoring documentation needs.\n\nOverall assessment:\nThis is a solid, well-targeted proposal that addresses a genuine compliance obligation. From my vantage point as Compliance Officer:\n- It directly supports regulatory compliance (Recital 71's \"up to date\" requirement)\n- The version control and review gate reduce enforcement risk\n- It's practical and implementable (Git-based workflows are well-understood in engineering)\n- It protects institutional survival by ensuring we can demonstrate documentation currency to regulators\n- Market reputation benefit: enterprise clients increasingly ask about AI compliance posture\n\nThe trigger mechanism being potentially narrow is a concern but not disqualifying - it can be expanded during implementation. The focus on Recital 71 rather than Article 12's logging is fine since they're separate obligations and this proposal appropriately scopes itself.\n\nI would approve this. It's a clear compliance obligation that needs to be addressed, the approach is sound, and it directly protects the institution from regulatory risk.\n\nPromise score: This is strong from my vantage point. It addresses a core requirement (documentation currency), has a practical implementation path, includes a review gate for compliance sign-off, and uses version control which creates an audit trail. The main gaps are minor scoping issues (trigger mechanism could be broader) but the overall direction is correct and necessary. I'd give it around 78-82. It's not a 90+ because the trigger mechanism is narrow and there's no explicit mention of how this integrates with Article 12 logging or post-market monitoring documentation, but it's clearly important and well-conceived for what it addresses.\n\nI'll go with 80.\n",
