speeds up prototyping - they get compliance-relevant telemetry \"for free\" by using the standard pipeline.\n- PRO: Reduces redundant effort across projects, which means engineers spend time on actual model development rather than plumbing.\n- CON: There's a risk that a one-size-fits-all framework becomes a bottleneck. If researchers need to log something non-standard for their specific prototype, they might be forced into an awkward abstraction or wait for the platform team to extend the library. This could slow prototyping for novel/experimental work.\n- CON: \"Mandatory CI/CD pipeline\" integration means every prototype must go through this framework before it can be deployed/tested. If the framework has bugs, performance issues, or is under-maintained, it becomes a single point of failure that blocks all prototyping activity.\n- PRO: Standardized data formats mean that when you do need to debug a prototype or analyze logs across projects, everything is consistent and tooling can be shared.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is fundamentally sound infrastructure engineering. It's the kind of thing that should exist in any serious R&D institution doing high-risk AI work. The technical approach (modular API, CI/CD integration, standardized formats) is well-established practice. From my vantage point:\n- Technical viability: HIGH - this is proven software architecture pattern\n- Impact on prototyping speed: NET POSITIVE but with some caveats about potential rigidity for novel research\n\nThe main concern from a pure \"speed of prototyping\" angle is the mandatory nature and the risk of it becoming a bottleneck if the platform team can't keep up with diverse prototype needs. But overall, having a standardized logging layer means researchers don't waste weeks building custom telemetry for each project. That's a net positive for speed.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's technically viable, well-scoped, and should improve prototyping velocity by removing repetitive infra