esses. Auto-generating or prompting updates when risk inputs change requires some workflow design (e.g., a form that must be filled before merging a PR that changes model architecture). But this is process engineering, not technical impossibility.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n\n- This actually *helps* prototyping speed rather than hindering it, as the proposal claims. Instead of a compliance engineer manually updating documentation after every release (which slows teams down and creates bottlenecks), the pipeline does it automatically. The \"mandatory update\" trigger could be implemented as a CI gate — if the doc isn't updated/validated, the build fails or the release is blocked. This is standard practice in regulated industries (pharma, aerospace) and has well-proven patterns.\n\n- From a prototyping standpoint, the overhead on developers is minimal: they push code, the pipeline regenerates the relevant sections of the dossier. The \"living document\" aspect means we don't have to do big-bang documentation updates before each release candidate.\n\n**Potential concerns (from my vantage point):**\n- The risk assessment part is where it gets fuzzy. Automated generation of *meaningful* risk assessments isn't really automatable in a meaningful sense — you can prompt for an update, but the content still requires human judgment. However, the proposal says \"prompts a mandatory update,\" which is reasonable — it's a trigger/gate, not full automation of the assessment itself.\n- Scope creep risk: trying to sync ALL three (model versioning, dataset changes, risk assessments) in one pipeline might be over-scoped for an initial prototype. But as an architecture, it's sound.\n- The \"living technical documentation file\" needs a clear schema that maps to Annex IV requirements. This is a design task but not a viability blocker.\n\n**Article 12 relevance:**\nThe proposal directly addresses Recital 71's requirement that \"technical documentation should be kept up to date