hanism, but the real compliance risk is about *what* gets logged and *how*, not just whether there's money in the pot. A funded line item without specific technical requirements tied to it could still produce non-compliant logging.\n   - \"Traceability engineering\" is somewhat vague. Article 12 requires specific capabilities: automatic event recording over lifetime, traceability appropriate to intended purpose, and for Annex III 1(a) systems, very specific data points (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, person identification). The proposal doesn't address whether the funded work will actually meet these specifics.\n   - That said, as a *resource allocation* mechanism within an R&D institution, having dedicated funding for compliance engineering is structurally sound. It prevents the common failure mode where \"compliance\" becomes whoever has spare time.\n\n3. **Institutional survival angle:** If we don't fund this work properly and a high-risk system ships without adequate logging capabilities, that's a clear violation of Article 12(1) — a mandatory \"shall.\" Enforcement could mean fines (up to €15M or 3% for prohibited practices; up to €7.5M or 1.4% for other violations under Art 99), market withdrawal orders, and severe reputational damage. Having dedicated funding reduces this risk.\n\n4. **Market reputation:** Being known as an institution that properly funds compliance infrastructure rather than treating it as an afterthought is a positive signal to EU regulators, business partners, and customers. It shows institutional seriousness.\n\n**Decision:** I lean toward approve. The core idea — dedicating funded resources specifically for compliance engineering so it doesn't compete with research budgets — is structurally sound from a compliance risk management perspective. While the mechanism (fixed percentage) could be refined, the principle addresses a real and common failure mode that would leave us exposed to Article 12 violations. It's 