t (automatic recording of events) and technical documentation is another. The proposal focuses on the technical documentation side - extracting experiment data into structured reports. This is legitimate but it's not the core of Article 12(1)-(3), which is about automatic event logging during operation.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- Using MLflow/W&B (which we likely already have or are cheap to adopt) rather than building a custom documentation system is sensible.\n- The automation aspect means one-time investment in the pipeline with ongoing savings in analyst/engineering time.\n- However, \"automatically linked to specific model versions in our registry\" implies we need a functioning model registry, which may or may not exist yet.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is the strongest selling point. The proposal explicitly says it \"transforms our existing R&D logs into the formal records.\" It's repurposing what we already track (hyperparameters, dataset hashes, validation results) rather than creating new tracking obligations.\n- MLflow and W&B are standard tools in modern ML labs. If we're already using them, this is a thin integration layer.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal addresses technical documentation generation but Article 12's core mandate (automatic event logging during system operation - start/end times, reference databases for biometric matching systems) is not directly addressed by an experiment-tracking pipeline. An experiment tracker captures training/validation data; it doesn't capture runtime events like \"when the system was invoked in production,\" \"what input data led to a match,\" or \"who verified the results.\"\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification), the logging requirements are very specific operational logs, not training documentation. MLflow/W&B won't naturally produce those unless specifically instrumented for runtime use.\n- The proposal may be solvin