        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2730 is about creating an \"Open AI Research Pedigree Standard\" - a standardized, machine-readable schema for documenting the lineage of high-risk AI models and their training data, in collaboration with other EU institutions.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Creating a machine-readable schema is technically straightforward in principle - JSON Schema, YAML, or similar formats exist. However, \"standardizing\" across institutions is a massive coordination challenge, not an engineering one.\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) building internal logging capabilities that comply with Article 12, and (2) creating an inter-institutional standard for documentation format. These are very different scopes of work.\n- Article 12 specifically requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - this is about runtime event capture, not just static metadata schemas. The proposal focuses on documenting \"lineage\" and \"training data\" which is more about technical documentation (Article 11) than the operational logging that Article 12 actually mandates.\n- The schema would need to handle: start/end timestamps of each use, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification. These are operational/dynamic fields, not just static lineage metadata.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Collaborating with \"other EU institutions\" to define a common format is an extremely slow process. Standards bodies, working groups, consensus building - this could take years before we have anything actionable.\n- This does NOT accelerate our prototyping. In fact, waiting for or contributing to a cross-institutional standard would delay our own implementation of logging capabilities.\n- The \"global impact\" and \"scientific transparency\" language is aspirational but doesn't address the core engineering challenge: how do we actually build the automatic event recording into our systems NOW?\n- There's no mention of what specific technical architecture we'd use, what integration points exist in current pipelines, or any concrete prototyping path.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The proposal is fundamentally a standards/coordination project, not an engineering implementation project. As a Dev Engineer, I need to build logging into our systems - this proposal doesn't get us there faster.\n2. Cross-institutional collaboration adds enormous coordination overhead with no guaranteed timeline or deliverable that helps my team prototype.\n3. It misidentifies the core technical challenge of Article 12 (runtime automatic event recording) as a metadata/documentation schema problem.\n4. \"Open\" and \"shared research\" language suggests this is more of a policy/academic exercise than an engineering solution.\n5. No mention of backward compatibility, performance overhead of logging at scale, data retention infrastructure, or how logs integrate with existing monitoring stacks.\n\nThis proposal would consume my team's attention on coordination and format debates rather than building the actual automatic logging infrastructure that Article 12(1) demands. From a prototyping speed perspective, it's actively counterproductive - we could be shipping a v0.1 logging layer in weeks if we focused on engineering, but this proposal sends us into multi-year standards processes.\n\nDecision: Reject. The promise score should reflect that while the idea of interoperability has some long-term value, from my narrow vantage point (technical viability NOW and prototyping speed), it's essentially irrelevant to the immediate engineering task and could even be a distraction. I'd give it maybe a 15-20 - there's a kernel of useful thinking about machine-readable formats, but as framed, this is not an engineering proposal that advances our implementation.\n",
