g frameworks, code repositories, pipeline tools) and adds a specialized layer on top.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- The scope is well-defined and bounded — it's specifically about Article 12 logging capabilities, not the entire AI Act. This makes it tractable.\n- \"Simulate high-risk events\" requires some domain knowledge about what those events look like for each system type (Annex III point 1(a) biometric systems vs. other categories). But this is manageable.\n- The proposal says \"before deployment\" which is the right checkpoint — catching issues early in the pipeline.\n- It's a tool, not a research program. It doesn't compete with scientific ambition for resources; it protects the lab from compliance failures that would waste much more resources.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a high-value, low-cost infrastructure investment that directly addresses a regulatory requirement in an efficient way. It scales well across projects, integrates with existing pipelines, and reduces risk without consuming significant ongoing resources. From my vantage point of resource efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure, this clears the bar easily.\n\nPromise score: This is very aligned with my priorities. It's efficient, uses existing infrastructure, has bounded scope, and delivers continuous value. I'd give it a high score — maybe 82-88. It's not a 100 because there are some execution risks (ensuring the simulated events actually cover all regulatory edge cases, maintaining the test suite as regulations or systems evolve), but from my vantage point it's strongly favorable.\n\nLet me settle on 84.\n",
