eal, the intersection is scientifically interesting, and if the team identifies genuinely novel technical challenges (e.g., proving properties of stochastic AI event streams with ZKPs, or doing MPC across heterogeneous logging schemas), there could be real scientific contribution. The broader impact potential is meaningful.\n\nI think this sits at a moderate level. It's not a clear reject — the problem space is legitimate and the intersection has frontier elements. But it's also not a clear approve from my vantage point because the intellectual depth and specific knowledge-frontier contribution are under-specified. It reads more like an application project with research aspirations than a commitment to pushing a scientific boundary.\n\nI'll lean toward a moderate-to-low promise score. The problem is real but the proposal doesn't clearly demonstrate it will advance the knowledge frontier in a meaningful way versus being an applied systems exercise. I'd say around 45-55 range. Let me settle on something like 48 or 50.\n\nDecision: This is borderline. From my vantage point specifically (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), the proposal as stated doesn't clearly demonstrate a commitment to advancing the scientific frontier in a way that distinguishes it from applied engineering work. The intellectual merit is uncertain due to vagueness. I'll reject it — not because the problem isn't important, but because as proposed, it doesn't meet my bar for intellectual merit and knowledge-frontier advancement. It's an application of well-known tools to a new domain without articulating what novel scientific questions will be addressed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The tension between Article 12 logging requirements (which require detailed event logs including input data, reference databases, etc.) and GDPR minimization/access principles is genuinely at the frontier of how we think about regulatory architecture for AI. If the work produces new metho