        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is \"P1785: Unified Compliance Logging Middleware\" - a standardized internal software library for automated event logging across all high-risk AI projects, built by the core engineering team as a plug-and-play middleware layer.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A unified logging middleware is technically very feasible. Event logging with timestamps, input metadata capture is well-understood software engineering. The \"plug-and-play\" aspect is the key technical challenge - can you really make a single middleware work across diverse AI systems (different model architectures, different data pipelines, different deployment environments)? This is ambitious but not infeasible.\n- The scope described is relatively narrow: \"captures timestamps and input metadata.\" This is achievable with standard logging patterns (structured logging, AOP-like interception points).\n- However, Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires more specific things: recording of reference databases used, input data that matched, identification of natural persons in verification. A \"one-size-fits-all\" middleware may not cover all these domain-specific requirements without significant customization. But the proposal frames it as a baseline layer that captures the common elements (timestamps, input metadata), which is reasonable.\n- The risk: over-engineering a generic solution that ends up being too rigid or too flexible, requiring per-project configuration that negates the \"plug-and-play\" promise. This is a known challenge in middleware design.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- PRO: If successful, this directly accelerates prototyping for high-risk AI projects because researcher