        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2918\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core architecture—an inference-time middleware/API wrapper that intercepts calls to capture inputs, timestamps, and system state—is well-trodden MLOps territory and technically straightforward; the modular-wrapper design means teams can adopt it incrementally per model without a big-bang migration, which protects prototyping velocity. My main reservations are (a) \\\"secure, immutable ledger\\\" risks over-specifying storage (hash-chained append-only logs in object storage would satisfy Art. 12's tamper-evidence need at a fraction of the operational and latency cost of a blockchain-style ledger), and (b) enforcing a single uniform interface across heterogeneous model architectures (vision, LLM, tabular classifiers) may introduce abstraction friction that slows early prototyping until the framework matures; but these are solvable within the build rather than fundamental blockers.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2918 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal: \"Automated Traceability Framework for High-Risk Models\" - a standardized software layer that automatically captures input data, timestamps, and system states during inference for every high-risk model. It includes a modular API wrapper logging to a secure immutable ledger, open-sourced for community verification.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A standardized logging layer/API wrapper is technically feasible. This is essentially building an inference-time observability/logging middleware. We've done similar things with request tracing, feature stores, etc. The core idea of wrapping model inference calls to capture inputs, timestamps, and system states is well-understood engineering.\n- \"Secure, immutable ledger\" - this introduces complexity. What kind of ledger? A blockchain? An append-only database? WORM storage? If they mean blockchain, that adds significant latency and operational overhead for what is essentially an audit log. For most Article 12 purposes, a tamper-evident append-only store (e.g., signed logs in object storage with hash chaining) would suffice. The term \"immutable ledger\" could be over-engineering unless there's a specific need.\n- \"Modular API wrapper\" - this is sound. Wrapping inference calls to inject logging is architecturally clean and doesn't require modifying model code.\n- Open-sourcing it for community verification is fine but adds maintenance burden and security review overhead. Not strictly necessary for Article 12 compliance but could be a nice-to-have.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A single unified framework across all high-risk models is attractive from an engineering standpoint - one codebase to maintain, consistent logging format. This reduces per-project setup time.\n- However, \"for every high-risk model we deploy\" and \"integrated across all our high-risk project pipelines\" suggests a large upfront investment before any team can start using it. The prototyping speed benefit only materializes AFTER the framework is built. There's a risk of building a one-size-fits-all layer that doesn't fit well with heterogeneous architectures (different model types, different serving stacks, batch vs. real-time).\n- \"Uniform logging interface across different architectures\" - this is the key technical challenge. Different models (vision, LLMs, structured data classifiers) have very different input/output structures. A truly uniform interface requires abstraction that might lose information or be too rigid.\n- The proposal mentions \"reducing the manual burden on researchers during the development phase\" which aligns well with my concern about prototyping speed - if logging is automated and invisible to the researcher, they can iterate faster without worrying about compliance overhead.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Scope: This is a significant infrastructure project. It's not a small prototype. The \"framework\" language suggests a multi-month (or longer) build-out before any value is delivered.\n- The immutable ledger requirement might be over-specified for the actual regulatory need. Article 12 requires logging capabilities, not necessarily blockchain-grade immutability. This could slow down prototyping if engineers are constrained by an overly rigid storage layer during development.\n- \"Integrated across all our high-risk project pipelines\" - this implies a large integration effort that could block teams while they wait for the framework to be ready and integrated into their specific stacks.\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- The core idea (automated logging middleware) is technically sound and well-motivated.\n- Reducing manual burden directly helps prototyping speed.\n- A modular API wrapper approach is architecturally clean - it can be adopted incrementally per model/project without requiring a big-bang migration.\n- Capturing the specific data points Article 12 requires (timestamps, inputs, system states) is straightforward engineering.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a technical viability standpoint, this is very doable. The core concept is well-established in MLOps/observability tooling. From a prototyping speed standpoint, it has both upside (once built, researchers don't have to think about logging) and downside (the build-out period could be long, and the \"one framework for all\" approach might not fit every architecture cleanly). The modular API wrapper design mitigates some of that risk.\n\nI'd say this is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It's technically feasible, addresses a real compliance need in a way that actually helps engineers (automated = less manual overhead), and the modular design allows incremental adoption. My main concern is the scope/lead time before it delivers value to prototyping teams, but the \"modular API wrapper\" framing suggests it could be built incrementally.\n\nI'll approve this with a moderately high promise score - maybe 72-78. It's technically sound and aligns well with my mandate, but the large upfront investment and potential architectural rigidity keep it from being a 90+.\n",
