iance as a driver, which is fine for motivation, but the intellectual content isn't clearly articulated.\n- There's an interesting tension: detecting whether a system has undergone \"substantial modification\" or is presenting risks requires understanding what the system *should* be doing vs. what it IS doing. This involves questions about specification, intent verification, and potentially alignment — which are genuinely hard research problems.\n- But the proposal doesn't really articulate these deeper questions. It reads more like a standard MLOps/observability engineering project dressed up in regulatory language.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:**\n- If done well, methods for detecting drift, modification, and risk-triggering events in AI systems could have broader applicability beyond compliance — to general AI safety monitoring, deployment reliability, etc.\n- However, as framed, the scope is narrow (specifically tied to Article 12 compliance) and the impact would be primarily regulatory/engineering rather than scientific.\n- The \"competitive advantage\" framing suggests the primary driver is commercial, not scientific.\n\n**Overall assessment from a researcher's perspective:**\nThis proposal reads more like an applied engineering/compliance project than a research contribution that advances the knowledge frontier. While there are interesting underlying questions (what constitutes anomalous behavior in AI systems, how to detect substantial modifications), the proposal as stated doesn't articulate a genuine research contribution. It says \"implement anomaly detection algorithms\" which suggests applying existing tools rather than developing new methods.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: if this is framed as a research project that genuinely explores open questions about detecting drift, modification, and risk in complex AI systems (not just bolting on a standard anomaly detector), it could have merit. But the text as written doesn't make that case strongly. It lean