evelop\" this - this is a resource commitment question, but from a compliance standpoint, having a dedicated, accountable implementation is good. However, I should note that the framework must be adaptable to different project types (different high-risk categories may need different logging).\n2. The mention of \"diverse environments\" is vague - compliance requires specific guarantees about data integrity, retention periods, and access controls for logs. The proposal doesn't address these.\n3. It mentions capturing \"timestamps, input data, and user IDs\" but Article 12(3)(b) specifically requires logging \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" This is a specific technical requirement that generic middleware might not capture without project-specific configuration.\n4. The framework must ensure logs are tamper-proof and retained for the lifetime of the system (Article 12(1)). The proposal doesn't explicitly address log integrity or retention.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\n- This is a strong alignment with Article 12's core mandate. Automatic logging is not optional; it's a \"shall\" requirement.\n- Standardization reduces institutional risk: if each project builds its own logging, we have N points of potential non-compliance rather than one.\n- Market reputation: being able to demonstrate a systematic, institution-wide approach to compliance logging is a positive signal to regulators and market partners.\n- Institutional survival: non-compliance with Article 12 could result in fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices; up to €15M or 3% for other violations including high-risk system requirements). A systematic approach reduces this risk.\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated is somewhat high-level. It doesn't specify:\n- Log retention policies aligned with \"lifetime of the system\"\n- Tamper-evident mechanisms\n- Specific handling of Annex III point 1(a) requirements (reference database