e perspective:**\n1. The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input data, and model versioning\" but Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.) requires specific things: reference database, input data leading to match, identification of natural persons involved in verification. The proposal doesn't explicitly address these granular requirements - though it says \"comprehensive metadata capture\" which could encompass them.\n2. Article 12(2)(a) requires logging events relevant for identifying risk situations and substantial modifications. The proposal mentions \"event recording\" but doesn't explicitly call out risk-relevant event classification. However, a well-designed framework would include this.\n3. Article 12(2)(b) and (c) require facilitating post-market monitoring and monitoring operation per Article 26(5). The proposal's \"audit-ready traceability\" language suggests it covers this.\n4. \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - the proposal doesn't explicitly address retention duration, but as a middleware framework, it would need to handle data lifecycle management.\n5. The scope says \"all high-risk projects\" which is good - comprehensive coverage.\n\n**Compliance Officer assessment:**\nThis is a strong, well-aligned proposal. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) (automatic recording), supports traceability purposes in Article 12(2), and provides institutional consistency that protects against per-project compliance failures. From my vantage point of institutional survival and regulatory compliance:\n\n- This is foundational infrastructure for demonstrating compliance\n- A unified approach means one system to audit, one standard to maintain\n- It proactively addresses the \"technically allow\" language - building it in rather than bolting on\n- It protects market reputation by ensuring consistent compliance posture across all products\n- The \"audit-ready\" framing shows regulatory awareness\n\nThe main g