al doesn't address the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) which are very particular (reference database, input data that led to a match, identification of natural persons verifying results). A generic telemetry service capturing \"timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" doesn't obviously cover all of these. You'd need domain-specific extensions.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping assessment:**\n- PRO: Once built, new projects get compliance logging \"for free\" via the library. This is a significant speed-up for prototyping because you don't have to solve logging from scratch each time.\n- PRO: CI/CD integration means you catch missing/incomplete logging early in development rather than at audit time.\n- CON: Building this framework itself is a substantial engineering investment before any project benefits. For an R&D lab that needs fast prototyping, this upfront cost could delay initial projects.\n- CON: If the framework is too rigid or has high latency overhead (centralized database writes on every event), it could slow down iterative development and testing cycles. Real-time telemetry into a \"secure database\" during rapid prototyping could be a performance bottleneck.\n- CON: The proposal doesn't address how this interacts with model experimentation loops where you might be running thousands of inference calls per minute during hyperparameter tuning or data exploration. Logging every single call into a secure centralized DB could be impractical at that scale.\n\n**Overall from my vantage point:**\nThe core idea is technically sound and aligns with good software engineering (DRY, cross-cutting concerns). The CI/CD integration is smart. However:\n1. The scope (\"all high-risk AI projects\") without acknowledging the heterogeneity of model architectures and data flows is somewhat naive.\n2. It doesn't clearly map to the specific Article 12(3) requirements (reference database, match data, verification person IDs) — it's more generic telemetry than compliance-specific logging.\n3. The \"secure database\" centralization could be a prototyping bottleneck during active development phases.\n4. It's a solid infrastructure investment but doesn't address the hardest technical problems in the regulation (how to automatically log \"situations that may result in a risk\" per 12(2)(a) — that requires domain-specific event detection, not just timestamp recording).\n\nI think this is a reasonably good proposal with clear technical merit. It's not groundbreaking but it's practical and would genuinely reduce friction. I'd approve it but with moderate confidence because of the gaps in addressing the specific regulatory requirements and potential prototyping overhead during active development.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 62-68. It's technically viable, addresses a real need, but has scope issues and doesn't fully map to the regulation's specifics. The centralized DB could slow prototyping. But as an architectural foundation, it's sound.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 65.\n",
