        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns are: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2143 is about a \"Privacy-Preserving Identity Logging Framework\" that uses zero-knowledge proofs or secure hashing to log human verification without storing raw personal identifiers, targeting Article 12(3)(d) which requires \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results.\"\n\nLet me think carefully about this from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Article 12(3)(d)** requires: \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results, as referred to in Article 14(5).\"\n\nThe key word here is \"identification.\" The regulation explicitly says you must be able to IDENTIFY the natural persons. This means you need to know WHO verified the results. If you use zero-knowledge proofs or cryptographic commitments that only prove someone with certain credentials performed a verification but don't allow you to identify WHICH specific person did it, this may NOT satisfy Article 12(3)(d).\n\nLet me think more carefully:\n\n- The requirement is \"identification of the natural persons involved in the verification.\" This implies you need to be able to say \"Person X verified at time Y.\" A ZKP that proves \"a verified employee performed this action\" without revealing which employee does NOT meet the identification standard. It meets a non-repudiation or authentication standard, but not an identification standard.\n\n- Secure hashing of identifiers: If you hash a person's name/ID with a fixed salt and store it, you could theoretically reverse-map (with the salt) to identify the person. But if the salt is lost or if you use a one-way function without a lookup table, you lose the ability to identify. This is risky for compliance because in a regulatory audit or incident investigation, the authority would need to be able to trace back to specific individuals.\n\n- The tension between GDPR data minimization and AI Act traceability is real, but the resolution isn't necessarily to obscure identity. GDPR allows processing of personal data where necessary for compliance with legal obligations (Article 6(1)(c) GDPR). The AI Act IS a legal obligation that requires identification logging. So you CAN store identifiers under GDPR's legal obligation exemption - you just need appropriate security measures and limited retention periods.\n\n- From a compliance risk standpoint: If an incident occurs (a high-risk system causes harm), the regulatory authority will demand to know WHO verified what and when. A ZKP-based system that can't produce identifiable records would be a critical compliance failure. This is not a \"nice to have\" optimization - it's a fundamental requirement.\n\n- The proposal frames this as an \"investigation\" and \"prototype,\" which suggests exploration rather than commitment. But the lab's rule says approving means committing implementation resources. So even framing it as exploratory, if I approve, I'm committing resources.\n\n- Market reputation: If we implement a logging system that a regulatory authority deems insufficient for identification purposes, we'd face enforcement action, fines (up to €35M or 7% global turnover for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for other violations including Article 12 non-compliance), and reputational damage.\n\n- However, there IS a legitimate compliance concern: how long do you store identity logs? What security measures protect them? The proposal touches on valid concerns but proposes the wrong solution (obscuring identity) rather than the right one (protecting stored identifiers with appropriate access controls, retention schedules, and encryption at rest).\n\n- From institutional survival: A non-compliant logging system that fails an audit could result in withdrawal of the high-risk AI system from the market, which is existential. The proposal risks building a system that doesn't meet the explicit \"identification\" standard.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal addresses a real tension (AI Act traceability vs GDPR minimization) but proposes a solution that likely does NOT satisfy Article 12(3)(d)'s explicit requirement for \"identification of natural persons.\" A ZKP or hash-based system that prevents identification contradicts the regulatory text. The correct compliance approach is to store identifiers with strong security, appropriate retention limits, and GDPR-compliant processing (legal basis under Art 6(1)(c), data protection impact assessment, encryption in transit and at rest). This proposal would consume implementation resources building a framework that creates regulatory risk rather than mitigating it.\n\nDecision: Reject. The core approach is fundamentally misaligned with the explicit \"identification\" requirement. While the underlying concern (privacy) is valid, the proposed solution creates compliance exposure rather than resolving it.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this maybe an 18-22. There's some value in thinking about data minimization and security of stored logs, but the core architectural premise (don't store identifiable data) contradicts the regulation's explicit requirement. It would waste resources building something that fails compliance review.\n",
