        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1846\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12 mandates that high-risk systems 'technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs)' and that those logs *enable* identification of risk situations; it does not require an autonomous AI agent performing unsupervised anomaly detection. As framed, this is an open-ended R&D program on high-dimensional stream analytics with no clearly bounded deliverable or acceptance criterion tied to the regulation, which makes it very difficult to prototype quickly, validate against a legal standard (Art. 79(1) 'risk'), or integrate into a compliance timeline; meanwhile the actual logging infrastructure (structured event capture with timestamps, reference-database pointers, input/match records per Art. 12(3)) is a well-understood engineering task that can be prototyped in weeks and already satisfies the letter of the requirement.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 35\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1846 is about developing an AI agent that monitors system logs to identify risks under Article 79(1) and detect emergent behaviors constituting substantial modifications, using unsupervised anomaly detection on high-dimensional event streams.\n\nLet me think through this from my technical viability and prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core idea is developing an AI agent for log monitoring with unsupervised anomaly detection. This is a well-studied area (anomaly detection in time-series, high-dimensional data). There's plenty of prior work here - isolation forests, autoencoders, statistical process control, etc.\n- However, the proposal frames this as a \"dedicated R&D project\" to develop an AI agent. The question is: what exactly needs to be built? Article 12(1) requires that high-risk systems \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" That's the logging infrastructure itself - timestamps, event capture, storage. Article 12(2)(a) says logs should enable identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification.\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) building the logging system (which is a concrete engineering task with clear requirements from Art. 12), and (2) building an AI agent to analyze those logs for anomalies. The regulation requires the *capability* to record events, not necessarily an AI-powered analyzer. The \"identifying situations\" in 12(2)(a) is about what the logs should capture/enable, not mandating an automated detection system.\n- Unsupervised anomaly detection on high-dimensional event streams is technically feasible but notoriously difficult to tune and validate. What constitutes a \"substantial modification\" is a regulatory/legal determination, not purely a statistical one. Building an AI agent to make that call autonomously introduces significant validation challenges.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A \"dedicated R&D project\" for an AI safety monitoring agent is a heavy commitment. It's not a quick prototype - it's a research program. From my prototyping perspective, this is a long-tail effort with uncertain deliverables.\n- The core regulatory requirement (automatic logging) can be met much more simply and quickly: structured event logging with appropriate metadata, timestamps, data references. That's a well-understood engineering task.\n- The \"AI agent\" layer adds complexity without being strictly required by the regulation. You could satisfy 12(2)(a) by designing logs to capture the relevant events (risk indicators, input changes, performance metrics) so that humans or simpler tools can identify situations. You don't need an autonomous AI for that.\n- The proposal also mentions \"transforms a regulatory burden into a new scientific contribution toward autonomous AI safety and monitoring\" - this sounds like scope creep / research justification dressed up as compliance engineering. As a development engineer focused on speed and quality of prototyping, I'd worry about mission drift: we start building a logging system for compliance, end up 18 months deep in unsupervised learning research that may or may not converge.\n\n**What's good about it:**\n- The direction isn't wrong - having automated monitoring of logs is valuable and aligns with the spirit of the regulation (post-market monitoring, traceability).\n- If we were going to build monitoring tooling anyway, incorporating some detection capabilities would be smart.\n- The high-dimensional event stream framing shows awareness of the technical challenge.\n\n**What's problematic from my vantage point:**\n- Over-engineered relative to what Art. 12 actually requires. The regulation says \"technically allow for automatic recording\" and that logs should \"enable the recording of events relevant for\" certain purposes. It doesn't mandate an AI agent.\n- As a prototyping concern, this is a large, open-ended R&D project rather than a bounded engineering deliverable. Hard to define success criteria clearly (what threshold? what's a \"substantial modification\" statistically?).\n- The validation problem: how do you validate that your anomaly detector correctly identifies \"risks within the meaning of Article 79(1)\"? That requires legal/regulatory interpretation, not just pattern recognition. You'd need labeled examples or expert judgment for every detection.\n- Speed concern: this will take significantly longer than building a robust structured logging system with appropriate alerting rules.\n\nMy decision: I'm inclined to reject this as framed. The core engineering task (building the logging infrastructure per Art. 12) is clear and bounded, but this proposal substitutes an open-ended R&D project for that. From a prototyping speed/quality standpoint, it's too diffuse, too long-tail, and doesn't map cleanly to what the regulation requires. The \"scientific contribution\" framing suggests research ambitions rather than engineering delivery.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge there's something valid here - if we're building logging anyway, adding detection capabilities is sensible. But as a *dedicated R&D project* framed around novel AI safety research, it overreaches from my prototyping vantage point.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this maybe 35-40. The underlying need (monitoring logs for risk) is real and valid, but the framing as an open-ended AI agent R&D project with unsupervised methods on high-dimensional streams makes it hard to prototype quickly or deliver cleanly. It's not a bad idea in principle, but it's mis-scoped from my engineering perspective. I'll go with 35.\n\nDecision: Reject. The proposal as stated is an open-ended research program rather than a bounded engineering deliverable that meets Art. 12's requirements. From a prototyping speed and technical viability standpoint, the core logging requirement can be met much more directly, and the \"AI agent\" layer introduces unbounded complexity without being mandated by the regulation.\n",
