otyping:**\n- This is a well-scoped, bounded engineering task. You don't need novel research or uncertain technology. It's: compute hash → store reference → tag inference calls with that reference. A senior engineer could prototype this in days to a couple of weeks depending on existing infrastructure.\n- The integration point is clear: where the inference pipeline resolves which dataset it's querying, that's where you log the hash. If the data lake already has versioning (most modern data lakes do - think Delta Lake, Iceberg, etc.), the \"hash\" might even be derivable from existing content hashes or file checksums.\n- Risk: if the reference databases are very large (billions of records), computing a full content hash at load time could add latency. But you'd compute it once at registration, not per-inference-call. The per-call cost is just looking up \"which version am I using\" and logging that identifier. Very low overhead.\n- Quality: cryptographic hashing is deterministic and well-tested (OpenSSL, hashlib in Python, etc.). No novel algorithm risk.\n\n**Regulatory fit:**\n- Article 12(3)(b) specifically requires recording \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" This proposal directly addresses that with a verifiable link. It's a clean, targeted solution to a specific sub-requirement.\n- It doesn't overreach - it focuses on one specific logging requirement rather than trying to be a grand unified solution.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Immutable\" is a strong word. In practice, you'd store hashes in a database or log that could theoretically be modified by an admin with sufficient privileges. True immutability would require WORM storage, blockchain, or similar. But for regulatory purposes, an append-only audit log with proper access controls and the cryptographic property of the hash itself (you can't forge a preimage) should suffice. The proposal says \"immutable hashes\" which is technically accurate - the hash val