ject.\n- From my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), the core question is: does this push the interpretability frontier in a meaningful way? Potentially yes, but the proposal as stated doesn't demonstrate sufficient methodological depth to be confident it will.\n\nI'd say this clears the bar for approval from my vantage point — the research direction has genuine intellectual merit and sits at a real frontier. But I'm not giving it a super-high promise score because:\n1. It's underspecified\n2. The \"high-fidelity\" claim is very ambitious and may be overreaching\n3. The regulatory mapping could constrain rather than enhance scientific novelty\n4. There's risk it becomes an engineering exercise (wrapping existing tools) rather than a genuine research contribution\n\nI'll approve it but with a moderate promise score — maybe 62-68 range. It has real intellectual interest and frontier potential, but the proposal as stated is somewhat thin on methodological commitment and the \"high-fidelity\" ambition may be hard to deliver in practice.\n",
