e research and meeting regulatory requirements. By automating logging, you free up researcher time for actual science rather than filling out logs manually.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\nCI/CD pipeline integration is key here - it leverages infrastructure that already exists in a modern R&D lab. The \"API hooks\" approach means you're plugging into existing software architecture rather than building parallel systems. The \"modular\" design means it can be added to new projects without redesigning the whole system. This is efficient use of what we already have.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Across various high-risk model architectures\" - this is ambitious scope. Building a framework that truly works across all our different model types (LLMs, computer vision, decision systems) could be complex. However, the modular design addresses this somewhat.\n- The proposal mentions \"input hashes\" and \"user IDs\" which maps to Article 12(3)(c) and (d) for Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, but also covers general logging from paragraphs 1-2. This seems appropriately scoped.\n- Building this requires dedicated engineering time upfront, which is a resource commitment. But the ROI across multiple projects makes it sensible.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped proposal that directly addresses the regulatory requirement (Article 12) in a way that:\n1. Is efficient (build once, use many times)\n2. Protects scientific throughput (no manual logging, no performance degradation)\n3. Leverages existing infrastructure (CI/CD, API hooks into existing systems)\n\nThe main risk is scope creep with \"various high-risk model architectures\" but the modular design mitigates this. This is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that makes sense for a lab - it's a one-time cost that pays dividends across all future projects and reduces ongoing per-project compliance costs significantly.\n\nI would approve this. It's well-aligned with my doma