ng speed by abstracting regulatory complexity away from researchers\" — this is exactly the right engineering philosophy for an R&D lab. Researchers should be building models, not writing audit log code.\n- Consistency across projects is a quality benefit: you get uniform logging that's easier to debug and validate.\n- The main risk is that if the SDK is too rigid or has performance overhead, it could slow down prototyping. But a well-designed middleware layer with async logging would be negligible.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Universal\" is a strong word. Different high-risk AI systems (biometric identification vs. credit scoring vs. medical devices) have very different event structures. A single SDK might need significant configuration or extension per project type. But even so, having a common foundation is better than starting from scratch each time.\n- The proposal says \"without manual coding\" — for the basic fields (timestamps, inputs, user IDs), this is reasonable. For domain-specific logging requirements (like reference database identification in biometric systems), some configuration or extension will be needed. But that's a minor caveat.\n- Resource commitment: Building and maintaining an SDK is a real engineering investment. But it amortizes across all high-risk prototypes, so the ROI is clear if the lab produces multiple such projects.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound, well-motivated proposal that directly serves my domain concerns (technical viability + prototyping speed). The pattern is proven, the scope is reasonable, and the benefit to the R&D workflow is immediate and significant. The minor overreach in \"plug-and-play without manual coding\" doesn't undermine the core value proposition. I'd approve this with high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This aligns very well with my function's priorities. It's technically viable (proven pattern), it accelerates prototyping significantly, and it ensures consis