ents in Article 12.\n5. This has broader scientific impact: if we develop this well, it could be open-sourced or shared as a methodology that benefits the wider research community.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. The scope is very broad — \"full lineage of model versions, hyperparameters, AND dataset slices\" is an enormous engineering undertaking. Dataset slicing in particular can be computationally expensive and storage-intensive at scale.\n2. \"Instantly reconstruct any historical system state\" may be aspirational rather than practical for large models with terabytes of training data. You can't always store every intermediate state.\n3. The proposal conflates two somewhat different things: reproducibility (can you rerun an experiment?) and traceability (can you track what happened in production?). These require different infrastructure.\n4. \"Elevates compliance to a gold standard for scientific reproducibility\" is a bit of a stretch — the EU AI Act's record-keeping requirements are about safety, accountability, and post-market monitoring. While overlap exists, the regulatory requirements don't actually demand full scientific reproducibility in the academic sense. However, as a researcher I'd argue that if we build to the higher standard, we exceed compliance AND advance science.\n5. The proposal doesn't specify what's novel here versus existing tools (MLflow, DVC, Weights & Biases, Neptune, etc.). What's the intellectual contribution beyond integrating existing best practices?\n\nFrom my researcher vantage point:\n- The intellectual merit is moderate-to-good. The idea of treating regulatory traceability as an opportunity to build superior reproducibility infrastructure is well-motivated. It aligns with a genuine research community need.\n- The broader scientific impact could be significant if executed well — better reproducibility tools benefit all AI research.\n- However, the knowledge frontier contribution is somewhat limited unless there's something genuinely new in HOW they capture dataset lineage or how they handle the computational/storage challenges of full state reconstruction.\n- As a researcher, I'd want to see: what's the novel technical contribution? Is this just \"use DVC + MLflow + git properly\" or is there a genuinely new approach to capturing and reconstructing system state at scale?\n\nThe promise score: From my perspective as a researcher focused on knowledge frontier and scientific impact, this is a solid proposal that addresses a real need. It has good potential for broader scientific impact if the tools are made accessible. The intellectual merit is in the integration and the framing of compliance as rigor-enhancing rather than burden-imposing. However, it's not pushing the knowledge frontier in terms of novel algorithms or methods — it's more about infrastructure and process. I'd rate this around 68-72. It's genuinely useful for the lab's research culture and could have external impact, but it's not a breakthrough scientific contribution on its own.\n\nLet me settle on: decision = approve (it clears my bar for intellectual merit and broader scientific impact — reproducibility is a core concern), promise = 70.\n",
