s a nice-to-have enhancement, not a requirement.\n2. The language \"turns mandatory data collection into a scientific asset\" and \"transforms a regulatory burden into a scientific tool for advancing the knowledge frontier of AI reliability\" suggests this is more of an R&D aspiration than a compliance necessity. While it supports compliance indirectly, it's not something that, if we don't do it, would put us at risk of non-compliance.\n3. However, from an institutional survival perspective: if our systems are high-risk and we have post-market monitoring obligations (Article 72), being able to actually USE the logs meaningfully to detect issues strengthens our position. If a system fails and we can't explain why from our logs, that's a compliance problem. This research could make us more resilient.\n4. Market reputation: publishing or demonstrating that we extract deep insights from our regulatory data positions us as a thought leader in AI governance. Regulators and market surveillance authorities would view this favorably.\n5. The proposal is well-scoped - it uses existing mandatory data (we're already collecting it), applies established methods (causal inference), and has clear deliverables (identifying systemic triggers, correlating events with unexpected outputs).\n\n**Key question from my vantage point:** Does this help institutional survival, regulatory compliance, or market reputation?\n\n- Institutional survival: Modestly yes - better understanding of failure modes means we catch problems earlier, reducing the risk of serious incidents that could threaten our operating authorization.\n- Regulatory compliance: Indirectly supports Articles 12, 72, 79(1), 26(5). It doesn't directly implement a requirement but deepens our capability to fulfill monitoring obligations effectively. The regulation says logs should enable \"identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk\" - this research directly supports that purpose.\n- Market 