rate effort.\n- However, designing and maintaining a full JSON-LD schema with versioning across all AI systems (algorithms, training data lineage, validation processes) is substantial new infrastructure.\n- The \"dynamic, versioned data structures\" moving beyond static PDFs makes practical sense for ongoing compliance — you need to update documentation throughout the system's lifetime per Recital 71.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. Scope: This proposal conflates a regulatory compliance requirement (logging and record-keeping) with a broader scientific transparency initiative. The regulation requires logs (Article 12(1)) and technical documentation (Recital 71). Building an open, community-facing JSON-LD schema is well beyond what's needed for compliance.\n2. Resource commitment: Designing, building, testing, and maintaining a full schema + pipeline integration across all systems is expensive. For a lab with funding constraints, this could be over-investment in one area of compliance while other articles (risk management, human oversight, etc.) remain unaddressed.\n3. The \"leader\" positioning language suggests prestige-driven motivation rather than pure efficiency. While that's not inherently bad, it may lead to scope creep and gold-plating.\n4. On the positive side: if you're going to build documentation infrastructure anyway, making it machine-readable and pipeline-integrated IS more efficient in the long run than maintaining PDFs. The ROI is real for a lab with multiple AI systems over their lifetimes.\n5. Risk: JSON-LD adds complexity (RDF, ontologies, linked data) that may not be necessary. A simpler structured format (YAML, structured JSON) might achieve 90% of the benefit at 30% of the cost.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea — integrating documentation generation into CI/CD pipelines as machine-readable output — is sound and resource-efficient in principle. It leverages existing infrastructure (CI/CD) rather than creating parallel processe