nnel must authenticate via secure credentials\" - this adds friction to workflows. If our existing R&D pipelines already have authentication and approval layers (which they likely do in any serious lab), building a *dedicated* new portal duplicates infrastructure rather than leveraging what exists. The question is whether we can integrate this logging into existing workflow tools vs. standing up a parallel system.\n- \"Total institutional accountability and avoid litigation\" - the framing suggests building for worst-case legal defense rather than proportionate compliance. This could drive scope creep.\n- The proposal doesn't mention how it integrates with existing infrastructure or what's already in place. A dedicated new portal is more expensive to build, maintain, and integrate than augmenting an existing system.\n- Resource allocation: This is a bounded, well-defined engineering task. It's not scientifically ambitious but it IS necessary compliance work. The question is whether the implementation approach (immutable ledger, dedicated portal) is proportionate to the requirement.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe underlying requirement is legitimate and must be met for Annex III systems. However, the proposed implementation has resource-inefficient elements:\n1. \"Immutable ledger\" is overkill - a tamper-evident logging system (hash-chained records in a standard database) achieves the same purpose at much lower cost\n2. A \"dedicated portal\" vs. integrating into existing workflow tools adds maintenance burden and user friction\n3. The scope seems appropriate to Annex III systems, which is good\n\nFrom my perspective: The *what* is necessary (we must comply with Article 12(3)(d) for Annex III systems), but the *how* as described is resource-inefficient. A dedicated immutable-ledger portal is over-engineered compared to integrating authentication-based logging into existing verification workflows with standard tamper-evident storage.\n\nHowever, I should note: if we alre