/Concerns:**\n- Training alone may not be sufficient — compliance ultimately requires technical implementation, governance oversight, and audit trails of whether the checklists were actually followed. A training program is necessary but not a complete solution.\n- \"Streamlined\" could mean it's too shallow to capture the nuances of Article 12(3) minimum requirements for specific system types.\n- The proposal focuses on researchers, but compliance also involves engineering, data science, and product teams. However, starting with researchers in an R&D institution makes sense as they drive initial design.\n- It doesn't mention how training effectiveness is measured or audited — from a compliance standpoint, I'd want to know we can demonstrate the program worked (e.g., through post-implementation audits).\n\n**From my specific vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n- This directly reduces compliance risk at the earliest and cheapest stage of development. If logging isn't designed in, retrofitting is expensive, time-consuming, and may still not fully meet Article 12's technical requirements.\n- It demonstrates a culture of compliance to regulators — if we're ever audited or investigated, showing that we trained staff proactively on traceability design is a strong mitigation factor.\n- Protects market reputation: systems built with proper logging from the start are more robust and trustworthy in the eyes of customers and partners.\n- The cost is relatively low (training program) compared to the cost of non-compliance or retrofitting.\n\nThis is a solid, practical proposal that addresses a real gap in our compliance posture. It's not a complete solution on its own, but it fills an important piece of the compliance puzzle at minimal cost and high leverage. I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is genuinely valuable from my perspective. It's practical, low-cost, addresses the design-time nature of Article 12 requirements,