ucture. The \"small percentage\" framing suggests this piggybacks on existing work rather than creating a wholly new silo.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- \"A small percentage\" is vague — but as an initial framework, it sets the right principle without over-specifying.\n- Updating all project charters is administrative overhead, but minimal compared to the alternative.\n- The risk of \"small percentage\" being too small in practice for complex systems — but this is an implementation detail, not a flaw in the proposal's logic.\n\nOverall, from my vantage point, this is a well-conceived resource-allocation strategy that directly addresses my domain of concern. It's pragmatic, forward-looking, and prevents the worst-case scenario (catastrophic late-stage redesigns) which would be the most expensive outcome for any R&D institution. The framing as \"built-in from start\" vs. \"retrospective fixes\" is exactly right from an efficiency standpoint — retrofitting logging into a complex AI system is enormously more expensive than architecting it in during the initial design phase.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. It's squarely in my domain and represents sound resource-allocation thinking. I'd put it around 82-85. The main reservation is that \"small percentage\" without further specification could be underfunded in practice, but as a framework-level proposal to be operationalized, the principle is strong.\n",
