iented.\n- The promise of \"early detection of logging failures prevents expensive architectural overhauls during the final deployment phase\" is genuinely valuable from a dev engineering standpoint. If I'm about to spend 6 months building features on top of a prototype that can't log events properly, that's wasted time.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Who does the actual work? \"Compliance engineers\" doing gap analyses and then \"recommending\" upgrades — this is not the same as engineering teams implementing logging infrastructure. The proposal doesn't commit to actually fixing anything, just assessing and recommending.\n- It's scoped to \"flagship projects\" which limits its breadth but also makes it feasible with a small team.\n- The deliverable is \"recommendations\" not implemented changes — so from a dev perspective, this is a prerequisite step, not the actual engineering work.\n\n**Verdict:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping), this proposal has real merit in catching logging gaps early. It's technically feasible to do gap analyses on existing prototypes. The main weakness is that it produces recommendations rather than implementations, and it's owned by \"compliance engineers\" rather than dev teams. But as a focused pilot, it could save significant rework time if the findings are actionable and fed back into the development pipeline quickly.\n\nI think this clears my bar with moderate confidence. It's technically viable, relatively low-cost (small team on existing prototypes), and directly addresses a concern that affects prototyping speed (building on broken foundations). But I'm not thrilled because it's framed as an audit rather than an engineering task, and the output is recommendations rather than fixes. I'd want to see the findings feed directly into sprint planning for the dev teams.\n\nI'll approve but with a moderate promise score — maybe around 58-62. It's useful but not transformative from my perspective, and the 