ults are not reproducible precisely because provenance isn't captured systematically.\n\n**Weaknesses / concerns from my perspective:**\n- The proposal frames this primarily as compliance infrastructure (\"satisfying Article 12 requirements\") rather than as a scientific tool. This framing matters — if it's built purely to check regulatory boxes, the design decisions may optimize for auditor readability rather than scientific utility. For example, what matters for reproducibility is not just \"start date and time\" but the full computational graph, random seeds, library versions, hardware configurations, intermediate model states, etc.\n- The claim that it \"guarantees scientific reproducibility\" is overreaching. Capturing hyperparameters and dataset versions helps, but true reproducibility in deep learning also requires capturing stochastic elements (random seeds, GPU non-determinism), software environment exactness, and sometimes the full training trajectory. A metadata layer may not go deep enough.\n- The proposal says \"without increasing manual researcher workload\" — this is a constraint that might limit the depth of provenance captured. True scientific reproducibility often requires some deliberate annotation (e.g., why a particular ablation was run, what hypothesis motivated a configuration choice) that can't be fully automated.\n- However, even as an automated baseline, it's a significant improvement over current practice where researchers manually note configs in README files or lose them entirely.\n\n**Intellectual merit:** The core idea is sound and well-established in the broader reproducibility literature (MLflow, DVC,Weights & Biases, etc.). This isn't novel research per se — it's infrastructure engineering applied to a known problem. But the application to high-risk AI with regulatory motivation gives it specific urgency and scope definition that pure voluntary reproducibility efforts lack.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:** If this framework creates