lutions, we might just adopt them rather than fund research. But for AI-specific high-granularity event logging (which can be very different from traditional software logging due to model inference patterns), there may genuinely be gaps.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: The proposal says we'll benchmark against industry standards and turn a regulatory burden into scientific contribution. This means:\n- We get compliance capability as an output\n- We potentially publish or contribute to the field (scientific ambition)\n- We improve our own infrastructure efficiency (direct operational benefit)\n- It leverages existing systems for benchmarking\n\nThe dual-use nature is attractive: we must implement Article 12 logging anyway, so doing it in a research context that also optimizes cost means we're not paying twice — once for compliance engineering and once for optimization. Instead, one effort serves both.\n\n**Concerns**:\n- \"Dedicated research project\" implies dedicated FTEs away from other work. Is the scope large enough to justify this? Compressed logging formats, selective event triggering, metadata indexing — these are three distinct sub-problems. Could be a 6-month focused project or could balloon.\n- The proposal says \"minimizing computational and storage costs\" but Article 12 requires specific minimum fields (especially for Annex III point 1(a) systems). There's a floor below which we can't go. The research needs to respect that constraint — it's optimization within a compliance envelope, not arbitrary reduction.\n- \"Selective event triggering\" is interesting but risky: if you selectively log events, you need to justify why certain events are excluded and ensure you still meet the traceability requirements of paragraphs 2(a), 2(b), 2(c). This needs careful alignment with the regulation.\n\n**Overall assessment**: From my vantage point on resource-allocation efficiency and infrastructure use, this is a strong proposal. It directly addresses a cost center that will grow with our portfolio. The research framing gives it intellectual depth beyond mere engineering compliance work. The scope is bounded enough to be fundable without being so narrow that it's just an engineering task. The dual benefit (compliance + efficiency gain + potential publication) makes good use of limited R&D resources.\n\nI would approve this. It hits all three of my domain concerns positively. Promise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's well-aligned with my mandate, but there are scope risks and the \"research\" framing could be inflated over what is essentially systems engineering optimization. Also, \"dedicated project\" commitment is a real resource ask that I need to weigh against other competing needs. But it clears my bar decisively.\n\nLet me settle on 80 — strong alignment with my domain, clear deliverables, dual-use value, but some uncertainty about whether the novelty justifies full research treatment vs. engineering adoption of existing techniques.\n",
