archers to slow down for compliance; it's making compliance automatic.\n- However, there's a risk of over-engineering early. If we're still in exploration phase and haven't yet committed specific systems as \"high-risk\" under the Act, building a full automated documentation pipeline might be spending resources on infrastructure that may need rework when our actual high-risk systems are defined.\n- That said, the tools described (pulling hyperparameters, dataset hashes, validation metrics) are things we're already tracking in our training logs and config files. The marginal cost of extracting them into a formatted document is low compared to building new data infrastructure.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This leverages what we already have: training logs, configuration files, CI/CD pipelines with merge triggers. It's not asking for new compute, new data stores, or new teams. It's a thin layer on top of existing systems.\n- The \"Doc-as-Code\" approach is well-aligned with modern ML engineering practices. We likely already have versioned configs and structured logs; this just adds an output format.\n- PDF reports are somewhat dated from a pure engineering standpoint (HTML or structured data would be more flexible), but for auditor-facing documents, standardized PDFs make sense.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Standardized PDF reports\" — who defines the standard? If we're building templates now based on our current understanding of what Article 12 requires, there's a risk the templates need significant revision when actual enforcement guidance clarifies requirements. The Doc-as-Code approach helps here (templates are code, easy to update), but the initial investment might be partially wasted.\n- \"Every successful model merge into the main branch\" — this could generate an enormous volume of documentation if we have many experiments merging frequently. Need to think about whether every merge is relevant or only certain milestone merges. Resource waste if we're generating 50 PDFs a day that nobody reads until audit time.\n- The proposal says it \"replaces manual report writing with dynamic, evidence-based record keeping.\" This is aspirational — in practice, some human review and context will still be needed (e.g., explaining WHY certain hyperparameters were chosen, or what a validation metric drop means). But for the mechanical parts, yes.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a reasonable, resource-efficient approach to a compliance requirement that would otherwise consume significant researcher time. It leverages existing infrastructure (CI/CD, structured logs, versioned configs) rather than requiring new investments. The main risk is building it too early or over-scoping the initial implementation. But the Doc-as-Code pattern means it's modular and can be extended incrementally.\n\nFrom a resource-allocation standpoint: low incremental cost relative to the compliance burden it addresses. It doesn't compete with scientific ambition for researcher time — in fact, it protects that time from being eaten by administrative documentation work. The upfront engineering effort is modest (scripts + templates + trigger configuration).\n\nI'd lean toward approving this. It's pragmatic, uses existing infrastructure, and the ROI becomes clearer as we get closer to having systems classified as high-risk. The main caveat is scope — start with what Article 12(3) minimums require for our specific Annex III category if applicable, rather than trying to document everything from day one.\n\nPromise score: I'd put this around 72-78. It's a good use of resources, leverages existing infrastructure well, and addresses a real compliance need without overreaching. Docked slightly because of the risk of premature build (we may not yet be at the stage where full Article 12 logging is mandatory for all our systems) and the PDF format choice which adds maintenance burden compared to structured data outputs.\n\n