geable between researchers and regulators\" aspect is valuable — it reduces the translation overhead when preparing submissions for notified bodies or responding to regulator queries.\n\n4. **Does it actually serve Article 12?** Yes, directly. Recital 71 calls for documentation of \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" A standardized schema ensures all these elements are captured consistently. It supports the \"kept up to date\" requirement by making it easier to track what's changed when you have a structured format rather than free-text documents.\n\n5. **Risks/weaknesses**: \n   - JSON-LD is somewhat niche; most labs use simpler formats (YAML, CSV, or just structured PDFs). There's a risk of over-engineering.\n   - The \"scientific reporting standards\" alignment is vague — which standards? If this becomes a scope-creep project trying to align with too many frameworks, it could balloon.\n   - Maintenance burden: the schema itself needs updating as the AI Act evolves or as new system types are developed.\n   - \"Interchangeable between researchers and regulators\" assumes regulators will actually consume JSON-LD, which may not be realistic in the near term.\n\n6. **Net assessment**: This is a solid infrastructure investment that directly supports compliance with Article 12 and Recital 71. The scope is reasonable (a schema + tooling), the payoff is multiplicative across all high-risk systems, and it leverages existing documentation practices rather than requiring new ones. From a resource-efficiency standpoint, standardization reduces per-system documentation cost over time. I'd approve this with moderate-to-high confidence, though I'd want to see a bounded scope (not \"align with ALL scientific reporting standards\" but specifically the EU AI Act technical documentation requirements).\n\nPromise score: