        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1966\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12 demands structured event logging (timestamps, inputs/outputs, reference databases, verifier IDs for Annex III-1(a) systems), which is a well-trodden software-engineering problem solvable with standard instrumentation frameworks in weeks; capturing full activation and attention tensors across frontier-scale models introduces enormous GPU-memory overhead, multi-gigabyte-per-inference storage burdens, and cross-framework API complexity that are neither required by the regulation nor quickly prototype-able. The proposal solves a research-level question (how decisions are internally reached) while the Act asks for an engineering-level one (which events occurred, with sufficient traceability), making it the wrong abstraction layer for our implementation timeline.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 32\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Neural Traceability Framework for Frontier Models\" - developing high-fidelity, low-overhead logging mechanisms that capture latent state transitions during inference, including activation patterns and attention weights across model versions, stored in queryable format compatible with the Act's requirements.\n\nLet me assess this from a technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Capturing \"activation patterns and attention weights across model versions\" is technically feasible for transformer-based models. You can hook into forward passes to record activations, attention heads, etc.\n- However, \"high-fidelity, low-overhead\" is a significant tension. Recording full activation patterns across all layers of frontier-scale models (billions of parameters) at every inference step creates enormous data volumes. A single inference through a large model can produce gigabytes of intermediate tensors. This is not trivial to make \"low-overhead.\"\n- \"Standardized instrumentation API\" - this is ambitious but doable for controlled environments. However, making it work across different architectures, model sizes, and inference frameworks (TensorRT, vLLM, ONNX Runtime, custom kernels) is a major engineering challenge.\n- \"Queryable format compatible with the Act's requirements\" - The Act (Article 12) actually requires logging of *events* relevant to identifying risks, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. It does NOT require recording activation patterns or attention weights. The minimum requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems are: period of use, reference database, input data that led to a match, identification of natural persons verifying results. These are relatively simple event-level logs.\n- So the proposal is solving a problem much more complex than what the regulation requires. The regulation asks for \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - not latent state capture.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Building a \"standardized instrumentation API\" that works across frontier models, different architectures, and inference backends is a massive engineering effort. This is months to years of work before you have something production-ready.\n- The proposal frames this as a \"research project\" which suggests it's exploratory rather than a concrete implementation plan. From my vantage point, I care about what can be prototyped quickly and deployed effectively.\n- For the actual regulatory requirements (Article 12), you need structured event logging: timestamps, input/output data references, system state at decision points, error events, etc. This is standard software engineering - message queues, structured logging frameworks, time-series databases. You can prototype this in weeks.\n- The \"neural traceability\" angle adds enormous complexity (GPU memory management for intermediate tensors, storage infrastructure for massive data volumes, query tools for high-dimensional data) without clearly mapping to what the Act actually requires.\n\n**The core issue from my vantage point:**\nThe proposal over-engineers the solution relative to what Article 12 actually demands. The regulation says \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" and specifies what those events should enable (identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operations). For Annex III 1(a) systems, it lists specific minimum data points that are all at the event/input/output level, not the latent state level.\n\nWhile capturing activation patterns *could* theoretically help with \"identifying situations that may result in a risk,\" it's:\n1. Not required by the regulation\n2. Technically very expensive to implement at scale (storage, computation overhead)\n3. A research-level challenge rather than an engineering deliverable\n4. Slow to prototype relative to standard structured logging solutions\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge that there IS a kernel of value here: standardized instrumentation for AI systems is a useful engineering artifact regardless of regulatory requirements. And the idea of making logs \"queryable\" in a format that supports the Act's traceability goals is sound engineering. The problem is the scope - going all the way to latent state capture is disproportionate.\n\n**Decision:** From my perspective as a development engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping, this proposal:\n- Over-scopes relative to regulatory requirements (the Act doesn't require activation logging)\n- Has significant technical challenges (storing/querying full attention patterns for frontier models at scale) that make it slow to prototype\n- Frames a research project as if it's the right response to what is fundamentally an engineering/logging task\n- The \"low-overhead\" claim is questionable given the data volumes involved\n\nI would reject this. It's not technically infeasible, but from my vantage point, it's the wrong level of complexity for the problem, it will be slow to deliver something useful, and the regulatory text doesn't demand it. A simpler structured event-logging solution would meet Article 12 requirements faster and more reliably.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this maybe a 30-40. There's some value in the standardization angle, but the core approach (latent state capture) is over-engineered for the regulatory need and technically challenging to deliver at quality/speed standards I care about.\n",
        "tool_calls": []
      },
      "logprobs": null,
      "finish_reason": "stop"
    }
  ],
