ually interesting questions are: What constitutes an \"event relevant for identifying situations that may result in risk\"? How do you automatically determine which internal states of diverse architectures warrant logging? This is genuinely open and would require understanding model internals. The proposal dismisses this as something to be handled by \"automatic recording\" via middleware, which suggests a shallow approach. There's no research component here — it's an engineering implementation task.\n\n3. **Broader scientific impact**: A compliance logging layer has minimal broader scientific impact. It's not going to publish in any venue, advance understanding of AI systems, or contribute to the knowledge base. It's operational infrastructure. While necessary for compliance, from a research perspective it's maintenance work.\n\n4. **The \"universal\" claim is problematic**: The proposal says it works \"regardless of architecture.\" This is actually a red flag from a research standpoint. The truly hard problem in Article 12(2) is determining what events are *relevant* to the intended purpose and risk profile of each specific system. A one-size-fits-all middleware that just logs timestamps, input matches, and data references (as described) would satisfy maybe Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems but would be insufficient for other high-risk categories where \"events relevant for traceability\" are far more architecture-specific.\n\n5. **Resource commitment perspective**: Approving this commits scarce implementation resources to building infrastructure that is (a) not research, (b) likely to be architecturally naive given the \"universal\" framing, and (c) has minimal scientific value. However, I should also note: if researchers spend their time on manual logging integration for each model, that IS a real productivity drag. The proposal does acknowledge this (\"maximizing the productivity of our scientific researchers\"). But the way it's framed — as a plug-and-play DevOps solution — suggests it will be shallow and may not actually solve the problem well enough to free up research time meaningfully.\n\nMy assessment: From my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal has very low value. It's an engineering/operations task dressed in compliance language. The \"universal\" claim is intellectually thin and potentially misleading about what's technically feasible without deeper architecture-specific research. There's no novel knowledge being generated, no interesting scientific questions being addressed, and no broader impact beyond operational compliance.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: it does free up researcher time from manual logging tasks. But the way it's proposed (as a one-size-fits-all plug-and-play layer) suggests it won't actually work well for diverse architectures, meaning researchers will still need to do architecture-specific work anyway. The net benefit to research productivity is uncertain and likely overstated.\n\nDecision: Reject.\n",
