ormation\" and \"clear and comprehensive form\").\n- Real-time capture aligns with the requirement that logging be over the \"lifetime of the system.\"\n\n**Potential gaps/concerns from compliance vantage:**\n- Article 12(3)(d) requires \"identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results, as referred to in Article 14(5).\" The proposal mentions input data, versioning references, and operational durations, but doesn't explicitly mention capturing verifier identities. This is a gap for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics, etc.). However, this could be considered an implementation detail that the framework would need to address.\n- Article 12(2)(a) requires logging events relevant to \"identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1).\" The proposal focuses on generic event capture but doesn't explicitly mention risk-event flagging or alerting integration.\n- Article 12(2)(b) references facilitating post-market monitoring (Article 72). A \"single source of truth\" framework could support this, but the proposal doesn't explicitly address data export interfaces for regulatory authorities.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level — it's a framework architecture rather than specifying the exact data fields required by Article 12(3). But as a candidate proposal from an ideation exercise, this level of abstraction may be appropriate.\n\n**Compliance risk assessment:**\nThis is fundamentally the right architectural approach. Without automated logging infrastructure, the lab would be non-compliant with Article 12(1) — it's a mandatory \"shall\" requirement. The risk of NOT having this is existential: if a regulator audits and finds no automated logs, that's a clear violation. The proposal directly mitigates the most fundamental compliance risk under Article 12.\n\nThe fact that it makes logging mandatory for all high-risk projects addresses the institutional governance gap