        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2825\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal directly supports Article 12(2)(a)–(b) by converting raw logs into an active detection layer for risk situations and post-market monitoring, which is exactly the traceability-and-monitoring purpose Recital 71 articulates. However, it presumes the underlying automatic-logging infrastructure of Art. 12(1) and the minimum data fields of Art. 12(3) already exist; as a standalone commitment it does not itself satisfy those core obligations, so I am approving it as a complementary compliance-enabling tool rather than as the foundational logging system, while noting that its value is contingent on that foundation being in place.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2825 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, with domain concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is for a \"Post-Market Risk Monitoring Dashboard\" - a centralized visualization platform that analyzes real-time logs to identify drifts or anomalies indicative of high-risk failures as mandated by the Act. It implements a telemetry system that flags specific event patterns defined in Article 12(2) and alerts the risk management team immediately.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Rationale:**\nArticle 12 specifically mandates:\n- Para 1: Automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime - this is about logging capability, not visualization\n- Para 2: Logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for (a) identifying risk situations/substantial modifications, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring under Article 72, and (c) monitoring operation under Article 26(5)\n- Para 3: Specific minimum logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems\n\nThe proposal frames this as a \"dashboard\" and \"visualization platform\" that \"transforms passive record-keeping into an active tool.\" From a compliance standpoint:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- It directly addresses the Article 12(2)(b) requirement about facilitating post-market monitoring\n- Real-time analysis of logs could help identify situations under 12(2)(a) - risk situations and substantial modifications\n- Alerting the risk management team supports the overall compliance framework\n- \"Transforms passive record-keeping into an active tool\" aligns with the spirit of Recital 71 about traceability, verification of compliance, monitoring operations\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns from compliance perspective:**\n- The core obligation in Article 12 is the *logging capability* itself (para 1) - the system must technically allow automatic recording. A dashboard is a secondary layer on top of that. If we fund this but haven't built the underlying logging infrastructure, we still don't comply with para 1 and 3.\n- The proposal says it \"flags specific event patterns defined in Article 12(2)\" - but Article 12(2) defines what events should be *recorded*, not what patterns to flag. The actual risk identification is more about Article 79 (substantial modification) and the broader risk management system under Chapter III Section 2.\n- \"Post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72\" - a dashboard could support this, but post-market monitoring is a broader obligation that includes much more than log visualization (it includes collecting information from users, updating technical documentation, etc.)\n- The proposal doesn't mention compliance with para 3's specific minimum logging requirements for Annex III(1)(a) systems.\n- From institutional survival: if regulators audit and see we have a fancy dashboard but incomplete raw logs, that's worse than having complete logs without the dashboard.\n- Market reputation: showing proactive monitoring is good, but only if the underlying compliance foundation (the actual logging) is solid.\n\nHowever, as a Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation:\n- This proposal supports Article 12(2)(a) and (b) - identifying risk situations and facilitating post-market monitoring\n- It's an active compliance tool rather than just passive storage\n- In the era of AI regulation, demonstrating proactive monitoring to regulators and in the market is valuable for reputation\n- The \"immediate alerting\" aspect could help with incident response obligations\n- It transforms compliance from a cost center into a demonstrable safety capability\n\nBut the key question: does this proposal, as stated, actually ensure compliance? Or is it a complementary tool that assumes the underlying logging infrastructure exists? The text says \"analyzes real-time logs\" - implying logs already exist. If this is a standalone proposal without the foundational logging system, it's building on sand.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is valuable as a complement but doesn't by itself satisfy Article 12. It addresses the monitoring and traceability aspects (Recital 71, Art 12(2)(a)-(b)) well. The \"active tool\" framing aligns with where regulators are heading - they want to see systems that don't just store data but actively detect issues. For market reputation, being able to demonstrate real-time compliance monitoring is a differentiator.\n\nI think this clears my bar as a useful compliance enabler, though I note it's not sufficient on its own for full Article 12 compliance (the logging infrastructure itself must exist). The proposal as described is a reasonable investment that supports multiple regulatory obligations simultaneously. It's not the foundational piece but a meaningful layer.\n\nDecision: approve - it directly supports Art 12(2) objectives and post-market monitoring, enhances our demonstrable compliance posture, and the alerting mechanism reduces institutional risk of undetected non-conformities.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 72-78. It's valuable but not the foundational piece. It assumes logging infrastructure exists. The framing slightly overreaches (calling it what Article 12 \"mandates\" when Art 12 mandates the logging, not the dashboard). But as a compliance support tool, it's solid. I'll go with 74.\n",
