s significant up-front investment in engineering resources. The question is whether we have enough high-risk AI projects to justify this fixed cost. If we only have 2-3 high-risk systems, the overhead of building and maintaining a full middleware layer might not pay off versus simpler per-project logging solutions.\n- \"Standardized software layer across ALL high-risk AI projects\" — if our portfolio is diverse (different architectures, different data flows), a truly unified framework could be over-engineered or too rigid. The risk is spending 12-18 months building something that doesn't fit all use cases well.\n- The proposal says \"we will develop internal API wrappers that researchers integrate into their models\" — this still requires researcher time and cooperation, though less than building from scratch each time.\n- Maintenance burden: a centralized middleware becomes a critical dependency. If it breaks or needs updating for regulatory changes, ALL projects are affected. This is a concentration risk.\n- However, the alternative (each team doing their own logging) is clearly wasteful in terms of resource allocation — duplicated effort, inconsistent formats, higher compliance audit costs.\n\nOverall assessment: From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is a strong proposal IF the lab has enough high-risk AI projects to amortize the fixed cost. The centralization argument is compelling — it's the textbook efficient approach to a cross-cutting compliance requirement. It frees expensive researcher time. It uses existing infrastructure patterns (shared services). The main risk is scope creep or over-engineering for our actual portfolio size, but the core concept of \"build logging once, expose via API\" is sound and resource-efficient.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a good use of engineering resources to solve a compliance problem efficiently rather than duplicating effort across teams. I'll approve it with a high promise score, maybe noting that 