ive to fix later (regulatory non-compliance penalties, or rushed documentation before an audit).\n4. The \"real-time\" and \"living document\" approach aligns well with the regulatory requirement that documentation be kept \"up to date... throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" Static PDFs go stale quickly in active R&D environments. This is a structurally better fit for how we actually work.\n5. Resource efficiency: if this works, it replaces recurring manual labor (documentation updates) with an automated pipeline that runs once and then self-maintains. For a lab with limited headcount, this is a meaningful reallocation of human time back to actual research.\n\nConcerns:\n1. \"Real-time\" extraction from training runs requires instrumentation at the training infrastructure level. This isn't free — we'd need engineers to build and maintain the pipeline. The question is whether the one-time engineering cost is justified by the recurring labor savings. For a lab that trains models regularly (which most R&D labs do), yes, this compounds quickly.\n2. The scope seems reasonable — it's not asking for a full MLOps platform rebuild, just metadata extraction and formatting into compliance structure. That's bounded.\n3. \"Structured compliance dashboard\" — we'd need to define what the fields are (per Article 12(3) for Annex III systems, or more broadly per Recital 71). But that's a one-time scoping exercise, not an ongoing resource drain.\n4. The proposal is well-bounded: it's a pipeline, not a platform. It uses existing artifacts. It automates a known pain point.\n\n**Decision:** This clears my bar easily. It addresses a real and recurring resource drain (manual documentation maintenance), leverages infrastructure we already have (Git, training metadata, dataset management), has bounded scope (a pipeline, not a new system), and directly serves the regulatory requirement for up-to-date technical documentation throughout the lifetime of the system. The one-time engineering investment is amortized across every model iteration and every compliance cycle. For a lab balancing scientific ambition against funding constraints, freeing researcher-hours from clerical documentation work back into actual experimentation is exactly the kind of resource-allocation efficiency I need to champion.\n\nI'd rate this highly — maybe 82-85. It's not a 100 because \"real-time\" extraction from training runs does require some engineering investment upfront, and there's integration risk with our existing infrastructure that could stretch timelines. But the fundamental logic is sound and the ROI is clear for a resource-constrained R&D lab.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
