y, identifying specific events). If you add differential privacy noise to a log entry about when a system was used or what input matched what reference database, you've potentially rendered the log useless for its intended regulatory purpose. This is a fundamental technical tension that makes the \"reconciliation\" goal dubious.\n- The proposal says \"experiment with... within our internal testing environments.\" This is research/exploration language, not prototyping language. It's an R&D project to *develop* methods, not to build a working prototype that satisfies Article 12.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- ZKP systems are notoriously slow to prototype and even slower to get production-ready. A development engineer looking at speed would see this as a multi-year research effort with uncertain outcomes, not something you can prototype quickly.\n- The proposal is explicitly framed as \"experiment\" and \"research project\" — it's exploring whether these ideas might work someday. But the instruction says: \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it, not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\" This is critical. This proposal IS essentially an exploration project dressed up as a targeted initiative.\n- The phrase \"transforms a regulatory burden into a publishable scientific contribution\" signals that the primary output envisioned is academic publication, not a working system.\n\n**Does it actually address Article 12?**\n- Article 12 requires: automatic recording of events, specific logging capabilities for traceability, and minimum requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (biometrics). The proposal doesn't specify how ZKPs or DP would fulfill these concrete requirements. It's vague about the actual engineering deliverable.\n- The regulation says logs should be kept and available for verification/compliance checking. Hiding them behind cryptographic layers doesn't necessarily help — regulators need to access the data. You'd still need a way to reveal it to authorized parties, which complicates the \"privacy-preserving\" angle.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering / prototyping speed perspective:\n- The technical viability of ZKPs for this specific use case is highly uncertain and unproven in production logging contexts.\n- Differential privacy applied to structured logs (timestamps, reference DB IDs) would likely destroy the information needed for compliance.\n- This is fundamentally a research/exploration project, not a prototyping deliverable. The language (\"experiment,\" \"develop cryptographic methods\") confirms this.\n- Speed: ZKP implementation timelines are typically 12-36 months minimum for anything production-grade.\n- Quality of prototype: You can't easily iterate on ZK circuits the way you iterate on standard software. Debugging is extremely difficult.\n\nThis proposal would NOT clear my bar as a development engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality. It's a research exploration project with high technical risk, uncertain applicability to the actual regulatory requirements, and no clear path to a working prototype in a reasonable timeframe. The \"conflict\" it tries to solve is also not clearly established by the regulation text — Article 12 requires you to keep logs, period.\n\nI'll reject this with a low promise score reflecting that while the ideas aren't insane in principle (privacy in logging is a real concern in broader data governance), from my specific vantage point of technical viability and prototyping speed, it fails.\n",
