ry commit or sprint), the overhead is manageable.\n- The risk of \"rebuilding systems late\" is real and significant. If you prototype a system without proper logging infrastructure baked in, retrofitting comprehensive lifetime logging can be extremely costly — especially for distributed AI systems where events span multiple services. This is a genuine technical concern.\n- On quality: catching logging gaps early does improve the final product's robustness. But a \"checklist\" approach risks being superficial — ticking boxes without verifying that the actual log infrastructure handles edge cases (log rotation, storage capacity over system lifetime, time synchronization across nodes, etc.).\n- The proposal says it will be conducted by \"the lab's lead architect.\" This is a bottleneck risk for prototyping speed if every project must wait for one person's review. But at milestone gates (not continuous), this is less problematic.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe idea has merit — integrating compliance verification into development milestones is good engineering practice and prevents costly late-stage rework. The technical feasibility of creating an Article 12 checklist is high. However, the proposal as stated is somewhat superficial: it describes a process (a gate, a checklist) without committing to specific technical infrastructure or architecture for the logging itself. From my vantage point, I care about whether this actually leads to technically viable logging implementations or just creates a bureaucratic checkbox exercise.\n\nThe proposal is directionally correct but underspecified on the technical side. It's a process improvement more than a technical solution. For prototyping speed, it adds one gate per milestone which is acceptable overhead. The \"mitigates financial risk of rebuilding\" argument is technically valid — retrofitting comprehensive logging into an AI system that wasn't designed with it in mind IS expensive.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar at a moder