        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1471\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The dual-use framing (compliance + reproducibility) is appealing in principle, but the proposal conflates Article 12's operational event-logging mandate with training-pipeline provenance metadata, which falls under Annex IV technical documentation rather than Art. 12 itself; building a bespoke 'immutable ledger' and retrofitting hooks across every pipeline is a disproportionate infrastructure commitment when we likely already have experiment-tracking and version-control tooling that could be extended at lower cost, and the 'absolute reproducibility' bar will lock in ongoing maintenance spend well beyond what the regulation actually requires of high-risk systems.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 42\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is P1471: \"Automated Provenance Engine\" - a system that captures full lineage of model development from data ingestion to deployment, integrating hooks into training pipelines to log hyperparameters, dataset hashes, and environment configurations in real-time, stored in an immutable ledger for audits and peer review.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- This is a substantial engineering undertaking. Building \"specialized hooks into our training pipelines\" across all of them is a significant development effort.\n- The \"immutable ledger\" infrastructure adds another layer of complexity and cost (storage, access management, potentially blockchain-like systems or at minimum append-only databases).\n- However, the proposal frames this as serving dual purposes: regulatory compliance AND scientific reproducibility. This means it's not purely a compliance cost — it generates value for the research mission itself.\n- The question is whether we already have partial infrastructure that could be leveraged rather than building from scratch.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The \"absolute reproducibility\" claim is ambitious. Full lineage capture across all model development stages is a large scope.\n- However, for high-risk AI systems under the EU AI Act, this logging is mandatory (Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events over lifetime). So this isn't optional ambition — it's a compliance requirement that we need to meet.\n- The key question: does Article 12 actually require ALL of what this proposes? Looking at the regulation:\n  - Article 12(1): automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime — yes, this aligns.\n  - Article 12(2): logging for traceability appropriate to intended purpose — this is about operational events (risks, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring), NOT necessarily training pipeline hyperparameters and dataset hashes.\n  - Article 12(3): specific minimum logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification) — start/end time, reference database, input data matches, identification of natural persons in verification.\n\nSo there's a mismatch here. The proposal conflates two things:\n1. Operational logging required by Article 12 (events during system operation/lifetime use)\n2. Training/reproducibility metadata (hyperparameters, dataset hashes, environment configs)\n\nArticle 12 is primarily about operational logs of the deployed system, not about training provenance. The technical documentation requirements (Article 11 and Annex IV) cover training details, but those are documentation, not \"automatic recording of events.\" The proposal's framing as responding to Article 12 is somewhat misaligned — it's more aligned with technical documentation and reproducibility needs.\n\nHowever, from a resource-efficiency standpoint:\n- If we're already going to build logging infrastructure for compliance (operational logs per Art. 12), integrating training pipeline hooks might be efficient if the architecture supports it.\n- But \"absolute reproducibility\" is a very high bar and may not be strictly required by the regulation for all systems.\n- The immutable ledger adds cost without clear regulatory necessity (the law says records should be kept, not that they must be on an immutable/append-only system).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- We likely already have some experiment tracking (MLflow, W&B, or similar) and environment management. The proposal seems to propose building a new system rather than extending existing ones.\n- \"Specialized hooks into our training pipelines\" suggests significant engineering work across all pipeline components.\n- The immutable ledger is additional infrastructure that may duplicate what we already have for version control.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has merit in direction (dual-use of compliance and research value), but it's over-scoped for the regulatory requirement as stated, potentially misaligned with what Article 12 actually demands (it conflates operational logging with training provenance), and the \"immutable ledger\" adds unnecessary complexity. The \"absolute reproducibility\" framing is scientifically aspirational but may not be proportionate to the funding available or strictly required by regulation.\n\nThat said, it's not a terrible idea. Training pipeline logging does have scientific value beyond compliance. But as written, it overreaches in scope and infrastructure requirements relative to what's needed, and doesn't clearly leverage existing infrastructure. It would consume significant engineering resources that could be directed more narrowly at the actual Article 12 requirement (operational event logging) plus a lighter-weight extension for reproducibility metadata.\n\nI'd lean toward reject because:\n- Scope is too broad for the regulatory trigger described\n- \"Immutable ledger\" is over-engineered for the requirement\n- Doesn't clearly identify how it leverages existing infrastructure\n- The conflation of Art. 12 operational logging with training provenance suggests the proposal may not precisely target what's legally required, risking resource misallocation\n- \"Absolute reproducibility\" sets a bar that will be very expensive to achieve and maintain\n\nBut I'll give it a moderate promise score because the underlying instinct (dual-use compliance + research value) is sound, and some version of this could work if scoped properly. Maybe 40-50? It's a decent idea in the wrong wrapper with too much scope for our constraints.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 42.\n",
