        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1524\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Decorator-based AOP logging middleware is a proven, low-intrusion pattern that directly externalises a repetitive compliance obligation out of per-project model code, which is exactly the kind of reusable infrastructure that accelerates prototyping. The main technical risk is that Art. 12(3) sub-requirements (reference-database identity, matched-input capture, verifier IDs) can sit deep inside inference pipelines where simple API-level hooks may not reach, so 'without altering core model logic' will likely need per-project adapter shims; still, the pattern is viable and a well-built middleware layer would cut logging scaffolding time substantially across our portfolio.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 80\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Universal High-Risk Logging Middleware\" — a reusable software layer with standardized API hooks and decorators that automatically captures system events, inputs, and timestamps across all high-risk prototypes, without altering core model logic.\n\nLet me assess this from my domain: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A logging middleware layer using decorators and API hooks is a well-established pattern in software engineering (similar to AOP - Aspect-Oriented Programming). This is technically sound and proven.\n- The key claim is \"without altering core model logic\" — this is achievable with decorator patterns for Python-based ML systems, or proxy/wrapper patterns in other languages. However, there are nuances: logging the \"reference database against which input data has been checked\" (Art. 12(3)(b)) and \"input data for which the search has led to a match\" (Art. 12(3)(c)) may require hooks deeper than simple API-level decorators — you might need instrumentation inside the model's inference pipeline or at the data layer.\n- The proposal says it logs \"data references and user IDs\" — this partially addresses Art. 12(3) requirements but doesn't explicitly mention all sub-points (start/end timestamps of each use, reference database, matched input data, natural persons involved in verification). Still, as a general middleware framework, these could be configurable hooks.\n- The \"universal\" claim is ambitious. Different high-risk systems have very different architectures — some are real-time inference servers, some are batch processing, some involve complex multi-step pipelines. A single middleware may need significant customization per system. However, the decorator/hook pattern does allow for this extensibility.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is where the proposal shines from my perspective. If we can drop-in a logging layer via decorators without rewriting core model code, that significantly reduces prototyping overhead. Instead of each new high-risk project spending weeks building custom logging infrastructure, they get it \"for free\" from the middleware.\n- However, there's a risk: over-engineering. Building a truly universal middleware upfront requires significant investment before any single prototype benefits. If the requirements across our prototypes are heterogeneous enough that the middleware needs 80% customization per project, it adds complexity rather than reducing it.\n- The \"standardized API hooks\" approach means we need to define an interface early and get buy-in from all teams. This has coordination costs but is a one-time investment.\n- Quality: having consistent, standardized logging across projects improves debuggability, compliance verification, and reduces the chance of missing a required log field.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is technically viable — the pattern exists and works. It directly serves my domain concern (prototyping speed) by externalizing a repetitive compliance requirement into infrastructure. The main risk is scope creep or over-generalization making it less useful than per-project custom logging for complex systems, but even as a partial solution covering 70% of cases, it's valuable.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a well-scoped, technically sound approach that directly addresses the prototyping speed concern by turning a per-project task into a reusable infrastructure component. The decorator/hook pattern is proven. The main caveat is that \"universal\" might be slightly optimistic — some complex systems may need additional custom instrumentation — but the core value proposition holds.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's solid, technically feasible, and directly serves my domain. Not a 90+ because \"universal\" across all high-risk prototypes is ambitious and the middleware itself has non-trivial build cost before it pays off. But it clearly clears the bar for approval from my vantage point.\n",
