        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1631 is about \"Privacy-Preserving Traceability Research\" - exploring zero-knowledge proofs and differentially private logging to create immutable logs that satisfy Article 12 without compromising data privacy or IP.\n\nLet me assess this from my perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) for AI system logging is an extremely research-heavy, frontier area. The technical challenge of proving properties about ML model behavior (inputs, outputs, reference databases used, matches found) without revealing the raw data is not solved in any practical sense today. ZKPs are computationally expensive and primarily designed for arithmetic circuits - applying them to arbitrary AI system logs (which involve complex model inference, database lookups, etc.) is far from tractable.\n- Differentially private logging: Adding differential privacy to event logs fundamentally changes the information content of those logs. Article 12(3) requires specific data points: start/end times, reference database identity, input data that led to a match, and identification of natural persons involved in verification. Differential privacy would degrade or eliminate precisely these required fields. You can't differentially private \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\" - it's an identifier, not a numerical value where noise injection makes sense.\n- The proposal conflates \"privacy\" with what the regulation actually requires. Article 12 is about traceability and compliance verification. The regulation explicitly requires recording specific data points. You can't ZKP your way around storing that the system used Database X to check Input Y and found Match Z - because the whole point is verifiability by regulators.\n- \"Immutable logs\" adds another layer of complexity (blockchain? append-only storage?) that doesn't directly address Article 12's requirements.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is framed as a \"scientific initiative\" and \"research\" - it's explicitly exploratory, not implementation-oriented.\n- The timeline to produce a working prototype using ZKPs for AI logging would be measured in years, if it's even technically feasible at the scale and latency requirements of production AI systems.\n- There's no clear path from this research to a shippable component that an engineering team could integrate into an AI system.\n- The publication goal (\"publish these findings\") signals academic research rather than product development.\n\n**Does it actually solve Article 12?**\n- The regulation requires specific, concrete data points in logs. ZKPs don't help you meet those requirements - they help you verify properties without revealing underlying data. But the regulator needs to SEE the data (or at least verify its existence and content) for compliance purposes.\n- Differential privacy would likely make the logs non-compliant because they'd fail to provide the specific information required by Article 12(3).\n- The \"tension between transparency and confidentiality\" is somewhat overstated - the regulation already has data protection requirements (GDPR applies), and those are handled through access controls, encryption at rest, etc., not through obliterating the log content.\n\n**From a prototyping/technical viability standpoint:**\nThis is a research moonshot with no clear path to a deployable solution that meets Article 12's specific technical requirements. The fundamental approach (ZKPs, differential privacy) conflicts with what the regulation actually asks for. The engineering team cannot prototype this in any reasonable timeframe, and even if the math worked out, the latency/cost of ZKP verification would be prohibitive for production AI systems.\n\nThis is not technically viable as a solution to Article 12 within any reasonable development timeline. It's an interesting academic question but doesn't serve our engineering mandate. I should reject this.\n\nPromise score: From my vantage point, this has very low promise for producing a deployable, compliant logging system. The technical approaches proposed are fundamentally misaligned with what the regulation requires (specific verifiable data points), and the research timeline is incompatible with our need to ship working prototypes. I'd put this at maybe 8-12. There's some intellectual interest but it doesn't clear my bar for committing implementation resources.\n",
