fication of natural persons involved in verification (Art 14(5)) - NOT explicitly addressed\n\n**Recital 71:** Requires logs over duration of lifetime, technical documentation kept up to date. The proposal addresses the logging aspect well.\n\n**Compliance Officer assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly implements Article 12(1)'s core requirement (automatic recording)\n- Addresses traceability which is central to compliance verification\n- \"Immutable record\" supports audit trail requirements - if records can be tampered with, regulators won't accept them\n- Removing manual overhead reduces human error in logging, which is a real compliance risk\n- Standardized middleware means consistent implementation across all high-risk models (important since we develop multiple systems)\n- Distributed ledger or secure database provides integrity guarantees\n\nConcerns:\n- \"Distributed ledger\" is over-engineered for this purpose and may introduce its own complexity/risks. A secure, access-controlled database with proper audit trails would suffice and be easier to manage. However, the proposal says \"or secure database,\" so it's not mandating blockchain.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention all Article 12(3) minimums (particularly person identification for Art 14(5), and match-specific data). But as a framework, these could be added as modules.\n- \"Absolute traceability\" is a strong claim - no system achieves absolute anything. For regulatory purposes we need \"appropriate\" traceability (per Art 12(2)). This overclaim could create internal expectations that are hard to meet, but it doesn't undermine the proposal's value.\n- The scope says \"all high-risk models\" which is correct per Article 12's applicability.\n- Integration into inference pipelines is the right architectural choice - logging must be technical and automatic, not post-hoc documentation.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This is core infrastructure that directly implements a mandatory regulator