ive to Article 12's current requirements — the Act requires logging capability, not necessarily \"provable traceability\" in the sense of semantic transparency baked into architecture\n3. A research project with publication goals (top-tier AI conferences) — this is more about intellectual merit and lab reputation than compliance\n4. However, from a market reputation angle, pioneering in explainability/transparency could position the institution favorably as regulations tighten\n\n**Compliance-specific concerns:**\n- Article 12 requires specific, concrete logging capabilities NOW for systems already classified as high-risk. This proposal is theoretical research that may or may not produce implementable results\n- The Act's requirements are specific and operational — you need logs of start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, verifier IDs. This proposal doesn't directly address these\n- However, the broader direction (traceability, transparency) aligns with the spirit of Recital 71 which emphasizes \"comprehensible information\" and \"clear and comprehensive form\"\n- From a market reputation standpoint, publishing in top-tier conferences on AI explainability could enhance the lab's standing as a compliance-forward institution\n- But there's also risk: if you publish speculative research claiming \"provable traceability\" and regulators later find your actual systems don't meet Article 12's specific requirements, that could be damaging\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- This is a pure research investment with uncertain timeline to any compliance-relevant output\n- It doesn't directly implement any Article 12 requirement for existing or upcoming high-risk systems\n- Resources committed here are resources NOT spent on implementing the actual logging infrastructure needed NOW\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- Positive: positions lab as forward-thinking in transparency\n- Negative: if the research is purely theoretical and published without practical 