ication). A generic \"data lineage\" mapper may not automatically capture all these specific fields without careful design. The proposal glosses over this specificity.\n- \"Absolute traceability of the knowledge frontier's evolution\" is somewhat vague and grandiose. From a compliance perspective, I need specific, verifiable logs tied to defined events, not an abstract notion of \"knowledge frontier.\"\n- The scope says \"for every high-risk system run\" — but Article 12(3) only applies the minimum logging requirements to Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). Other high-risk categories have different requirements. A one-size-fits-all approach might over-engineer for some and under-deliver for others.\n- \"Middleware layer\" and \"versioned data lake\" are architectural choices that may or may not be appropriate. From a compliance standpoint, I care about whether the output meets the regulatory requirements, not the specific architecture. However, a versioned data lake does support the \"lifetime\" requirement well.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address log retention periods, access controls, or integrity (tamper-evidence) — which are important for regulatory compliance even if not explicitly in Article 12 text, they're implied by the need to \"verify compliance.\"\n- \"Removes the administrative burden from researchers\" is a nice side benefit but isn't a compliance requirement per se.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires — automatic logging, traceability over lifetime, and reducing manual intervention improves reliability of the record-keeping obligation. The core concept (automated data lineage tracking) directly supports the \"automatic recording\" mandate. However, the proposal is somewhat high-level and makes a strong compliance claim (\"Article 12 compliant logs\") without detailing how it maps to the specific granular requirements of Article 12(3). It also doesn't address log integrity, retention, or access controls.\n\nThat said, as a screening decision: the core direction is correct and addresses a real regulatory obligation. The concept is sound and directly responsive to the regulation. My concern is that it's framed as a complete solution (\"generates Article 12 compliant logs\") when in reality it would need significant tailoring per system category and would need additional controls (integrity, retention, access). But as an implementation commitment, building this infrastructure is necessary for compliance — you can't meet Article 12 without some form of automated logging infrastructure.\n\nI think I'd approve this because:\n1. The core capability (automatic event recording) is explicitly mandated by Article 12(1)\n2. Data lineage mapping supports the traceability requirements in Article 12(2)\n3. Versioned storage supports lifetime documentation per Recital 71\n4. Removing manual intervention reduces compliance risk from human error\n5. Without this, we simply cannot meet our obligations\n\nMy concerns (specificity of fields, retention, integrity controls) are implementation details that can be addressed during development. The strategic direction is correct and necessary.\n\nPromise score: This is a core regulatory requirement. Not having automated logging infrastructure would put us out of compliance. However, the proposal as stated is somewhat overreaching in its claim of \"absolute\" compliance and doesn't address all the specific sub-requirements. I'd say around 72-78. It's necessary but not sufficient on its own. Let me settle on 75.\n\nDecision: approve (it addresses a mandatory regulatory obligation, the direction is correct)\nPromise: 75\n",
