on to be kept up to date.\n5. \"Shared\" means it's a one-time infrastructure investment that benefits all projects in the lab — high leverage per dollar spent.\n6. The dual benefit (compliance + reproducibility) means the scientific output is improved, which strengthens grant applications and publication records — addressing funding constraints indirectly.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n1. Integration into the compute cluster's orchestration layer could have performance overhead — if done poorly, it slows down training runs, wasting expensive GPU time. But this is a design risk, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n2. \"Shared middleware layer\" requires some upfront engineering effort and ongoing maintenance. Who maintains it? This is a resource allocation question.\n3. It captures training hyperparameters, dataset versions, runtime execution logs — but does it fully cover Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification)? The proposal seems more oriented toward the general case. However, as a foundational layer, it could be extended. This is a gap but not necessarily a reason to reject.\n4. The scope seems reasonable — it's not trying to solve everything, just the core logging and provenance capture that Article 12 requires.\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency assessment:**\n- High leverage: one infrastructure investment serves compliance + reproducibility for all projects\n- Uses existing infrastructure (compute cluster orchestration) rather than building new systems\n- Reduces recurring manual effort (no researcher time spent on logging)\n- The \"shared\" nature means marginal cost per additional project is low\n\n**Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints:**\n- This proposal actually enhances scientific output (reproducibility improves publication quality, which helps with grant renewals)\n- It doesn't divert significant research talent — it's an infrastructure/engineering investment\n- The automation aspect protects researcher time