nger need a separate \"write the docs\" step after each experiment.\n- The toolchain approach means once built, every new project benefits immediately - high leverage.\n\n**Concerns/limitations:**\n- MLflow captures hyperparameters and metrics well, but some of Article 12's requirements (like \"reference database against which input data has been checked\" for Annex III point 1(a) systems) may need additional instrumentation beyond standard experiment tracking.\n- The proposal focuses on training-side documentation; runtime logging (Article 12(1)) is a separate concern that this doesn't address. But that's fine - it's scoped to technical documentation, not operational logs.\n- \"Auto-generates required technical documentation\" - the mapping from raw metadata to compliant document format needs careful design to ensure all regulatory requirements are met. This isn't trivial but is manageable.\n- The proposal says \"replaces manual reporting with live data\" - in practice there will still be some human review/annotation needed, but the bulk of structured data capture becomes automatic.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a high-value, technically straightforward proposal. It leverages mature tooling (MLflow or equivalents), addresses a real workflow pain point that directly impacts prototyping speed, and aligns well with what Article 12 requires for technical documentation to be \"kept up to date.\" The implementation risk is low because the underlying technology is proven. The ROI on engineering time is high - build it once, benefit every subsequent project.\n\nI would approve this. It's a clear win for prototyping velocity and technical compliance simultaneously. The promise score should be high - maybe 82-85. I'm not giving 90+ because there are some implementation details around ensuring the auto-generated docs actually meet all regulatory formatting/content requirements, and the scope could get complicated if you're trying to cover every Ann