ounds like an academic research initiative rather than a compliance implementation effort. It doesn't directly implement Article 12 requirements in our systems.\n- The primary goal stated is \"intellectual merit\" and \"pioneering the field\" — not ensuring our high-risk AI systems technically allow for automatic recording of events as required by Article 12(1). It's using logs as a research subject rather than building the logging infrastructure to meet regulatory obligations.\n- \"Design synthetic failure modes\" — this is exploratory research, not compliance implementation. Compliance needs us to have working logging systems in our deployed high-risk AI systems NOW (or within applicable timelines), not research into how forensically interesting those logs might be.\n- The proposal doesn't address the specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (recording periods, reference databases, input data matching, identification of natural persons). It's generic about \"Article 12 logs.\"\n- Publication: while it could enhance reputation, there's also a risk. Publishing that our log-based forensic methods have limitations or gaps could create regulatory exposure. If we publicly demonstrate what CAN'T be reconstructed from logs, regulators might use that against us. Also, publishing methodology details could raise IP concerns for the institution.\n- \"Committing scarce implementation resources to pursue it\" — this research project diverts resources away from actual compliance work (implementing logging infrastructure, updating technical documentation, ensuring post-market monitoring systems are functional).\n\n**Key assessment**: This is a well-meaning but misaligned proposal from a pure compliance perspective. It treats Article 12 logs as an object of scientific study rather than a regulatory obligation to implement and maintain. The value it offers to compliance (understanding log capabilities) is real but secondary — the primary stated purpose is research publication and intellectual pioneering. A compliance officer would want resources focused on ensuring our systems MEET Article 12, not studying what can be done with the data they produce.\n\nHowever, there IS a legitimate compliance angle: understanding the diagnostic power of your logs helps you ensure they're sufficient for their regulatory purposes (Article 12(2) requires \"appropriate to the intended purpose\"). If you don't understand what your logs can and cannot reveal, you might have gaps in your post-market monitoring capability.\n\nBut the framing as a \"research project\" with publication goals, synthetic failure modes, and pioneering AI forensics makes this more of an academic R&D initiative than a compliance action. It doesn't directly advance institutional survival or ensure regulatory compliance of our deployed systems. The reputation benefit is speculative (publication might help, but could also expose gaps).\n\n**Decision**: I'm leaning toward reject. The proposal's primary value proposition is intellectual/research merit, not compliance. While there's a tangential compliance connection (understanding log utility), this isn't a compliance implementation or risk-mitigation action. It diverts resources from actual compliance work. The publication angle adds reputational risk without clear compliance benefit.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Could I approve this at a low promise level? The argument for approval: it does engage with Article 12 content, and understanding forensic capabilities of logs could inform whether our logging is sufficient for regulatory purposes. But the proposal as written doesn't say \"we will use findings to improve our compliance posture\" — it says we'll publish a new methodology. That's an academic goal, not a compliance goal.\n\nI'll reject this. From my vantage point, this doesn't directly serve institutional survival, regulatory compliance, or market reputation in a meaningful way. It's a research curiosity that tangentially touches on logs but whose stated purpose is publication and intellectual pioneering, not ensuring our systems meet Article 12 obligations.\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 15-20. There's a slim thread of relevance (understanding log capabilities), but the framing, goals, and resource commitment are misaligned with compliance objectives.\n",
