 Article 12's requirement that logging must be over the lifetime of the system. You can't easily bolt on logging after deployment; it needs to be in the architecture from design (or at least before market placement).\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level and doesn't specify methodology, timeline, or deliverables. But that's typical for a budget-cycle proposal.\n- It says \"comprehensive audit\" — could scope creep. But as a compliance function, I'd want it comprehensive.\n- It focuses on R&D pipelines specifically, which is correct because that's where we control the architecture before deployment.\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This is a core compliance activity. Without knowing whether our systems meet Article 12, we cannot claim conformity, cannot issue CE marking for high-risk systems, and expose ourselves to enforcement.\n- The timing (before deployment) is critical because Article 12 requires the technical capability to exist in the system; it's not something you can paper over with documentation alone.\n- Market reputation: if a regulator or competitor discovers we deployed non-compliant logging architecture, the reputational hit would be severe.\n- This is essentially due diligence for our own compliance obligations.\n\nI would approve this. It directly addresses my core mandate. The promise score should be high — maybe 85-90. I'd reserve 100 for proposals that are perfectly specified with clear KPIs and timelines, but the substance here is exactly right for a compliance officer to champion. The slight vagueness in methodology and lack of specific deliverables keeps it from being a perfect score, but the strategic direction is spot-on.\n\nLet me settle on 87: very high value, directly serves my mandate, well-timed, but the proposal could be more specific on scope boundaries (what counts as \"existing systems\" — does it include prototypes? early-stage R&D that may never