is protects the balance between meeting regulatory obligations and maintaining productive R&D output. Manual logging would be a significant tax on researcher time.\n4. **Scalability**: A standardized API means that as new high-risk systems come online (new research projects), compliance is \"baked in\" rather than bolted on later, which would be more expensive.\n\nArguments AGAINST or concerns:\n1. **Upfront development cost**: Building the middleware itself requires engineering resources. There's a significant upfront investment before any benefit is realized. If we only have 1-2 high-risk systems currently, the ROI might not justify building shared infrastructure versus simpler per-project solutions.\n2. **Integration complexity**: Getting researchers to integrate a new API into their existing training/inference loops has its own friction. It could disrupt workflows initially.\n3. **Maintenance burden**: The middleware itself needs ongoing maintenance, updates as Article 12 requirements evolve or Annex III categories change, and support for new model architectures that don't fit the existing pipeline patterns.\n4. **\"Optimizes use of existing compute resources\"** — this claim is somewhat vague. Logging does add some I/O overhead and storage. The proposal asserts it's optimized but doesn't detail how. However, automated structured logging is generally more efficient than ad-hoc manual approaches, so this seems reasonable.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom the Lab Director's perspective focused on resource efficiency and protecting scientific productivity under funding constraints, this is a strong proposal. The core value proposition is correct: compliance is mandatory, it will recur across multiple systems, and a shared solution avoids N× duplication of effort. The integration into existing loops (rather than standalone tools) is the right architectural choice for minimizing disruption.\n\nThe main risk is the upfront engineering cost and whether we have enough high-r