        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P1147 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze proposal P1147:\n\n\"High-Risk Classification and Logging Audit Protocol. A mandatory review gate at the project initiation phase to determine if a system falls under Article 12's high-risk categories. The Lab Director and legal counsel will review project charters to assign specific logging requirements based on Annex III criteria before funding is released. This ensures we do not over-engineer logs for low-risk systems. It prevents wasteful resource allocation on unnecessary record-keeping.\"\n\nFrom a compliance perspective:\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n1. A mandatory review gate at project initiation is actually very aligned with regulatory compliance. Article 12 applies specifically to \"high-risk AI systems,\" so correctly identifying which systems are high-risk before implementation is critical. If you misclassify, you either (a) fail to implement required logging on a system that IS high-risk (non-compliance, fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover), or (b) over-implement on non-high-risk systems (waste, though not illegal).\n2. Having both Lab Director AND legal counsel review is good — it's not just an engineering judgment call; legal input ensures the classification aligns with regulatory interpretation.\n3. Reviewing project charters against Annex III criteria before funding release creates a clear accountability trail and prevents scope creep where a system drifts into high-risk territory without reclassification.\n4. This directly supports Article 12 compliance by ensuring the right logging requirements are applied to the right systems.\n\n**Potential concerns from a pure compliance standpoint:**\n1. The proposal says \"before funding is released\" — this is