ipelines as a gate before deployment.\n- The tool itself is relatively simple and unlikely to introduce bugs that affect the AI system's behavior — it's a read-only verification tool.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The \"simulating regulatory audits\" language might imply scope creep beyond what's technically needed (just verifying log presence/structure). But the core value is clear: automated log validation.\n- It requires close coordination with legal/compliance to maintain an accurate checklist, but that's a process concern, not a technical viability issue.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, the required fields are well-defined (start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, verifier IDs), making the checklist concrete and implementable.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage:**\nThis is technically viable, relatively fast to prototype, and would improve engineering quality by catching logging gaps early. It's a pragmatic, low-risk tool that fits well into existing development workflows. From a speed/quality of prototyping standpoint, this is a good investment — it's not a massive undertaking but provides concrete value in the dev cycle.\n\nI'd approve this. The promise score should reflect that while it's solid and useful, it's also somewhat routine engineering work (not groundbreaking) and its value depends on maintaining an accurate regulatory checklist. I'd put it around 72-78 — clearly worthwhile, technically straightforward, good ROI in terms of prototyping speed, but not a transformative capability.\n\nLet me settle on 74.\n",
