        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1230\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A unified logging SDK directly operationalises Article 12(1)'s core mandate—'technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system'—by embedding traceability capability at the architectural layer rather than leaving it to individual project teams, which is precisely the fragmentation pattern that invites regulatory findings during market-surveillance audits. The main compliance caveat is the single configuration flag: for any system in active high-risk deployment, logging must be non-optional and non-disableable by a developer; the SDK's governance model needs to enforce mandatory logging as a deployment gate, not a per-developer choice. Additionally, the described capture set (timestamps, input data, system state) should be explicitly mapped to each sub-clause of Art. 12(2)(a)-(c) and, where applicable, Art. 12(3)'s Annex III minimum fields before the schema is frozen.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P1230 as the Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically regarding Article 12 (Record-keeping). My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Proposal P1230: \"Unified Compliance Logging SDK\"**\n\nThe proposal describes a modular internal library with standardized API calls for automatic event recording across all high-risk prototypes. Key features:\n- Standardized API calls for automatic event recording\n- Consistent telemetry without forcing each researcher to design their own schema\n- Python wrapper integrating with current orchestration tools\n- Captures timestamps, input data, and system state automatically\n- Single configuration flag to toggle compliance-level logging\n- Minimizes engineering overhead per project while guaranteeing technical capability for traceability\n\n**Compliance Assessment:**\n\nStrengths from a compliance perspective:\n1. Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified SDK that provides this capability across all prototypes directly addresses this requirement. This is a strong alignment.\n\n2. The standardized approach ensures consistency, which is critical for compliance. If each team designs its own logging schema, you get fragmentation, gaps, and inconsistency — exactly what regulators would flag during audits or market surveillance.\n\n3. \"Guaranteeing the technical capability for traceability\" aligns with Recital 71's emphasis on comprehensible information for traceability and verification of compliance.\n\n4. The single configuration flag is interesting — it means compliance logging can be enabled/disabled. From a compliance standpoint, this is a double-edged sword. It ensures researchers CAN enable it (reducing the chance they forget), but it also means someone could turn it OFF. For high-risk systems, logging should be non-optional during operation. However, as a development tool for prototypes that may not yet be classified as high-risk, this flexibility is reasonable.\n\n5. The SDK approach ensures that even early-stage prototypes have the technical capability built in from the start, rather than retrofitting logging later (which is error-prone and often incomplete).\n\nConcerns:\n1. Article 12(2) specifies that logging must capture events relevant for: (a) identifying risk situations or substantial modifications, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring, and (c) monitoring operation under Article 26(5). The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input data, and system state\" but doesn't explicitly address all three sub-requirements. However, this is a high-level description of the SDK's approach; the detailed schema would be defined in implementation.\n\n2. Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification) requires specific minimum data points: period of each use, reference database, input data for matches, and identification of natural persons involved in verification. The proposal doesn't specifically address these, but again, this is a general SDK description.\n\n3. The \"single configuration flag\" concern: For deployed high-risk systems, logging must be active. If the flag can be turned off by a developer without oversight, that's a compliance risk. However, for prototypes in development (which is what this lab does), having it toggleable during development and mandatory before deployment is reasonable.\n\n4. The proposal says \"across all high-risk prototypes\" — good scope definition. Not overreaching to non-high-risk systems where the requirement doesn't apply.\n\n5. From a market reputation standpoint: Having a unified, well-documented compliance logging infrastructure demonstrates organizational maturity to regulators and clients. It shows the institution takes traceability seriously at the architectural level rather than as an afterthought.\n\n6. Institutional survival angle: A single SDK reduces the risk of a regulatory finding that \"Team X didn't implement proper logging.\" Centralized responsibility for compliance features is operationally sound.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from a compliance standpoint. It directly addresses Article 12(1)'s core requirement (technical capability for automatic recording), promotes consistency across the organization, and reduces the risk of non-compliance due to individual team variation. The main concern is ensuring the SDK's default configuration for deployed systems makes logging mandatory (non-toggleable), and that the schema covers all sub-requirements in Article 12(2) and potentially 12(3). But as a foundational infrastructure proposal, it's well-aligned with compliance needs.\n\nI'll approve this. Promise score: From a compliance vantage point, this is highly valuable. It addresses the core technical requirement of Article 12 systematically. I'd put it around 82-85. The \"toggle\" feature introduces a small risk that needs governance oversight, and the description doesn't explicitly map to all sub-clauses of Article 12(2)(a)-(c) or 12(3). But as an infrastructure backbone, it's exactly what compliance needs. I'll go with 84.\n",
