ompliance document\" approach means we're not rebuilding documentation from scratch each cycle, which is cost-effective over time.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- MLflow is already in the lab's stack (the proposal references \"our MLflow instances\"). This means no new infrastructure procurement.\n- The bridge pattern (existing tool → regulatory template) is a thin integration layer, not a system replacement.\n- Model promotion events (testing/production) are likely already tracked; we're just hooking into existing workflows.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Integrated system\" could be scope-creep territory — how complex does the \"bridge\" need to be? But the description is reasonably bounded: MLflow → regulatory template, triggered by model promotion events.\n- The proposal focuses on Recital 71's documentation requirements but doesn't explicitly address Article 12(1)'s automatic logging requirement (event logs over lifetime). However, that might be a separate workstream or already handled elsewhere. Actually, re-reading: the proposal says \"automatically captures training parameters, dataset versions, and validation results\" — this is more about the technical documentation side than the event-logging side. But Recital 71 does say \"keeping records AND the availability of technical documentation,\" so this addresses a significant portion.\n- The scope seems appropriately bounded: it's an integration layer, not a ground-up platform build.\n\n**Assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is a strong proposal. It:\n1. Leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow) — no new procurement needed\n2. Reduces ongoing labor costs (automates what would be manual work)\n3. Addresses a real regulatory requirement with a proportionate solution\n4. Is bounded in scope (a bridge/integration, not a new system)\n5. Frees researcher time back to scientific work\n\nThe main risk is that \"integrated system\" language could balloon scope, but the described implementation (br