        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate a single proposal (P1120) as the Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal from my vantage point:\n\n**Proposal P1120: \"Automated Compliance Logging Framework\"**\n\nThe proposal describes a standardized software library/middleware layer that would:\n- Automatically record system events and telemetry for all high-risk AI projects\n- Capture input data, timestamps, and decision triggers across different models\n- Store logs in an immutable database for auditability\n- Satisfy Article 12(1) consistently without manual developer effort\n\n**Assessment from Compliance Officer perspective:**\n\nArticle 12 requirements:\n- Art 12(1): High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\n- Art 12(2): Logging must enable recording of events relevant for: (a) identifying risk situations/substantial modifications, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring, (c) monitoring operation per Article 26(5).\n- Art 12(3): For biometric systems (Annex III point 1(a)), minimum requirements include specific data points.\n\n**Strengths from compliance perspective:**\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) — automatic logging over lifetime\n- Standardization across all high-risk AI projects is excellent for regulatory consistency — regulators hate patchwork approaches\n- Immutable storage supports auditability, which is critical for demonstrating compliance to notified bodies and market surveillance authorities\n- \"By default\" design means compliance isn't an afterthought but built-in — this aligns with the \"design by regulation\" approach the Act encourages\n- Reduces reliance on manual developer effort, which reduces human error risk (a significant compliance gap)\n- Cross