roval from my vantage point. It directly addresses the most critical operational requirement of Article 12(1), standardizes our approach across projects (reducing regulatory risk), and builds institutional capability that supports multiple other obligations. The gaps I noted (specific Art 12(3) requirements, explicit mapping to 12(2)(a)-(c)) are implementation details that would be addressed during development but don't negate the strategic value of committing resources to this framework.\n\n**Promise score:** This is a high-value commitment for compliance. It's not a 100 because there are gaps in scope (doesn't explicitly address all sub-requirements, doesn't mention retention policies, access controls, or the specific Annex III 1(a) minimums), and \"middleware across different models\" is technically ambitious with potential integration challenges that could delay delivery. But it's fundamentally aligned with our most critical regulatory obligation. I'd say around 82-85. Let me settle on 83 — it addresses the core requirement well, standardization is excellent for institutional compliance posture, but the proposal as stated has scope gaps relative to the full text of Article 12 that would need to be filled in before we could call it complete.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. From a pure compliance perspective, this is one of the most important infrastructure investments we can make. Without it, every high-risk system we ship is non-compliant by default. The fact that it's \"by default\" and \"automated\" removes the single biggest compliance risk (developer forgetfulness/inconsistency). I'll go with 84.\n",
