        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1986\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core deliverable—authenticating a reviewer, capturing their credential against a specific output ID, and writing that to an append-only/immutable log—is a well-bounded integration task built from standard components (SSO/OAuth, audit-log storage, cryptographic hashing for tamper-evidence). A competent team can produce a working prototype in a matter of weeks; there is no research risk or novel algorithmic challenge. The only mild execution uncertainty is coupling to the existing dashboard architecture and keeping the 'secure' scope from ballooning into a full PKI/zero-trust redesign, but those are manageable with a tight iteration loop.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1986 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Human Verification Attribution Module\" — a specialized interface for recording the identity of natural persons who verify high-risk AI outputs, specifically targeting Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification/verification). It involves:\n- Secure authentication layer\n- Digital sign-off mechanism\n- Embedded within model evaluation dashboards\n- Links output IDs to verified user credentials in an immutable log\n\nFrom my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- A secure authentication layer + digital sign-off is a well-understood engineering problem. OAuth, SSO, JWT tokens, audit logs — all standard building blocks. Not novel, not risky technically.\n- \"Immutable log\" suggests something like an append-only database, blockchain-like structure, or at minimum a write-once storage with cryptographic hashing. Again, well-trodden ground.\n- Embedding it in existing model evaluation dashboards means integrating with whatever frontend/backend stack already exists. The coupling is straightforward: add a verification endpoint that captures user identity + output ID + timestamp and writes to the log store.\n- The \"without disrupting the speed of the validation process\" claim is reasonable — a sign-off click or API call adds negligible latency to an otherwise human-paced review workflow.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- This is a relatively bounded, well-scoped module. It's not a research problem; it's an engineering implementation task with clear requirements (record who verified what output, when, against which system).\n- The scope is narrow: authentication + attribution + logging. No ML model training, no complex pipeline redesign.\n- A competent team could prototype this in weeks, not months. The \"secure\" part might add some complexity (key management, audit trail integrity), but it's still within standard DevSecOps practices.\n- Quality considerations: the main risk is ensuring the immutable log is actually tamper-evident and that the authentication can't be spoofed. But these are solved problems with known patterns.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Embedded within our model evaluation dashboards\" — this assumes a specific architecture. If the dashboards aren't already structured to track individual output IDs through verification, there might be integration friction. But this is manageable.\n- The proposal says it \"fulfills legal accountability requirements without disrupting the speed of the validation process.\" From a prototyping standpoint, I'd want to confirm that the sign-off mechanism doesn't add UX friction that slows down human reviewers in practice. A quick prototype could validate this.\n- Is there a risk this is over-engineered? A simple \"user X verified output Y at time T\" log with proper authentication might be sufficient. The \"secure authentication layer\" and \"digital sign-off mechanism\" language sounds like it could balloon scope, but the core functionality is straightforward.\n\n**Verdict from my perspective:**\nThis is technically very viable. It's a bounded engineering task with well-known components. Prototyping speed should be high — this isn't an exploratory or research-heavy proposal. The quality bar is achievable with standard practices. I'd approve this. It's not groundbreaking, but it's exactly the kind of \"doable, scoped, clear-requirement\" engineering work that a dev team can ship quickly and reliably.\n\nPromise score: This is solid, low-risk, well-scoped engineering. From my vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality), I'd rate this quite high. It's not the most exciting project, but it's exactly what clears a development bar efficiently. I'd say around 82-85. It's very doable, clearly scope