al privacy angle**: Applying DP to individual regulatory events is problematic because the whole point of Article 12(3) is per-event traceability (specific start/end times, specific reference database checks, specific input data matches). DP typically adds noise or aggregates — both seem incompatible with the granular event-level logging requirement. This part of the proposal may be less well-matched to the regulatory requirements than the ZKP angle.\n\nOverall assessment: The core research question — how to provide verifiable compliance evidence while minimizing exposure of sensitive data in logs — is at a genuine knowledge frontier. It's intellectually meritorious and has broader impact potential beyond EU compliance. However, there are significant feasibility questions (especially around the identification requirement in Art. 12(3)(d) and the per-event granularity vs. ZKP performance). The proposal is ambitious but well-motivated by a real tension.\n\nI think this clears my bar as a research frontier question with genuine intellectual merit and potential broader impact, even if some of the specific technical approaches (especially DP on individual events) are more speculative than others. It's not a slam-dunk 90+, because of the feasibility concerns, but it's clearly above the reject threshold for a knowledge-frontier researcher. I'd put it around 72-78.\n\nLet me settle: The proposal addresses a real gap in the literature at the intersection of cryptography and AI governance. The ZKP angle is novel for this specific regulatory context. The DP angle is weaker but still an interesting exploration. The broader impact on \"governance by design\" research is significant. I'll approve with a promise score around 74.\n",
