*\n- This actually supports scientific ambition by protecting exploratory research from premature compliance overhead. If researchers fear that starting any AI project triggers full record-keeping obligations, they'll be reluctant to explore novel directions. A clear boundary says \"you can explore freely until you cross into high-risk territory.\"\n- The peer-review committee could become a bottleneck if not well-designed. But as a pre-development gate (not during development), it's a one-time cost per project.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A central registry is lightweight — it could be maintained in existing project management tools rather than requiring new systems. The \"peer-review committee\" could leverage existing internal review structures (e.g., ethics boards, technical review panels) rather than creating entirely new bodies.\n- This proposal doesn't require significant new infrastructure — it's primarily a process/governance change.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The risk is that this becomes overly bureaucratic and slows down project initiation. If every single research project needs committee review before starting, even obviously low-risk ones, we've created overhead.\n- The \"central registry\" sounds like it could grow into an unnecessary administrative apparatus.\n- However, the proposal specifically says it's to prevent \"unnecessary application of restrictive record-keeping burdens to purely exploratory, low-risk frontier research\" — this is squarely in my wheelhouse.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a high-value, relatively low-cost governance intervention that directly addresses my core concern of resource-allocation efficiency. The main risk is over-bureaucratization, but the proposal as stated focuses on preventing misallocation rather than adding compliance layers. It's a prerequisite for efficient compliance — you can't allocate implementation resources to Article 12 logging if you don't know which projects are actually i