mation or introduces ambiguity, that's a compliance gap.\n  - Asynchronous logging means there's a window between event occurrence and log entry. In a crash scenario, events in the buffer could be lost. This creates a traceability gap — exactly what Article 12 is designed to prevent. Recital 71 emphasizes \"comprehensible information\" and \"clear and comprehensive form.\"\n  - The integrity of logs is critical for regulatory inspection. If an auditor asks for logs from a specific time window, asynchronous buffering could mean some events aren't yet written to persistent storage.\n\n- On the positive side: if logging overhead is so severe that it makes the system commercially unviable, the institution faces existential risk — but that's more of a business/technical concern than a compliance one per se. The compliance obligation doesn't get lighter because implementation is expensive.\n\n- Market reputation angle: A lab known for \"optimizing away\" regulatory requirements (even in good faith) could damage its credibility with regulators. The framing of \"preventing regulatory requirements from compromising scientific ambition\" suggests a tension between compliance and research goals, which could be read as adversarial toward the regulation.\n\n- From an institutional survival standpoint: this proposal doesn't directly help or hurt compliance. It's tangential. The actual compliance obligation is to HAVE logging that meets the spec. Making it faster is nice-to-have but not a compliance requirement. Investing scarce resources here rather than in actually ensuring logs meet Art 12(2) and (3) specifications could be misallocation.\n\n- Risk: If \"compressed data formats\" are interpreted as lossy compression, this could actively VIOLATE the spirit of Article 12 by making records less comprehensible or complete. This is a genuine compliance risk introduced by the proposal itself.\n\nMy assessment: The proposal is tangential to actual compliance obligations and introduces