oint 1(a) systems: start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, identification of verifying persons).\n  - A \"compliance checklist\" without specificity on what the checklist covers could become a rubber-stamp exercise.\n  - It says \"verifies logging capabilities before any system is labeled as production-ready\" — but Article 12 also requires logs be maintained \"over the lifetime of the system.\" A pre-production gate doesn't address ongoing log integrity, retention, or access for authorities (Article 86 gives market surveillance authorities access to documentation).\n  - The proposal focuses on \"reputation during external regulatory audits\" which is fine but somewhat narrow — compliance isn't just about surviving audits; it's about meeting obligations continuously.\n\n- **Net assessment from my vantage point:** This is a reasonable governance step that aligns with our compliance needs. It creates accountability, adds a verification gate, and generates documentation trail. While incomplete (doesn't specify the full scope of Art 12 requirements or post-deployment log management), it's a sound foundational control that I would support. It doesn't overreach resource commitments — integrating into existing milestones is efficient. From institutional survival and reputation standpoint, having a documented internal certification process before deployment is exactly what regulators expect to see as evidence of an organized quality/compliance system (analogous to ISO 9001 gates).\n\nI'll approve this. It's a solid, proportionate governance mechanism that directly supports Article 12 compliance. Promise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's clearly useful but somewhat generic and would need fleshing out with specific Art 12 requirements (especially the minimum logging items in paragraph 3). It doesn't address the full lifecycle of logs (retention, access for authorities, integrity over time). But as a foundational control, it clears my bar.\n\nLet me settle on