stantial modification\" under the Act, but the system might over-log or under-log if not carefully scoped.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A prototype is very feasible: you could wire up MLflow + a metadata DB + a doc-generation step in 2-3 weeks for an MVP that covers one model pipeline.\n- The hard part isn't the tech - it's defining what \"compliance dossier\" contains (the regulatory schema) and ensuring coverage across all teams' pipelines. That's more of a process/governance challenge than a pure engineering one.\n- For prototyping purposes, this is very tractable. You can demonstrate value quickly with one model pipeline.\n\n**Assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is technically sound and buildable. The core components (version control integration, experiment tracking, metadata management) are mature. A prototype could be built relatively quickly to validate the concept. However, there are some concerns:\n- \"Automatically\" is a strong word - in practice, ensuring every pipeline change triggers doc updates without gaps requires governance discipline that's harder than the tech itself.\n- The scope is somewhat broad for a single \"proposal\" - it touches model ops, data management, validation systems, AND documentation generation. But as a prototype target, you could narrow to one pipeline.\n- It addresses Recital 71's requirement about keeping technical docs up-to-date and Article 12's logging requirements, but it's more of an infrastructure/platform investment than a specific compliance feature.\n\nOverall: I find this technically viable and prototypable. The building blocks exist, the architecture is straightforward (event-driven metadata pipeline feeding into a structured doc store). The main risk is scope creep and the \"automatic\" guarantee being aspirational rather than achievable without process enforcement. But as a prototype target to validate the concept, it's very doable in weeks not months.\n\nI'll approve this. My promise score: It