cle 72). Article 12 requires that systems *technically allow* for automatic recording — it's about the capability to log, not about building an analytics layer on top. This toolkit is more of a downstream consumer of logs rather than addressing the core technical requirement of Article 12 itself.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The scope is extremely broad: \"a set of monitoring tools,\" \"analytics dashboard,\" real-time processing, anomaly detection, drift detection. This is not a focused prototype — it's an entire product suite being described in a paragraph.\n- No clear MVP or phased approach is articulated. Building all of this before validating any single component would be slow and risky.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what logging infrastructure it depends on. You can't build the analytics layer without first having the logging capability (which IS the actual Article 12 requirement). This creates a dependency that isn't addressed.\n- \"Detecting drifts or anomalies\" is a well-known hard problem in ML ops, but applying it to \"high-risk failures\" as defined by regulation adds a layer of domain-specific mapping that's underspecified.\n\n**Does it actually address Article 12?**\nArticle 12(1) says: \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is about BUILDING LOGGING INTO THE SYSTEM. The proposal here builds a monitoring/analytics layer ON TOP OF logs. It's adjacent but not directly addressing the core technical requirement. It assumes logs already exist and builds a consumption layer.\n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- The idea has some merit as a downstream capability, but it's over-scoped for a prototype commitment.\n- It doesn't clearly address the actual Article 12 requirement (building logging into the system architecture).\n- Technical viability of \"detecting drifts indicating high-risk failures\" in a general sense is questionable without specifying which AI sy