ulators and market participants.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level - it describes an architecture but doesn't specify how it handles the specific minimum requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (Article 12(3): reference database, input data matches, identification of natural persons in verification). A \"standardized\" approach might not adequately cover the specialized logging needs of biometric systems.\n- It says \"captures timestamps, input data, and system state changes by default\" - but Article 12(2) requires recording events relevant for specific purposes (risk situations, substantial modifications, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring). The proposal doesn't explicitly address what constitutes an \"event\" or how it maps to the regulatory categories.\n- It's a one-size-fits-all approach that might not fully satisfy the nuanced requirements across different high-risk system categories.\n- However, as a baseline architecture with project-specific extensions, it could work well.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is strongly aligned with my compliance mandate. Article 12 makes automatic logging a hard technical requirement (\"shall technically allow for\"). Having a unified, default-enabled architecture directly serves institutional survival (reducing regulatory risk), ensures consistent compliance across the portfolio, and protects market reputation by demonstrating organizational commitment to compliance infrastructure. The fact that it's integrated into base templates means new projects can't accidentally be non-compliant.\n\nThe main weakness is that it's described at a somewhat general level and may need project-specific extensions for Annex III 1(a) systems' specific minimum logging requirements. But as an architectural foundation, it's exactly the right kind of investment from a compliance perspective.\n\nI would approve this. It directly addresses a mandatory \"shall\" requirement in the regulation, reduces systemic