eet Article 12's requirements. A straightforward approach: structured event logging with the specific fields mandated (timestamps, reference database IDs, input data, verifier IDs). This is well-understood engineering with clear specifications.\n- Committing scarce implementation resources to a speculative research project about \"novel algorithms\" for correlating activation maps with logs would NOT help meet Article 12 compliance on any timeline. It's not even the right technical approach — you don't need neural activation maps to comply with Article 12.\n- The proposal doesn't address what Article 12 actually requires. It's solving a different (harder, more open-ended) problem and calling it regulatory compliance.\n\n**From my vantage point:**\n- Technical viability: Low for the specific claim made. Correlating logs with activation maps to \"reconstruct complex decision-making pathways\" is not a solved problem and has no clear engineering path. The regulation doesn't require this.\n- Speed of prototyping: Very poor. This is explicitly a research project with novel algorithms — the antithesis of speed and deterministic delivery.\n- Quality: You can't measure quality against a moving target in open research.\n- It does NOT address what Article 12 actually asks for technically. The regulation wants you to build logging that captures specific events. This proposal is about using those logs as input data for a separate interpretability research effort.\n\nThis is clearly a reject from my perspective. It's not technically viable within reasonable timelines, it doesn't solve the actual regulatory problem (which is straightforward engineering), and it commits resources to open-ended research when what's needed is deterministic implementation of logging infrastructure. The promise score should be low — maybe 10-15. There might be a small kernel of \"interesting to explore someday\" but as an implementation commitment against Article 12, it fails completely.\n",
