s / Concerns:**\n- The proposal says the \"compliance office will implement quarterly audits.\" This is a resource commitment. My function has limited staff. However, the proposal frames it as a protocol/mechanism, not necessarily requiring massive headcount — it's a structured review process.\n- \"Rigorous checklist derived from Recital 71\" — Recital 71 is a purpose statement, not an operative obligation list. The actual obligations are in Article 12 (and the technical documentation requirements in Annex IV). A checklist derived solely from Recital 71 might miss specific operational requirements in Articles 11, 12, and Annex IV. However, this is a minor drafting concern — in practice, any competent compliance officer would derive the checklist from the full regulatory text including Article 12's operative provisions and Annex IV. The proposal says \"derived from Recital 71\" but in context, it's reasonable to read this as using Recital 71 as the framing/structural guide for what categories of documentation exist.\n- Quarterly frequency: Is that sufficient? For rapidly evolving AI systems (frequent model updates), quarterly might leave gaps. But as an internal audit cadence (not a logging frequency), quarterly is defensible — the logs themselves must be continuous (Article 12(1)), and the audits are verification checkpoints. This seems reasonable.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention the technical logging requirements of Article 12(1)-(3) (automatic recording, specific fields for Annex III point 1(a) systems). It focuses more on documentation. However, \"event logs\" is mentioned in the title text, and validating that logs are being properly generated could be part of the audit checklist.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a squarely within-my-mandate proposal. It directly operationalizes Article 12's lifetime record-keeping obligation through structured internal verification. It addresses institutional survival (penalty avoidance), regulatory compliance (de