hat enable recording of events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring. A documentation generator doesn't inherently provide these logging capabilities - it needs the underlying system to actually capture those events first.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics/identification), Article 12(3) requires very specific data points: period of use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification. A documentation generator that parses Git and MLflow logs may not capture all of these at the operational level.\n- The proposal says \"transforms raw metadata into clear, comprehensive reports\" - but compliance requires more than just having a report; it requires the technical capability to log events automatically during system operation.\n- However, as a supplementary tool that ensures documentation completeness and currency, it does serve an important compliance function.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a useful tool for the documentation aspect of Article 12/Recital 71, but it's not sufficient on its own to meet the full requirements of Article 12, particularly the automatic logging capability requirement in paragraph 1 and the specific event recording capabilities in paragraphs 2-3. It addresses the \"technical documentation\" side well but under-addresses the \"automatic recording of events\" side.\n\nThat said, it's still a valuable compliance tool - having automated, up-to-date technical documentation that flags gaps is important for demonstrating compliance to regulators. The fact that it draws from existing sources (Git, MLflow) means it leverages what we already have rather than creating new parallel systems. This reduces implementation burden and increases the likelihood of actual adoption by researchers.\n\nFrom a market reputation standpoint, having robust automated documentation infrastructure signals regulatory seriousness to partners, customers, and regulators.\n\