lid point: building Article 12-compliant logging for every single research prototype is wasteful and technically unnecessary. We need SOME triage mechanism to know which systems need the full logging stack.\n\n**Technical viability of the specific mechanism proposed:**\n- A \"quarterly review board\" is not technically viable as a real-time gating mechanism. It's too slow, too coarse-grained, and creates a binary gate (high-risk vs not) when in practice the risk classification can shift during development.\n- From a prototyping perspective, this adds a serialization point: you must WAIT for the board before you can provision logging infrastructure. That's weeks or months of idle time.\n- A better technical approach would be to build modular, swappable logging layers that can be attached to any system post-hoc, rather than gating on upfront classification.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe underlying concern (don't waste engineering resources on unnecessary logging) is valid and important from my vantage point. But the proposed mechanism (quarterly board, pre-allocation gate) is technically problematic for prototyping speed and introduces a bureaucratic bottleneck that will slow down development cycles. It's not really a \"technical solution\" - it's an administrative process dressed up as engineering strategy. The technical viability of actually implementing Article 12 logging isn't addressed at all.\n\nI would lean toward reject because:\n1. It doesn't address the actual technical implementation challenge\n2. The quarterly cadence is antithetical to prototyping speed\n3. It gates technical work behind administrative approval, which is a bottleneck\n4. It's not technically \"viable\" as described - it's a governance structure, not an engineering solution\n\nBut I acknowledge the underlying resource-allocation concern has merit. Maybe a 25-35 promise score? The idea of triage is reasonable but the implementation proposed (quarterly board) would hurt prototyping velocity sig