nt, which would eat into our research budget.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is squarely in my domain. MLflow trackers are already in place. CI/CD pipelines already exist. The proposal repurposes existing data flows for regulatory purposes without requiring new systems or infrastructure purchases.\n- The integration point (CI/CD) means it doesn't create a parallel process that engineers have to remember separately - it's embedded in the workflow they're already following.\n\n**Concerns / weaknesses:**\n- The scope seems narrower than what Article 12 actually requires. Article 12(1) requires the system itself to \"technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime.\" This is about the AI SYSTEM's runtime logging, not just training/documentation metadata. MLflow captures training-time data, but Article 12 also requires operational/production logs (start/end times of each use, reference databases checked, input data matches, verification personnel).\n- The proposal says \"compliance reports\" and \"technical documentation\" which is more Annex IV territory. Article 12's logging requirements are about the system's runtime behavior.\n- However, as a Lab Director focused on resource efficiency, even a partial solution that handles the training/documentation metadata side reduces the compliance burden significantly and sets up architecture for the runtime logging pieces.\n\n**Decision assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is highly efficient use of existing infrastructure. It's low-cost (engineering time to script an integration with tools we already have), it removes recurring manual overhead, and it frees researcher capacity. Even though it doesn't fully cover Article 12's runtime logging requirements, it addresses a meaningful portion of the record-keeping burden using assets we already own. The resource-allocation logic is strong: convert sunk infrastructure investment into regulatory compliance capability at