ld. Annex III has many different categories (employment, credit scoring, law enforcement, etc.), and Article 12(3) specifies minimum logging requirements that vary by category. A truly universal SDK might need significant customization per use case.\n- However, the proposal says \"lightweight wrapper API\" — so it's not claiming to be a monolithic everything-solution, but rather a base layer. That's reasonable.\n- There's a risk of over-engineering: building an SDK for something that could potentially be handled by existing infrastructure or off-the-shelf logging tools. But the specific domain (AI system compliance logging with ML pipeline integration) is likely not well-served by generic logging solutions, so custom development has justification.\n- The \"without requiring custom code for every project\" claim is optimistic — some projects will need custom event definitions. But as a base layer reducing 80% of the work, it's still highly valuable.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\nThis is a high-leverage investment. One team builds the SDK once; all future and current high-risk projects benefit. It reduces per-project compliance overhead significantly. It protects research velocity (scientific ambition) by making compliance an infrastructure concern rather than a per-project tax. It leverages existing ML pipeline infrastructure.\n\nThe main risk is scope creep — \"universal\" could balloon into a massive engineering project that consumes more resources than it saves in the short term. But as framed (lightweight wrapper, core primitives like timestamps/hashes/versions), it's a bounded, high-ROI investment.\n\nFrom my vantage point, this is one of the most resource-efficient approaches to compliance I could imagine. It turns a per-project cost into an amortized infrastructure cost. This directly addresses my concerns about funding constraints and protecting scientific ambition while meeting regulatory obligations.\n\nI'd approve this with high confidence. The p