ut leveraging what we already have: experiment tracking tools, training scripts, dataset manifests. It's an integration layer, not a new system.\n- The \"documentation-as-code\" approach means the documentation lives in version control alongside the code, which is how we already manage our research artifacts. Low friction with existing workflows.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Template-driven systems can become rigid. If our research methods evolve (new training paradigms, new data types), the templates need updating. This is an ongoing maintenance cost.\n- \"Automatically generates\" — there's a risk that auto-generated documentation misses nuances or misrepresents intent, creating compliance liability rather than solving it. Someone still needs to review and validate the output. The proposal doesn't explicitly address human review gates.\n- It addresses Recital 71 (technical documentation) well but doesn't seem to fully cover Article 12(1)-(3) logging requirements (automatic event recording during system operation). That's a different technical challenge — runtime logging vs. development-time provenance documentation. So this proposal is necessary but not sufficient for full Article 12 compliance.\n- \"Accelerates the path to deployment\" — from my perspective, this is good because it means we don't have a separate \"compliance sprint\" before every deployment; the docs are already there as part of normal R&D workflow.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, cost-effective proposal that leverages existing infrastructure. It addresses a real recurring resource drain (manual compliance documentation). The build cost is moderate because it's an integration layer on top of tools we already use. The main risk is maintenance burden as research methods evolve and the need for human validation gates to ensure auto-generated docs are actually accurate and sufficient. But these are manageable risks, not deal-breakers.\n\nFrom my vantage point, this clears the bar.