tomatically captures system events\" but doesn't specify whether it covers all the specific requirements in Article 12(3) (period of each use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons involved in verification).\n- \"Immutable ledger\" - while good for integrity, I need to consider whether this actually supports the post-market monitoring and risk management requirements. Immutable records are good for audit trails but the proposal doesn't explicitly mention how it connects to Article 72 (post-market monitoring) or Article 79(1) (risk situations).\n- The scope says \"all high-risk projects\" which is good, but I need to ensure it covers Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements (biometric identification use cases have additional logging requirements under Art 12(3)).\n- \"Lightweight\" - I need to be careful that \"lightweight\" doesn't mean it misses some of the more granular data points required by Article 12(3).\n- The proposal mentions \"metadata in an immutable ledger\" but Article 12 requires logging of events, inputs, and potentially output-related data. Metadata alone might not be sufficient - we need actual event records, input data, etc.\n\n**Compliance Assessment:**\nThis is a strong foundational proposal that directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) - automatic recording of events over the lifetime of the system. It creates an institutional infrastructure that ensures compliance rather than relying on individual project teams to implement their own logging solutions. From a compliance standpoint, this is exactly what we want:\n- Standardization reduces risk of inconsistent implementation\n- Integration into existing pipelines means it's hard to bypass\n- Immutability supports audit and traceability\n- Reduces researcher burden = higher adoption = fewer compliance gaps\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated is somewhat incomplete - it doesn't explicitly map to all Article 12(2)(a)-(c) requirements or the sp