ng.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- This is genuinely useful for engineering: before deploying high-risk AI systems, you want to know your logging actually works under failure conditions.\n- It's essentially chaos engineering / fault injection testing applied to compliance-critical infrastructure.\n- The technical approach aligns well with how we'd validate any logging system in production - you need known-bad inputs and verify the output.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Specialized testing environment\" could be overkill. A test suite within our existing CI/CD pipeline might suffice for initial validation.\n- The proposal frames it as mitigating \"institutional survival risks\" which is dramatic but not technically grounded in a different way than normal QA testing.\n- It's somewhat redundant with standard software testing practices - we already need to test logging pipelines. The novelty here is just the regulatory framing of what to test.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, building an entirely new \"suite\" when you could integrate a few test cases into existing infrastructure adds unnecessary overhead.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe underlying engineering task (verify logging captures risk events) is technically sound and necessary. However, framing it as a standalone \"Simulation Suite\" suggests a larger commitment than needed for the initial prototyping phase. A focused set of integration tests within our existing pipeline would achieve 80% of the value at 20% of the cost. That said, if the lab genuinely needs to demonstrate to regulators that logging works under failure conditions, having dedicated synthetic scenarios is reasonable.\n\nI think this clears a moderate bar. The technical approach is viable and the prototyping path is clear (build test fixtures → inject into pipeline → verify log output). It's not revolutionary but it's necessary engineering work. I'd give it a moderate promise score - it's useful but potentially over-scoped as stated, and much of it 