r Annex III point 1(a) systems: the input data for which a search led to a match, and identification of natural persons involved in verification (Article 14(5)). The proposal's description seems to focus on generic \"timestamps and reference data\" — it may not fully capture all the specific elements required, particularly the human verification identifiers.\n- The phrase \"providing researchers with rich datasets for analyzing model behavior in the wild\" is a double-edged sword from a compliance perspective. While useful, making sensitive logging data (which may include personal data under Annex III 1(a) systems like biometric identification) available to researchers raises GDPR/data protection concerns and could create secondary use risks. The logs must be protected appropriately.\n- \"Middleware API that intercepts system inputs and outputs\" — this is a specific architectural choice. From a compliance standpoint, I need to ensure it captures ALL relevant events, not just inputs/outputs. Article 12(2)(a) requires logging situations that \"may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk\" or \"substantial modification.\" This might include internal state changes, model version switches, drift detection alerts — not just I/O interception.\n- The proposal says it removes \"administrative friction\" which is good for compliance culture, but I need to ensure the automated capture doesn't become so opaque that we lose human understanding of what's being logged and why. Compliance requires comprehensibility (Recital 71).\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" — the proposal doesn't explicitly address retention periods or lifecycle management. What happens when a system is deprecated? Logs must be retained per the regulation.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires. A centralized, automated logging infrastructure is exactly the kind of technical capability the regulation demands. The fact that it's unified across a