ated state, that directly reduces compliance risk and could save significant legal/compliance review costs later.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The proposal has some aspirational language: \"transforms passive record-keeping into a proactive safety mechanism,\" \"preserves our market reputation.\" This sounds more like a marketing/safety pitch than an R&D efficiency play.\n- However, the core engineering task (compare live metrics to baseline, alert on threshold breach) is well-understood, not scientifically risky. It's more of an ops/infrastructure build than an R&D bet. The scientific ambition risk is low - this isn't a research project with uncertain outcomes.\n- The resource commitment is moderate but real: you need engineers, compute for real-time analysis, and ongoing maintenance. For a lab that also needs to do actual AI research, this diverts engineering headcount from core R&D work.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- If we already have logging in place (which Article 12 mandates), the marginal cost of adding an alerting layer is lower than building everything from scratch. The proposal implicitly assumes the logging exists (\"analyzes logs in real-time\").\n- Real-time analysis requires some compute resources - streaming infrastructure, potentially dedicated nodes. This competes with training/inference workloads for GPU/CPU cycles.\n- The \"predefined thresholds\" approach is practical and doesn't require ML to detect drift (which would be much more expensive). Simple statistical monitoring against baselines is doable with existing tooling (Prometheus, Grafana-like stacks, or custom scripts).\n\n**Key considerations from my vantage:**\n- This is NOT purely a compliance requirement. Article 12 requires logs; it doesn't require real-time alerting on those logs. Post-market monitoring under Article 72 could be satisfied by periodic review of logs rather than continuous automated alerts.\n- However, 