ry to enable the R&D systems to be compliant.\n- The proposal is relatively straightforward and low-risk in terms of scientific uncertainty.\n\nOverall: This is a reasonable, necessary infrastructure investment that directly enables compliance with Article 12(1). The tiered storage approach shows cost consciousness. The audit-first framing is prudent. It's not glamorous but it's essential groundwork. From my resource-allocation perspective, this is the kind of thing you do proactively rather than reactively (which would be more expensive in emergencies). I'd approve this - it's practical, bounded in scope, and directly tied to a regulatory requirement that has no ambiguity about whether we need to do it.\n\nPromise score: It's necessary but not particularly \"promising\" in an innovative sense - it's a plumbing task done well. But from my vantage point of resource efficiency and avoiding emergency spending, it scores quite well. I'd say around 72-78. It's clearly needed, the approach is sound (audit then tiered upgrade), but it's not transformative or high-upside. It's solid operational hygiene.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 74.\n",
