- But the proposal frames it as \"quarterly review process\" which implies ongoing overhead rather than a one-time pre-deployment gate. From a prototyping speed perspective, adding recurring audit cycles could slow iteration if teams have to pause for traceability validation.\n- The \"separate internal team\" requirement adds coordination overhead and potential bottlenecks in the development pipeline.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. \"Trace specific model decisions back to training data\" — this is technically dubious for many AI architectures. It conflates log completeness with model interpretability. A good logging system records inputs, outputs, timestamps, model version, feature flags, etc., but it doesn't and can't always reconstruct the causal path through a neural network's weights back to specific training examples.\n2. The quarterly cadence is arbitrary and adds recurring cost without clear incremental value over a one-time pre-deployment validation plus continuous log quality checks.\n3. It's a process proposal more than an engineering solution — it doesn't specify what logging infrastructure to build, what format, what retention policy, etc. It's meta-level (\"we will check if our logs are good\") rather than prescriptive (\"here is how we build the logging layer\").\n4. The \"proactive auditing prevents costly retrospective changes\" argument has merit but could be achieved more cheaply with pre-deployment log schema validation and CI/CD checks on log completeness, rather than a full quarterly reconstruction exercise.\n\n**Verdict:**\nThe underlying concern (verify your logs can support traceability before deployment) is valid and aligned with Article 12's requirements. However, the specific implementation proposed is technically problematic (tracing to training data for opaque models), adds recurring process overhead that could slow prototyping iterations, and doesn't specify actual engineering deliverables. It's more of a QA/process sugg