        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is a \"Unified Logging Framework\" - a standardized middleware layer for automatic event recording across all high-risk AI projects. Let me assess it:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A unified logging middleware is technically feasible. We've seen similar patterns in other domains (APM tools, audit logging frameworks). Building a universal API that captures system events, timestamps, and input matches into an immutable encrypted database is straightforward engineering.\n- However, \"universal\" is the key risk here. Different high-risk AI systems will have vastly different architectures - some may be real-time inference pipelines, others batch processing, some multi-modal, etc. A single middleware layer that handles ALL of these without significant per-project adaptation could become a bottleneck or require heavy abstraction that slows down prototyping.\n- The \"immutable, encrypted database\" requirement is technically sound from a compliance perspective (Article 12 requires recording over the lifetime), but adds operational complexity. Immutability means no corrections if logging malfunctions; encryption adds latency and key management overhead.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- PRO: Eliminates per-project logging boilerplate. If done well, a new high-risk project could start with logging \"batteries included.\" This is a clear speed win for prototyping.\n- CON: Building this framework itself is a significant upfront investment before any specific project benefits. The lab commits scarce implementation resources to build infrastructure before we know the full shape of what all our high-risk systems will look like.\n- CON: \"Universal\" APIs tend to become bloated or too abstract. When prototyping a new system, if the logging framework doesn't quite fit the event patterns you