ght per-project loggers first.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe idea has merit - we DO need automatic event recording across all projects, and some standardization reduces duplication. But the \"universal middleware\" framing is risky from a prototyping speed perspective. It's a big upfront investment with uncertain generalizability. The technical approach (immutable encrypted DB) adds complexity that may slow iteration during development. For my domain (speed/quality of prototyping), this is a moderate-promise idea - it would help once built, but the build itself consumes resources and the \"universal\" design risks being too rigid for diverse system architectures.\n\nI'd say this clears a low bar - there's genuine value in shared logging infrastructure, and the technical approach is sound. But it's not something I'd back \"without hesitation\" because of the prototyping speed risk during development and the generalization challenge. I'll approve it with a moderate promise score - maybe 58-62 range. It's technically viable and addresses a real need, but the execution model (build universal first) is somewhat at odds with agile prototyping principles.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"eliminating the need to reinvent logging for every new project.\" If we're building multiple high-risk systems, this IS valuable. But the question is whether we should commit scarce implementation resources NOW to build a universal framework vs. letting each prototype develop its own needs first. From a pure prototyping speed standpoint, if I have 3 projects starting simultaneously and none of them can start logging until the middleware is done (say 2-3 months), that's a significant delay. But if we're already past the initial ideation phase and heading into implementation, having this ready does save time per project.\n\nI'll approve it with a promise around 58. It's technically sound and addresses a real compliance need, but the \"universal\" scope and upfront investment model create prototyping speed risks that prevent me from scoring higher.\n",
