ailable and up to date.\n\n**Speed of Prototyping:**\n- MLflow integration: quick to prototype, APIs are well-documented. A proof-of-concept pulling model cards from MLflow into a structured document could be done in days to a couple weeks.\n- Template generator: straightforward engineering, maybe 1-2 sprints to get a v0 working for the main fields.\n- The hard part is completeness and accuracy of the regulatory mapping - ensuring you capture everything Annex IV requires and that it's \"in a clear and comprehensive form.\" This isn't just a technical problem; it requires domain knowledge of the regulation.\n- Risk: scope creep in making it truly \"regulatory-ready\" vs. \"mostly-there-and-a-human-finishes-it.\"\n\n**Quality concerns:**\n- The proposal frames this as reducing manual burden on scientists, which is good, but the critical question is whether automated extraction produces documents that actually satisfy the regulatory requirements or just produce documents that look like they do.\n- \"Designed specifically for EU AI Act reporting requirements\" - who designs this? The template needs to be validated against Annex IV and any implementing acts. This is a regulatory interpretation task as much as an engineering one.\n- For Article 12 specifically (record-keeping/logging), this proposal addresses the documentation side but the actual runtime logging infrastructure (automatic recording of events over lifetime) would need separate work. However, Recital 71 does emphasize \"availability of technical documentation\" and keeping it up to date, which is what this addresses.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a prototyping speed perspective, this is quite feasible. MLflow integration is low-risk engineering. The template generation is straightforward. The main risk is the gap between \"we can extract data from our trackers\" and \"this document actually satisfies the regulator.\" But as a prototype, it's very achievable quickly - maybe 2-4 weeks to a working v0 that demons