ecification to fully satisfy all sub-requirements (especially Art. 12(3) for biometric systems), the architectural approach is correct and the right direction. From a compliance standpoint, having ONE standardized system rather than N different ad-hoc implementations dramatically reduces audit risk and regulatory exposure.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's not perfect — I'd want to see it expanded to explicitly address Art. 12(2) and (3) requirements, the retention period, immutability, etc. But as a foundational architecture that is mandated across all high-risk projects, it's exactly the kind of thing that protects institutional survival and demonstrates serious compliance posture to regulators.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's strong directionally but somewhat underspecified on the specific sub-requirements. The fact that it only claims \"baseline technical compliance with Article 12(1)\" while Art. 12 has more requirements is a slight concern, but as an architectural foundation to be built upon, it's very promising.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 80.\n",
