on't build systems that fail regulatory requirements. It's a risk-mitigation tool that allows the lab to pursue ambitious projects with more confidence, knowing compliance issues are caught early.\n4. **Practical and bounded scope**: The proposal is well-defined - it's about writing test probes against known Article 12 requirements (automatic logging, event recording, specific fields for Annex III point 1(a) systems). This isn't an open-ended research project; it has a clear deliverable and endpoint.\n5. **Scalability across the portfolio**: Once built, these tests can be applied to multiple AI systems in our pipeline, amortizing the development cost across projects.\n\nArguments against or concerns:\n1. The scope might creep - \"specific system events\" could expand to cover many event types across different system architectures.\n2. It's a compliance tool, not a scientific advancement - but that's fine for my role since I'm managing resources and infrastructure.\n3. There's some dependency on the logging implementation being in place first - you can't test what doesn't exist yet. But this is manageable as long as it's scheduled alongside (or just after) the logging feature development.\n\nOverall: This is a high-leverage, low-cost proposal from my perspective. It directly addresses resource waste (late-stage redesigns), leverages existing test infrastructure, and has bounded scope that won't consume disproportionate funding. The ROI in terms of preventing audit failures and costly rework is clear and quantifiable. I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my domain concerns. It's efficient, uses existing infrastructure, prevents resource waste, and has a clear bounded scope. I'd give it around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because there are some execution risks (scope creep on \"specific events,\" dependency on logging being implemented first), but the fundamental concept is highly valuable from a resource management standpoint.\n\nLet me sett