nce culture, which is favorable in any regulator interaction\n- Market reputation: showing that we go beyond minimum compliance and actually verify our systems can be audited strengthens our position\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Resource intensity: monthly cross-team audits are labor-intensive for R&D teams who should be building models\n- \"Full audit using only the generated logs\" might set an unrealistically high bar - some aspects of system behavior may inherently require context beyond logs (though Article 12 is specifically about what logs must capture)\n- The proposal doesn't specify who owns this protocol or how it interfaces with existing QA processes\n- Risk of creating a parallel compliance bureaucracy that slows R&D velocity\n- \"Blind reconstruction\" might be overly aggressive - if one team can't reconstruct another's model, that could create inter-team tension and blame-shifting rather than constructive improvement\n- The scope is clear (Article 12) but the implementation detail is thin\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nFrom a pure compliance standpoint, this is highly valuable. Article 12 requires specific logging capabilities. Recital 71 emphasizes that records must be sufficient for traceability, compliance verification, and monitoring. A systematic internal test to verify that logs actually achieve these purposes is exactly the kind of proactive compliance mechanism that demonstrates institutional commitment. It also creates an audit trail of our own compliance efforts (meta-compliance documentation), which regulators would view favorably.\n\nThe \"check-the-box\" concern is real - many organizations implement logging mechanically without verifying it's actually sufficient for traceability and monitoring purposes. This protocol directly addresses that gap.\n\nHowever, the monthly cadence might be too aggressive initially. But the proposal as stated is sound in principle. The resource cost is a consideration but not a disqualifier from a compliance perspectiv