rapper with decorators.\"\n2. Scope ambiguity: The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" but Article 12(3) for biometric systems (Annex III point 1(a)) requires specific things like reference databases, matched input data, identification of natural persons in verification. A generic SDK might not capture all the domain-specific fields needed without per-project configuration. The proposal doesn't address configurability for different Annex III categories.\n3. \"Middleware layer between the model and system logs\" is vague architecturally. In practice, logging in ML pipelines is complex — you need hooks at data ingestion, preprocessing, inference, post-processing stages. A decorator-based approach might miss a lot of events unless very carefully designed.\n4. Maintenance burden: The SDK itself becomes infrastructure that needs updating as frameworks change (PyTorch versions, new JAX features, etc.). Who owns this? It could become a single point of failure or a bottleneck if the team maintaining it is small.\n5. Cost vs. benefit: If we have, say, 3-4 active AI projects, is building an SDK worth it vs. just having each project implement logging with shared templates/checklists? The break-even point matters. But for a lab that's going to keep developing AI systems (which the prompt implies), this likely makes sense over time.\n\n**Net assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point on resource-allocation efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure, this is a strong proposal. It's a one-time investment that amortizes across all current and future projects. It directly addresses compliance (Article 12) while freeing researcher time for actual R&D. The main risk is underestimating the engineering effort to make it truly work across diverse ML stacks, but the strategic logic is sound.\n\nI'd approve this. The promise score should reflect high confidence in its resource-efficiency value, tempered slightly by the engineering complexity and scope ambiguity. I'd put it around 78-82. It's clearly aligned with my mandate (efficiency, infrastructure reuse, reducing per-project overhead) but I'm not giving a 90+ because the proposal is somewhat hand-wavy on implementation specifics and doesn't address how it handles the domain-specific logging requirements for different Annex III categories without becoming another fragmented solution.\n\nLet me settle on 80. Strong approval from an efficiency standpoint, with minor reservations about scope definition and long-term maintenance ownership.\n",
