ounding in novel domains, but the core methodological contribution seems incremental—applying known anomaly detection techniques to a new data stream.\n\n**Intellectual Merit:**\n- The proposal is essentially \"build anomaly detectors for compliance logs.\" This is a solid engineering task but not a high intellectual merit research project. It doesn't propose to develop new algorithms, test hypotheses about AI system behavior, or contribute generalizable scientific knowledge.\n- The framing as \"turns a regulatory burden into a competitive advantage\" is commercial positioning language, not a statement of research contribution.\n- There's no mention of what new scientific questions will be addressed, what hypotheses will be tested, or what the expected contribution to the broader AI monitoring/drift literature would be.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- If done well, results could feed into the broader literature on AI system monitoring and drift detection. But as framed, this is a narrow application project with limited generalizability.\n- The specific context (EU regulatory logs for high-risk AI systems) is a novel domain for anomaly detection, which gives it some marginal scientific interest. But the proposal doesn't articulate how findings would generalize beyond this compliance context.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis reads more like an applied engineering/product development project with a research veneer than a genuine knowledge-frontier pursuit. The intellectual content is \"apply known anomaly detection to structured operational logs.\" While there are some interesting sub-questions (defining algorithmic thresholds for \"substantial modification,\" detecting subtle performance degradation vs. noise), the proposal as written doesn't elevate these to a research agenda. It's more of an implementation task than a discovery program.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: there IS legitimate scientific interest in how to operationalize drift detection specifically for AI systems (as opposed to classical ML models), and the regulatory context creates new data types not well-studied in literature. But the proposal doesn't lean into that—it leans into \"build tools\" and \"competitive advantage.\"\n\nMy decision: This is borderline. From a pure research merit standpoint, it's more engineering than science. It doesn't clearly push a knowledge frontier or articulate a novel scientific contribution. I would reject it from my vantage point because it reads as an applied product development task dressed in regulatory language, rather than a genuine research program with intellectual ambition and broader scientific impact.\n\nPromise score: I'd put this around 35-40. There's some legitimate scientific interest (drift detection for AI systems is not trivial), but the proposal as framed doesn't justify it as a knowledge-frontier research investment. It's more of an engineering deliverable. Let me say 38.\n\nDecision: reject.\n",
