duces compliance risk significantly.\n\n5. \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - a unified framework that persists across projects ensures logs are maintained for the full lifecycle, not just until a project ends or a team disbands.\n\n6. Market reputation: Demonstrating to clients and regulators that you have a systematic, organization-wide approach to compliance logging (rather than ad-hoc per-project solutions) signals maturity and reduces regulatory scrutiny risk.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n1. \"Plug-and-play\" might be too simplistic - different high-risk systems may need different types of event capture. But the proposal says it's for \"eligible R&D projects\" which suggests scope-limited application.\n2. The framework needs to be maintained and updated as regulations evolve (Recital 71 mentions keeping documentation up to date). A unified framework makes this easier, though.\n3. Risk that researchers bypass or misuse the API - but a dedicated task force integrating into existing deployment pipelines mitigates this.\n4. \"Small engineering task force\" - is it sufficient? From a compliance perspective, under-resourcing could lead to gaps. But the proposal is about building the framework, not necessarily about ongoing operation.\n\n**Compliance Officer assessment:**\nThis is strongly aligned with what I need. The biggest compliance risk under Article 12 is that individual research teams build inconsistent, incomplete, or missing logging capabilities. A unified framework:\n- Ensures baseline compliance (all required fields are captured)\n- Makes audits tractable (one system to review)\n- Scales as we develop more high-risk systems\n- Reduces the chance of a specific project shipping without proper logging\n- Creates institutional knowledge and continuity\n\nThis is not just \"nice to have\" - it's close to a compliance necessity for an institution developing multiple high-risk AI systems. The alternative (each team building their own) creates significant regulatory exposure.\n\nThe promise score: This is highly aligned with my function. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12 in a scalable, auditable way. I'd rate this very high - maybe 85-90. It's not 100 because \"plug-and-play\" might oversimplify the complexity of different logging needs across system types, and a \"small task force\" raises questions about whether it can cover all edge cases in Annex III categories. But from my vantage point, this is one of the most important infrastructure investments I'd want to make.\n\nDecision: Approve. This directly serves institutional survival (regulatory compliance), protects market reputation (demonstrates systematic approach), and is a foundational element for meeting Article 12 obligations across all high-risk systems.\n",
