t I'll give a moderate promise score because the \"real-time\" framing and the conflation of code changes with capability disclosures introduce risks to prototyping velocity that need careful engineering.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to approve or reject. The bar is: does this clear my professional threshold for committing scarce implementation resources? \n\n- Is it technically viable? Yes, partially. Auto-generating architecture docs, data schemas, test summaries from code is very doable. Generating \"capability disclosures\" in a regulatory sense is harder and may require human-in-the-loop steps.\n- Does it help speed/quality of prototyping? Potentially yes (less manual doc writing) but potentially no (if it adds mandatory pipeline steps). The proposal doesn't clearly resolve this tension.\n\nI think I'll approve with a moderate-to-low promise score because the core technical approach is sound and commonly done, even if the full scope as described has gaps. The \"sync code changes\" part is very buildable; the \"capability disclosures in real-time\" part is where it gets murkier but still achievable with appropriate scoping (e.g., auto-generate structural docs on commit, regenerate capability assessments on a schedule or after evaluation runs).\n\nLet me settle: approve, promise around 55-60. It's a reasonable engineering task that could genuinely help compliance without being technically infeasible, but the proposal underspecifies key design decisions and the \"real-time\" framing risks adding prototyping friction if not carefully scoped.\n",
