        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1800\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises Recital 71's mandate for technical documentation in 'a clear and comprehensive form' that is kept 'up to date … throughout the lifetime of the AI system.' A version-controlled wiki hard-linked to model repositories gives us an auditable, timestamped trail of what was documented at each stage—precisely the traceability and audit-readiness infrastructure I need to demonstrate compliance if a market-surveillance authority requests our files—and the researcher-compliance pairing closes the gap that currently leaves me unable to verify whether documentation actually captures capabilities, limitations, and risk-management logic as Article 12's logging events will require context. The main residual risk is governance: 'living templates' without a defined change-approval workflow and update-trigger protocol could drift into staleness or inconsistency across teams, which would itself become a compliance finding.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 83\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P1800 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze proposal P1800:\n\n\"Interdisciplinary Technical Documentation Standards Initiative. A collaborative effort to create living templates that translate abstract mathematical descriptions of our models into the clear, functional characteristics required by Recital 71. This bridges the gap between theory and regulation. Researchers will work with compliance experts to develop a standardized lexicon for documenting capabilities and limitations. We will implement this via a version-controlled wiki linked directly to our model repositories. It prevents the loss of intellectual nuance when translating frontier research into the simplified forms required by regulators.\"\n\nLet me assess this from my Compliance Officer vantage point:\n\n**Relevance to Article 12 / Recital 71:**\n- Recital 71 explicitly requires technical documentation \"containing information which is necessary to assess the compliance of the AI system with the relevant requirements and facilitate post market monitoring\" in a \"clear and comprehensive form.\"\n- It mentions documenting \"the general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\"\n- The technical documentation should be kept \"up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\"\n\n**Strengths from compliance perspective:**\n1. Directly addresses Recital 71's requirement for clear, comprehensive technical documentation — this is exactly what Article 12 and the surrounding articles demand.\n2. \"Living templates\" and \"version-controlled wiki linked directly to our model repositories\" — this supports the requirement that documentation be kept up-to-date throughout the system's lifetime. Version control provides traceability of changes to documentation, which aligns with Article 12(1)'s emphasis on traceability.\n3. Bridging researchers and compliance is practical: if compliance officers can't understand what the models do, they can't verify compliance. This directly serves my function.\n4. A \"standardized lexicon for documenting capabilities and limitations\" addresses Recital 71's explicit mention of documenting capabilities and limitations in clear form.\n5. The link to model repositories means documentation stays synchronized with actual system state — critical for the \"up-to-date throughout lifetime\" requirement.\n\n**Weaknesses / concerns from compliance perspective:**\n1. This is about technical documentation broadly (Recital 71, Article 11/Annex IV territory), while my specific article of concern is Article 12 (record-keeping/logging). However, Recital 71 is provided as context and the proposal does reference it explicitly. The logging requirements in Article 12 are more about automatic event recording, whereas this proposal is about documentation templates. But they're complementary — you need both logs AND technical documentation to be compliant.\n2. \"Living templates\" could be under-specified. What governance structure ensures these templates actually meet regulatory requirements? Who signs off? This is a process risk if not well-managed.\n3. The proposal says \"prevents the loss of intellectual nuance when translating frontier research into the simplified forms required by regulators.\" From my perspective, I don't need the full nuance — I need compliance with the regulation's requirements. Over-documenting or under-documenting are both risks, but the framing here is slightly misaligned: it's about preserving researcher intent rather than ensuring regulatory completeness. However, this is a minor concern.\n4. It's an initiative/process improvement rather than a concrete deliverable. \"Collaborative effort\" and \"living templates\" are somewhat vague on timeline, scope boundaries, and accountability. But for a screening exercise, the direction is sound.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- Non-compliance with Article 12 (and related documentation requirements) could lead to enforcement action, fines, or withdrawal of systems from the market. Having a structured approach to technical documentation reduces this risk significantly.\n- A version-controlled system linked to repositories creates an audit trail — if regulators come knocking, we can show exactly what was documented and when. This is critical for demonstrating compliance.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- Being known as a lab that takes documentation seriously (rather than bolting it on after the fact) enhances credibility with regulators, partners, and customers.\n- Standardized approaches across the institution signal maturity and reduce the risk of one team's sloppy documentation becoming an institutional liability.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis proposal directly supports compliance obligations under Recital 71 and by extension Article 12 (the documentation is needed to contextualize and interpret the logs). The version-controlled wiki linked to repositories is a practical, implementable mechanism that addresses the \"kept up to date throughout lifetime\" requirement. The interdisciplinary approach (researchers + compliance) is exactly what's needed because without it, we'll either have documentation too technical for regulators or documentation that misses critical system characteristics.\n\nThe main risk is vagueness — but as a screening exercise, the direction and mechanism are sound and directly serve my mandate. I would approve this. It's not about logging per se (Article 12(1)-(3)), but it's a necessary complement: you need technical documentation that makes sense of the logs, and Recital 71 ties them together explicitly.\n\nPromise score: This is strongly aligned with my function. The version-control + repository link is particularly compelling for audit readiness. I'd say around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because it's somewhat process-heavy and the \"living templates\" concept needs more structure to be truly compliance-grade (who owns updates, what triggers re-documentation, how do you handle version conflicts). But it's clearly worth pursuing.\n\nI'll go with 83.\n",
