ust be complete and accurate. If the scraping misses something critical (e.g., a dataset change that wasn't properly versioned), we'd have an incomplete record\n- \"Automatically generate\" - regulators expect the provider to understand and attest to what's in the documentation. Auto-generated docs without human review could be seen as insufficient due diligence\n- The proposal focuses on technical documentation generation but Article 12(1) specifically requires the system to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" - this is about the AI system itself having logging capabilities, not just generating documents from existing logs. The proposal seems to conflate these two things somewhat\n- However, the proposal does address the documentation aspect well\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\nThis is a strong operational tool that directly supports our obligations under Article 12 and Recital 71. It reduces the risk of non-compliance due to human error or oversight in maintaining technical documentation. The integration with existing infrastructure (MLflow, Git) means we're not building from scratch. The template approach ensures consistency across different AI systems in our portfolio.\n\nThe key compliance concern is that \"auto-generated\" documentation still needs human review and attestation. But as a pipeline/tool, it's a significant enabler for compliance. It doesn't replace the need for a compliance process but makes that process far more reliable and auditable.\n\nFor institutional survival: if we get a regulatory inquiry or audit, having a system that can produce consistent, complete, versioned technical documentation on demand is critical. Manual processes are error-prone and don't scale.\n\nFor market reputation: demonstrating robust internal compliance infrastructure (automated provenance tracking) is a positive signal to partners, customers, and regulators that we take the AI Act seriously.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's