al says it covers \"all high-risk AI models\" — but Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.) that go beyond general logging. A unified framework must be parameterizable to handle those additional fields (reference database, input data matches, natural person identification under Art 14(5)). The proposal as stated doesn't explicitly mention these specific fields. However, \"metadata tagging\" could encompass this if properly configured.\n- \"Core engineering team will develop a middleware layer that automatically intercepts critical system calls\" — the word \"critical\" is vague. Compliance requires logging of events relevant for risk identification (Art 12(2)(a)), post-market monitoring (Art 12(2)(b)), and operation monitoring (Art 12(2)(c)). The framework must be configurable to capture all these categories, not just \"critical system calls\" as the engineering team defines them.\n- Resource commitment: this is a significant development effort for the core engineering team. But from my perspective, the alternative — each project building its own logging — creates far greater compliance risk and audit complexity.\n\n**Compliance-specific assessment:**\n- This directly addresses Article 12(1) (automatic recording over lifetime).\n- Supports Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c) if properly configured for the relevant event types.\n- The \"immutable\" aspect supports the requirement that logs be reliable evidence in regulatory proceedings.\n- Standardization reduces the risk of inconsistent implementation across projects, which is a major compliance risk at an R&D institution with multiple high-risk systems.\n- For audits: having one unified system means one set of procedures to explain to auditors, rather than N different implementations. This significantly reduces audit burden and the risk of findings.\n\n**Risk if rejected:** Without this, each project team would likely implement logging differently (or