ld initially slow prototyping because teams must adopt and integrate with it before they can ship anything. There's an upfront cost: building the library, documenting it, getting buy-in across teams.\n- However, once built, it accelerates compliance for every new model — you don't reinvent logging each time. For speed of prototyping specifically, this is a mixed bag: early on it adds friction, long-term it removes repetitive work.\n- The quality benefit is significant: standardized logging means consistent data formats, which makes debugging and post-market monitoring much easier across the portfolio.\n- Risk: if the framework becomes a bottleneck (e.g., every team must go through one central team for integration support), it could slow prototyping. But as a library with a clean API, teams can self-serve.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Across all high-risk AI models currently in development\" — scope is broad. Different model architectures will need different integration points. The framework needs to be sufficiently abstract without being over-engineered.\n- The proposal focuses heavily on Annex III 1(a) (biometric identification) logging requirements (input data matches, verification IDs). A truly \"unified\" framework should also accommodate Article 12(3)(a)-(b) for other high-risk categories and the general Article 12(2) requirements. But maybe this is just one component of a broader effort.\n- Performance overhead: adding logging to every execution could add latency, especially in real-time biometric systems where response time matters. Need to ensure async/non-blocking logging.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is technically very viable — it's standard infrastructure engineering, not research. The pattern is well-understood. From a prototyping speed perspective, there's an initial investment cost but long-term payoff. The main risk is over-engineering or under-scoping the abstraction layer to handle diverse model architectures.\n\nI'd say this cle