rove their logs work. But this is more of an engineering/QA concern than a \"peer review\" concern. The technical documentation requirement (Article 11/17 and Annex IV) already covers documenting the system's capabilities, limitations, algorithms, etc. The \"intellectual merit\" framing seems like it conflates regulatory compliance with scientific validation.\n\n**Resource efficiency concerns:**\n- Adding a peer-review gate specifically for log reconstruction is process overhead. Logs either capture the right events or they don't — this is verifiable through systematic testing, not necessarily through adversarial red-teaming in the sense implied here.\n- \"Red-teaming sessions focused on traceability of decision paths\" sounds like it could become a recurring time sink. Red-teaming is valuable for safety/robustness, but applying it specifically to whether logs capture events seems misapplied — you're not trying to find adversarial failure modes in the logging system, you're verifying completeness.\n- The \"genuine intellectual merit\" language suggests this proposal might be over-scoped: treating a compliance/logging requirement as if it requires the same rigor as model validation. This could inflate effort allocation on a relatively narrow technical task at the expense of more impactful R&D work.\n\n**However, there are some merits:**\n- Verifying that logs actually capture what's required before deployment is sensible — better to catch gaps now than during a regulatory audit or incident investigation.\n- Some form of validation that logging works as specified is good engineering practice.\n- The \"periodic\" aspect acknowledges that systems evolve and logging needs re-validation after updates.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea (validate that your logs meet the Article 12 requirements before and during deployment) has merit, but the proposed mechanism (peer-review requirement + periodic red-teaming sessions framed around \"intellectual merit\") is overwrought fo