ng quality). The implementation is low-complexity (Cookiecutter templates are easy to build), the value is clear (reduces time-to-first-prototype for high-risk work, reduces risk of forgetting compliance infrastructure), and the risks are manageable. The main technical risk is that it might become stale or too rigid, but those are maintenance concerns rather than viability blockers.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's a practical, implementable engineering solution that directly addresses prototyping speed while embedding compliance by default. I'd approve it with a solid promise score — maybe 72-78 range. It's genuinely useful and technically feasible, but it's not a breakthrough; it's good engineering hygiene. The \"modular templates\" part adds some complexity that might slow initial delivery, and the long-term maintenance of keeping the scaffold aligned with evolving regulatory guidance is non-trivial.\n\nLet me settle on 74. It's clearly valuable and technically viable, directly in my wheelhouse, but it's a \"nice to have\" infrastructure investment rather than something transformative. The scope is reasonable and the implementation path is clear.\n",
