nce database queries, and session durations\" but doesn't explicitly mention: (a) identifying situations that may result in risk within meaning of Article 79(1) or substantial modification (Art 12(2)(a)), (b) facilitating post-market monitoring per Art 72 (Art 12(2)(b)), (c) monitoring operation per Art 26(5) (Art 12(2)(c)). However, these are more about the *purpose* of logs rather than specific data fields.\n- It doesn't explicitly mention \"identification of natural persons involved in verification\" (Art 12(3)(d)) - though \"input matches\" might partially cover this.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is addressed by \"every active model\" and \"session durations.\"\n- The proposal says \"eliminates the risk of manual errors\" - this is a strong claim but reasonable for an automated framework.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:** Article 12 is a mandatory technical requirement. Non-compliance could lead to enforcement action, fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, though Art 12 violations would fall under the general penalty regime). Having a unified framework reduces the risk of any single project being non-compliant.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:** A standardized compliance framework signals to regulators and clients that the institution takes traceability seriously. It also makes it easier to demonstrate compliance during audits or market surveillance activities.\n\n**Resource commitment concern:** This is a significant engineering investment - developing a modular API integrated into all high-risk projects. However, from my vantage point, this is exactly the kind of investment that prevents existential regulatory risk. The cost of non-compliance (fines, inability to place systems on EU market, reputational damage) far exceeds the implementation cost.\n\n**Decision:** This clearly addresses a core mandatory requirement of the AI Act in a structured, portfolio-wide way. It's well-scoped, directly maps to