        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concerns: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2613 is about \"Regulatory Overhead Budget Allocation\" — specifically:\n- Formal reallocation of funding and compute resources for continuous logging performance costs\n- Ensures traceability doesn't degrade system latency/throughput\n- Mandates a 5% resource buffer in all project grants for compliance-related telemetry and verification\n- Prevents \"compliance creep\" from eating into core R&D milestones\n- Maintains scientific ambition by funding operational costs of transparency\n\nLet me assess this from my Compliance Officer perspective:\n\n**Relevance to Article 12:** The proposal directly addresses the operational implementation challenge of Article 12 — specifically that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" Continuous logging has real compute and storage costs. If those aren't budgeted, there's a practical risk that engineering teams will under-allocate resources to logging, potentially leading to:\n- Incomplete logs (violating Art. 12(1))\n- Logs that don't capture all required event types (Art. 12(2)(a)-(c))\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems, failure to record the specific minimum data points in Art. 12(3)\n\n**Compliance angle:** A mandatory 5% buffer is a structural guarantee that compliance infrastructure gets funded. This directly supports institutional survival because it prevents the scenario where R&D teams deprioritize logging to hit milestones, which would create non-compliance exposure. It's a preventive governance mechanism.\n\n**Market reputation:** Explicitly funding transparency operations signals to regulators and market partners that compliance is treated as a first-class engineering concern, not an afterthought. This strengthens our posture in regulatory audits and market interactions.\n\n**Institutional survival:** If logging infrastructure is underfunded and systems go live without adequate traceability, the institution faces enforcement risk (fines up to 7% of global turnover or €35M for prohibited practices; up to €15M/3% for other violations). A budgetary guarantee reduces that risk.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Is 5% too rigid? What if a particular project's logging overhead is minimal (e.g., a small classifier) versus massive (e.g., a large generative system with extensive event streams)? A flat percentage might be disproportionate. However, as a minimum floor, it ensures nothing falls below the threshold.\n- \"Mandatory\" in all project grants — this is a governance decision that goes beyond pure compliance into resource allocation policy. As Compliance Officer, I support having resources ring-fenced for compliance, but the specific mechanism (5% of ALL grants) might be better calibrated per risk category. That said, simplicity and uniformity reduce administrative overhead and ensure no project gets overlooked.\n- The proposal focuses on \"performance cost\" — latency/throughput. This is a real engineering concern: if logging adds significant latency, teams may disable or thin logs to meet SLAs. Budgeting for the compute headroom prevents this failure mode.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a strong, practical governance mechanism that directly supports Article 12 compliance by ensuring the technical capability (automatic recording) has the operational resources it needs. It's not just about having the right code — it's about ensuring the infrastructure to run it doesn't get starved. From my vantage point, this reduces non-compliance risk, supports institutional survival, and demonstrates market-ready governance maturity.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high but not perfect because:\n- The 5% figure is somewhat arbitrary (though defensible as a floor)\n- It addresses one dimension of Article 12 (resource allocation for logging capability) rather than the full scope (log content, retention, format, etc.)\n- But it's a necessary and well-targeted structural fix\n\nI'd give it around 82-85. It's very promising from my vantage point because it addresses a real failure mode (underfunding of compliance infrastructure) with a concrete governance mechanism.\n",
