ion phases\" - this implies continuous integration with existing ML pipelines, which requires engineering effort upfront\n- However, much of this infrastructure (version control, experiment tracking) likely already exists in the lab. The incremental cost might be moderate if we're building on what's there rather than greenfield\n- The proposal is reasonably scoped - it's not trying to solve all of Article 12 (logging during operation), just the development-phase documentation aspect from Recital 71\n- Resource efficiency: once built, this should reduce recurring compliance costs significantly vs. manual documentation\n- Risk: over-engineering. If we build a highly sophisticated system when simpler version control + structured templates would suffice, we waste resources\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped proposal that leverages existing infrastructure (version control, experiment tracking) to address a clear regulatory requirement. The automation angle is smart from an efficiency standpoint - it converts recurring manual compliance labor into one-time engineering investment plus low marginal cost per model version. The scope is appropriately bounded to the development/training/validation phase documentation rather than trying to solve all of Article 12. \n\nThe main risk is that \"automatically generates technical dossiers\" could be interpreted as building a complex NLP/document-generation system when in reality what's needed is structured data capture + templated output. But even with that interpretation, the core idea (version-control hooks archiving into a structured repository) is practical and builds on existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this - it's efficient use of resources, builds on existing infrastructure, addresses a clear regulatory requirement, and has good ongoing ROI through automation. The promise score should reflect that while it's solid, the \"automatically generates dossiers\" framing has some scope risk that could inflate costs if not carefully managed. I'd put it around 72-78.\n\nLet me settle on my assessment:\n- Rationale: Leverages existing version-control and experiment-tracking infrastructure (likely already in place) to convert recurring manual documentation labor into a one-time engineering investment with low marginal cost; appropriately scoped to the development-phase record-keeping requirement of Recital 71 without overreaching. The main resource risk is if \"automatically generates\" gets interpreted as building a complex document-generation system when structured templates + data capture would suffice, but the core architecture (versioned repository linking model versions to dev records) is efficient and practical.\n- Decision: approve\n- Promise: 74\n",
