difications. While this could be RELATED to post-market monitoring (Article 72) and to identifying risks, it's not the same as implementing the Article 12 logging capability itself.\n\nHowever, let me think more carefully from a compliance perspective:\n\n1. Does this proposal help satisfy Article 12? Partially — it addresses the \"monitoring\" aspect (Art. 12(2)(b), (c)) but it's not really about implementing the LOGGING requirement itself (Art. 12(1)). The logging is a technical capability of the system; this dashboard is an analytical/visualization layer on top.\n\n2. Is it useful for compliance more broadly? Yes — post-market monitoring is a critical obligation under the AI Act. Having operational telemetry that can detect drift and substantial modifications would be extremely valuable for demonstrating compliance with Article 72 (post-market monitoring) and for fulfilling the risk management obligations. It could also help identify when a \"substantial modification\" has occurred, which triggers re-assessment requirements.\n\n3. From institutional survival perspective: If we fail to demonstrate adequate post-market monitoring, we face regulatory sanctions. This suite directly addresses that gap.\n\n4. Market reputation: Having robust operational monitoring demonstrates due diligence and responsibility to regulators and market participants.\n\n5. However, the proposal is somewhat vague — \"specific triggers and outcomes identified as critical in our internal risk management assessments\" — this references an internal process but doesn't specify whether it actually implements Article 12's logging requirement (automatic recording of events) or just visualizes data that may or may not be logged per Art. 12.\n\n6. The proposal says \"real-time telemetry on high-risk AI deployments\" — this is adjacent to but distinct from the mandatory logging requirement. It could complement it, but I need to consider whether it actually delivers what Article 12 requires or just a 