ducing the risk of gaps or omissions. Manual input is a common source of non-compliance.\n6. The \"modular\" and \"all high-risk projects\" scope suggests institutional-scale coverage rather than project-by-project ad hoc solutions — this is what a compliance officer wants for systemic risk management.\n7. \"Minimizes administrative burden on scientists\" — practically important because if the logging process is too burdensome, researchers will resist or work around it, creating compliance gaps.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n1. Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics). The proposal mentions \"mandatory EU AI Act metrics\" but doesn't explicitly address the biometric-specific logging requirements (start/end times of each use, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons in verification). However, a modular system could be configured to capture these.\n2. \"Over the lifetime of the system\" — the proposal needs to ensure retention policies align with this. A versioned database helps but the retention period must match regulatory requirements.\n3. The scope says \"all high-risk projects\" which is good for institutional coverage, but I need to confirm it covers the full lifecycle including post-market monitoring (Article 72) and not just training.\n4. \"Silent observer during model training and deployment\" — this covers training and deployment, but what about post-market operation? Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system.\" The proposal mentions deployment but doesn't explicitly address ongoing operational monitoring in production beyond initial deployment.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\nThis is a strong, well-conceived infrastructure proposal that directly addresses the core obligation of Article 12. It's systemic (covers all high-risk projects), technical (automatic recording per Article 12(1)), and practical (reduces human error in record-keeping). The tamper-pr