iod of use, reference database, input data matches, and identification of natural persons in verification.\n- It says \"required regulatory format\" somewhat vaguely — who defines that format? The EU AI Act doesn't prescribe a single template; it requires certain information to be present. This could be a gap if interpreted too narrowly.\n- \"Automatically extracts\" is promising but the question is whether all required elements can truly be auto-extracted or whether human curation will still be needed (e.g., risk-management system documentation, capabilities and limitations descriptions).\n- No mention of how this handles post-market monitoring logs specifically (Article 72 reference in Art. 12(2)(b)) vs. build-time documentation.\n\nHowever, from a compliance perspective, this is a strong foundational infrastructure investment. The ability to maintain current technical documentation throughout the system lifetime is one of the most fundamental obligations under Article 12 and Recital 71. Without it, you cannot demonstrate compliance with the broader framework (Article 11 technical documentation, Article 72 post-market monitoring, etc.). It's a force multiplier for compliance.\n\nThe fact that it integrates into build cycles is critical — it means the documentation won't become stale, which is the #1 failure mode in regulatory audits of AI systems.\n\nFor institutional survival: If we cannot produce up-to-date technical documentation on demand (as Article 42 requires upon request by national competent authorities), we face enforcement action. This system directly mitigates that risk.\n\nFor market reputation: A demonstrable, automated compliance pipeline signals to customers and partners that we take regulatory obligations seriously — a competitive differentiator in enterprise sales.\n\nMy decision: This is clearly an approve from my vantage point. It addresses a core, non-negotiable obligation. The promise score should be high but not 100 because of the gaps I not