lf takes engineering time upfront. But if multiple high-risk prototypes are in development, the ROI comes quickly. Even for a single prototype, having a clean, well-documented logging layer speeds up development versus ad-hoc solutions.\n- One concern from my perspective: if the SDK is too rigid or has heavy dependencies, it could slow down rapid prototyping. But \"modular\" and \"toggle via config flag\" suggest they've thought about keeping it lightweight.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- Standardized schema means consistent data for post-market monitoring (Art 72) and compliance verification - this is good for the long term.\n- Automatic capture of timestamps, input data, system state covers Article 12(1) well.\n- The SDK approach means we can update logging capabilities across all prototypes simultaneously when requirements evolve or are clarified by guidance.\n\n**Potential weaknesses from my vantage:**\n- \"Minimizes engineering overhead per project\" - true, but the initial build cost of the SDK is real. However, this is a one-time investment vs. repeated per-project costs.\n- The proposal doesn't mention retention, storage architecture, or data lifecycle management explicitly, but those are infrastructure concerns that can be decoupled from the SDK's API layer.\n- For the specific Annex III 1(a) requirements (biometric systems), the SDK would need additional endpoints beyond generic logging - but \"modular\" suggests extensibility.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a technically sound, well-scoped engineering proposal that directly serves my function's goals of prototyping speed and quality. It reduces per-project overhead, ensures compliance capability is built-in rather than bolted-on later (which always costs more), and the modular design keeps it flexible. The main risk is scope creep or over-engineering the SDK initially, but as stated, it seems appropriately scoped. I'd approve this with a high promise score - maybe 82-85. It's very promising but not a 1