et versions, validation results). Article 12(2) also requires logging of events relevant for: identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operation. The proposal doesn't explicitly address operational logging or post-market event capture. However, it does say \"over the lifetime\" which could encompass this.\n2. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (Article 12(3)), specific minimum logging requirements include recording periods of use, reference databases, input data for matches, and identification of natural persons in verification. The proposal doesn't explicitly address these specific operational logging requirements.\n3. \"Real-time during training\" — but Article 12 applies to the full lifetime including deployment. The pipeline needs to extend beyond training to capture operational events.\n4. However, this is a foundational infrastructure piece. Even if it doesn't cover everything, it's a critical building block for compliance with the documentation and record-keeping requirements.\n\n**Compliance Officer assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal is highly relevant and directly addresses core obligations under Article 12 and Recital 71. The automatic nature of the recording aligns perfectly with the \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording\" language. The synchronization aspect addresses the \"kept up to date\" requirement. Using existing infrastructure (MLflow) makes it cost-effective and feasible.\n\nThe main gap is that it seems focused on training/documentation generation rather than operational event logging during deployment. But as a foundational piece, it's essential. A compliance officer would want this in place because without automated documentation, the institution cannot demonstrate compliance to regulators, which threatens institutional survival. The risk of manual record-keeping (errors, omissions, version mismatches) is a significant compliance liability.\n\nI'd approve this. It directly 