        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer concerned with institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1270 suggests a \"Dynamic Risk Classification Taxonomy\" that would:\n1. Categorize AI modules by risk level based on development stage\n2. Distinguish between exploratory research and deployable high-risk systems\n3. Collaborate with legal experts to map research trajectories against Annex III criteria using a living document\n4. Allow toggling logging intensity based on project maturity\n5. Delay full record-keeping until deployment readiness\n\nLet me assess this from a compliance perspective:\n\n**Positive aspects:**\n- It's smart to have an internal framework for classifying risk levels - this is good governance\n- Collaborating with legal experts to map against Annex III is prudent\n- A \"living document\" approach is appropriate given the evolving nature of AI development\n- The general principle of proportionality (matching compliance effort to actual risk level) aligns with the Act's structure\n\n**Critical concerns from a Compliance Officer perspective:**\n\n1. **The core premise is dangerously flawed.** Article 12 states: \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" The key word is \"the lifetime of the system.\" If a system is classified as high-risk, logging must be in place from the moment it becomes a high-risk system - not from \"deployment readiness.\" The Act doesn't provide a grace period for \"development stage\" to exempt you from record-keeping. Once your system meets the definition of a high-risk AI system, Article 12 applies.\n\n2. **\"Toggle logging intensity based on project maturity\"** is extremely dangerous language. This sounds like it's proposing to reduce or delay mandatory logging obligations based on an internal assessment of \"maturity.\" The Act doesn't have a maturity-based exception for high-risk systems. If the system IS high-risk (per Annex I products or Annex III categories), Article 12 applies in full.\n\n3. **\"Delaying full record-keeping until a project reaches deployment readiness\"** - This is a compliance violation risk. Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system.\" If you're developing a high-risk system and it's already technically capable of being used, the logging obligations attach to the system itself. You can't say \"it's not deployed yet so we don't need logs.\" The technical capability for logging must be BUILT INTO the system - which means it needs to be in place during development if the system is already high-risk.\n\n4. **However**, there IS a legitimate distinction: exploratory research that doesn't meet the definition of a \"high-risk AI system\" under the Act may not be subject to Article 12 at all. The proposal does say it distinguishes between \"exploratory research and deployable high-risk systems.\" That part is valid - you don't need to apply Article 12 to something that isn't yet (and won't become) a high-risk system.\n\n5. **The \"living document\" approach** for mapping against Annex III is actually good practice, but the framing of \"toggling logging intensity\" suggests the framework might be used as a justification to under-implement mandatory controls once a system IS classified as high-risk.\n\n6. **Market reputation risk**: If regulators see an internal framework that explicitly contemplates \"delaying full record-keeping,\" it could signal a culture of compliance minimization rather than compliance embedding. This is a reputational and institutional survival risk.\n\n7. **The collaboration with legal experts** is good, but the framing suggests this is about finding ways to reduce compliance burden rather than ensuring robust compliance. The \"compliance tax stifling early-stage exploration\" framing, while understandable from an R&D perspective, puts me on alert as a Compliance Officer - it suggests the primary motivation is operational convenience over regulatory adherence.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has some legitimate elements (internal risk classification, legal collaboration, distinguishing non-high-risk research from high-risk systems). However, its core mechanism - \"toggling logging intensity based on project maturity\" and \"delaying full record-keeping until deployment readiness\" - is fundamentally incompatible with how Article 12 operates. The obligation attaches to the system when it IS a high-risk AI system, not when it's deployed. If you're building something that will be high-risk, the technical architecture for logging needs to be designed in from early development (at least before the point where the system becomes capable of functioning as described in Annex III).\n\nThe proposal as written risks creating an institutional culture of compliance deferral that could lead to violations. From a survival standpoint, being caught with a framework that explicitly planned to delay mandatory record-keeping would be devastating in a regulatory enforcement action or litigation discovery.\n\nHowever, I should note: the proposal does say \"categorizes AI modules by risk level\" and distinguishes research from deployable systems. That part is fine. The problem is specifically the \"toggle logging intensity\" and \"delay full record-keeping until deployment readiness\" language for what it calls \"deployable high-risk systems.\"\n\nI think this is a REJECT. The proposal's core operational mechanism (toggling/delaying logging based on maturity) contradicts Article 12's requirement that high-risk systems \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events over the lifetime of the system.\" While the risk-classification framework itself could be useful, as written, the proposal is structured around a compliance-avoidance logic rather than a compliance-implementation logic. The promise score should reflect that while I can see some salvageable elements (the classification taxonomy, legal collaboration), the operational core is problematic.\n\nI'd give it maybe a 30 - there's value in having an internal risk-classification framework and working with legal on Annex III mapping, but the \"toggle\" and \"delay\" language makes this as-written something I cannot endorse from a compliance standpoint.\n",
