ent — who builds it? What's the timeline? This diverts engineers from active projects to build infrastructure that may not be needed immediately if you only have 1-2 high-risk systems.\n- The proposal says \"across all high-risk AI projects\" — but how many do we actually have right now? If we have very few, the overhead of building a universal framework might not pay off yet. However, if we expect growth, it's forward-looking efficiency.\n- \"Mandatory data schemas\" could be rigid and slow to evolve as the regulation is further clarified through implementing acts and standards. Over-engineering for compliance that isn't fully specified yet.\n- The proposal frames it as \"guarantees regulatory compliance\" — this is overclaiming. Compliance is a broader obligation (technical documentation, risk management, etc.). Logging is one component. But from my perspective, the core value is operational efficiency.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is fundamentally a good resource-allocation play. It's the kind of infrastructure investment that pays off by reducing per-project compliance costs. The key concern is timing and scope — building a universal framework when you might only need logging in 2-3 projects could be premature. But if the lab has multiple high-risk AI systems in development or planned, this is exactly the right move: build once, deploy many times.\n\nThe \"mandatory\" aspect and design-phase integration are appropriate for efficiency (avoids costly retrofits). The shared library model is standard engineering practice and efficient.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a reasonable infrastructure investment that addresses resource-allocation efficiency directly. The main risk is over-investment if the number of high-risk systems is small, but the proposal doesn't specify scope beyond \"all high-risk AI projects,\" which suggests it scales to what we actually have.\n\nPromise score: I'd put this around 72-78. It's a solid operational investment with clear effic