        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2191\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the hard technical obligation in Article 12(1) by embedding automated event recording into the development template as a default rather than a per-project afterthought, which is exactly the architectural posture Recital 71 envisions for 'lifetime' logging; consistency across research teams also mitigates the principal audit-risk in multi-team R&D environments (uneven or missing log coverage during market-surveillance inspections). My only reservation is that the stated capture scope ('inputs, outputs, timestamps') is generic and does not yet enumerate the specific data elements mandated by Article 12(3)(a)–(d) for Annex III 1(a) systems (reference database identity, match inputs, verifier identification) or the purpose-proportional calibration required by Article 12(2), so the library will need a configurable schema layer per system category before it can be declared sufficient; nonetheless, committing two engineers to build the foundational wrapper is the correct compliance infrastructure investment for institutional survival and regulator-facing credibility.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 87\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to act as a Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. I need to screen proposal P2191 from my professional vantage point: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**P2191: Unified Compliance Logging Framework**\n- A standardized internal software library for automated event recording and timestamping for all high-risk AI modules\n- Plug-and-play utility for researchers\n- Two software engineers to build an API wrapping existing model execution calls to capture inputs, outputs, timestamps automatically\n- Integrated into the lab's core development template\n- Reduces per-project engineering burden and ensures consistency across teams\n\n**My vantage point as Compliance Officer:** institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation.\n\nLet me think about this carefully:\n\n**Strengths from a compliance perspective:**\n1. Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified framework that embeds logging at the infrastructure level directly addresses this requirement. This is not optional — it's a hard technical requirement.\n2. Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities enabling recording of events relevant to identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. A standardized library ensures these capabilities are consistently present across all high-risk systems.\n3. Recital 71 emphasizes that \"high-risk AI systems should technically allow for the automatic recording of events, by means of logs, over the duration of the lifetime of the system.\" The \"lifetime\" aspect is critical — a framework integrated into the development template ensures logging persists as models are updated and deployed.\n4. Consistency across teams reduces the risk that one team forgets to implement logging or implements it incompletely — this is a major compliance gap risk in multi-team R&D environments.\n5. From a market reputation standpoint, having a unified compliance infrastructure signals maturity and preparedness to regulators (market surveillance authorities) during inspections or when responding to complaints.\n6. Institutional survival: non-compliance with Article 12 could result in fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover under Article 99). A systematic approach reduces this existential risk.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n1. \"Two software engineers\" — is that sufficient? For a comprehensive framework covering all high-risk AI modules, capturing the specific requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (which require recording start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification), two engineers might be thin. However, this is an initial build — it could iterate.\n2. The proposal says \"wraps existing model execution calls\" — this is good architecturally but may not capture all the specific logging requirements. For example, Article 12(3)(b) requires recording \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system\" — a simple wrapper around model execution might not naturally capture that unless the framework is designed with those specifics in mind.\n3. The proposal focuses on \"inputs, outputs, and timestamps automatically\" — this addresses the basic requirement but doesn't explicitly mention the specific elements required for Annex III 1(a) systems (reference databases, match data, verification personnel identification). However, as a foundational library, it could be extended.\n4. \"Plug-and-play utility for researchers\" — from a compliance standpoint, I want to make sure this doesn't create a false sense of security where researchers think they're compliant just by using the template, when in fact the specific logging requirements vary by system category and intended purpose (Article 12(2) says \"appropriate to the intended purpose\"). The framework needs to be configurable per system type.\n5. Integration into \"core development template\" is strong — it means logging isn't an afterthought but a default. This aligns with Recital 71's emphasis on lifetime recording.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strongly compliance-aligned proposal. It directly addresses the core technical requirement of Article 12(1) and creates the infrastructure for meeting 12(2) and potentially 12(3). The fact that it's embedded in the development template rather than being a per-project afterthought is excellent from a compliance standpoint. The risk reduction from consistency across teams is significant — in a multi-team R&D environment, ad-hoc logging implementations are where compliance gaps emerge during regulatory audits.\n\nThe two-engineer allocation is a resource question more than a compliance design question. From my vantage point, the architecture and approach are sound; whether two engineers can deliver it well is an operational/resourcing concern that leadership would address at implementation stage. The proposal as stated commits resources to build this, which is what matters for institutional survival.\n\nFrom market reputation: having a unified, auditable logging framework is something you'd want to demonstrate to regulators, enterprise clients in regulated sectors, and during any post-market monitoring interaction. It shows organizational maturity.\n\nI would approve this with high confidence. The promise score should reflect how strongly I'd back it from my compliance vantage point. This is directly on-mission for Article 12 compliance. My main caveat is that the proposal as stated doesn't explicitly enumerate all the specific data fields required under Article 12(3) for certain system categories, but as a foundational framework it's the right architectural approach. I'd give it a high score — maybe 85-90. It's not a perfect 100 because the scope description is somewhat generic (\"inputs, outputs, and timestamps\") and doesn't explicitly address the specific logging requirements of Article 12(3)(a)-(d) for biometric identification systems or the \"appropriate to intended purpose\" calibration of Article 12(2). But it's a very strong foundation.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 88.\n",
