me architecture works across \"diverse models regardless of the specific research team\" — this is good reuse.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- Is it over-engineered? A single middleware layer for ALL high-risk systems might be too rigid if different use cases need very different logging granularity (e.g., Annex III 1(a) biometric identification needs specific fields per Article 12(3), while other high-risk uses may need less). However, the proposal says \"at a minimum\" captures timestamps, input queries, operator IDs — and the regulation does require different levels of detail for different system types. The proposal could be seen as potentially too one-size-fits-all.\n- However, even if some customization is needed at the edges, the core middleware approach still saves massive resources compared to per-project solutions.\n- \"Secure, immutable database\" — this may have cost implications but is a reasonable regulatory requirement (audit trail).\n- The proposal doesn't specify how it handles the specific requirements of Article 12(3) for biometric systems (reference databases, match data, natural person identification), but as an architecture layer, those could be plug-in modules.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my resource-allocation and infrastructure perspective, this is a strong proposal. It addresses a real compliance requirement (Article 12 is mandatory for high-risk systems) in the most cost-efficient way possible — build once, deploy across all teams. It reduces per-project overhead, leverages existing IT infrastructure, and frees researcher time for science. The main risk is that it might be slightly too rigid to handle edge cases, but the core architecture soundness is high.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score: I'd give it around 82-85. It's very well-aligned with my domain concerns (efficiency, infrastructure reuse, not consuming research time for compliance). The slight deduction is because I'd want to see how it handles the diff