/continuous nature of Article 12 compliance (lifetime recording). A one-time audit at cycle end isn't sufficient for a lifetime requirement.\n- \"Rotating team\" could create inconsistency in interpretation and standards — from a compliance standpoint, you want consistency in how requirements are assessed. However, rotation also prevents groupthink and keeps the process fresh.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention Annex III point 1(a) specific logging requirements (start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons for verification). It's somewhat generic about \"validating logs against Article 12 requirements.\"\n- No mention of post-market monitoring integration — Article 12(2)(b) specifically requires logs to facilitate post-market monitoring under Article 72. The proposal is deployment-focused, not lifecycle-focused.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n- This is a solid structural mechanism that would demonstrate good faith and systematic compliance effort to regulators\n- Having a documented internal audit trail of your own compliance checks strengthens your position in any regulatory inquiry\n- The \"quality gate\" concept directly protects against the scenario where you deploy a system whose logging doesn't meet Article 12 — that's an existential risk for institutional survival\n- However, the timing limitation (end of development cycle only) is a gap. For a compliance officer, I'd want this to also include periodic reviews during operational lifetime, not just pre-deployment gates.\n- Still, as a foundational mechanism, it addresses the core requirement and creates accountability structures. It's not perfect but it's directionally correct and practically implementable.\n\n**Decision:** This is fundamentally sound from a compliance perspective. It establishes accountability, creates a verifiable process, and directly targets Article 12 requirements. 