        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1878\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core Article 12 requirement—automatic event logging with timestamps, inputs/outputs, and specific metadata for Annex III biometric systems—is a well-bounded engineering task solvable with existing structured-logging patterns within weeks to months. This proposal instead frames the response as an open-ended R&D program on causal tracing and model interpretability ('semantic insight into decision-making,' 'full forensic transparency'), which are unsolved frontier research problems with no clear target state, no prototype milestone, and a technical path that cannot be validated against a crisp acceptance criterion. From a prototyping-speed standpoint, I cannot define what 'done' looks like here, nor can I iterate in sprints against an undefined goal; committing implementation resources to this would stall the concrete logging infrastructure we actually need to ship for compliance.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 12\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1878 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Advanced Traceability and AI Forensics Project\" - an R&D initiative to develop next-generation logging with semantic insight into model decision-making, going beyond basic event recording toward full forensic transparency. It involves linking low-level system logs with high-level causal tracing and interpretability tools, publishing findings as a new standard for AI accountability, and framing it as turning regulatory constraint into competitive advantage.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability concerns:**\n- \"Semantic insight into model decision-making\" is an extremely broad and unsolved research problem. Causal tracing and interpretability are active research areas with no consensus solutions, especially for complex models. This is not a well-scoped engineering task — it's open-ended fundamental research.\n- \"Linking low-level system logs with high-level causal tracing\" is technically very challenging. The gap between raw event logs (timestamps, inputs/outputs) and semantic/causal explanations of *why* a model made a particular decision is enormous. There's no clear engineering path here.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what AI systems it targets (foundation models? smaller specialized models?), making technical feasibility hard to assess.\n- \"Full forensic transparency\" is a very high bar that may not be achievable with current interpretability techniques, especially for large neural networks.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping concerns:**\n- This is framed as an R&D initiative, which inherently means long timelines and uncertain outcomes. It's not a prototype — it's a research program.\n- The scope is enormous: semantic logging + causal tracing + interpretability tools + publishing a new standard. That's multiple multi-year projects in one.\n- There's no clear deliverable or milestone structure that would allow us to iterate quickly and validate progress.\n- \"Publish these findings as a new standard\" implies a very long timeline with significant coordination overhead (standards bodies, consensus building).\n\n**What Article 12 actually requires:**\n- Automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system\n- Recording events relevant for: identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation\n- For biometric identification systems (Annex III point 1(a)): specific minimum logging requirements (period of use, reference database, input data with match, identification of natural persons in verification)\n\nThe regulation's actual requirements are relatively concrete and engineering-friendly. You need to log events, timestamps, inputs/outputs, and some metadata. This is well within current engineering capability. The proposal dramatically overreaches beyond what the regulation requires.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed:\n- The core regulatory requirement (logging) is straightforward to implement with existing tooling patterns. You don't need \"semantic insight\" or \"causal tracing\" to comply with Article 12.\n- This proposal confuses compliance with research frontier problems. It's trying to solve interpretability — one of the hardest open problems in AI — as a means to meet a logging requirement that has clear, bounded engineering solutions.\n- The scope is too broad, the technical path unclear, and the timeline unpredictable.\n- However, there IS some kernel of value: thinking about structured, queryable logs vs. flat event streams is a legitimate engineering design choice. But \"semantic insight into model decision-making\" and \"full forensic transparency\" go far beyond what's needed and are not technically viable on any reasonable timeline for prototyping.\n\nI'd lean toward rejecting this from my vantage point. The technical viability of \"next-generation logging that provides semantic insight into model decision-making\" as a deliverable is questionable — it's research, not engineering. The speed of prototyping would be very poor because there's no clear target state to prototype against. The quality question is: what does \"done\" look like? It's undefined.\n\nThat said, I can see some partial value in thinking about log architecture and structured event recording. But as framed — an R&D initiative on causal tracing and interpretability linked to logging — this is not a technically viable prototyping project. It's a multi-year research bet with unclear deliverables.\n\nMy decision: reject. The technical scope is too open-ended, the path from current state to \"semantic insight into model decision-making\" is not well understood enough to prototype against, and it significantly overreaches what Article 12 actually requires (which has clear, bounded engineering solutions).\n\nPromise score: Maybe around 10-15. There's a tiny sliver of interesting thinking in there about log architecture, but as framed, this is not something I'd commit implementation resources to. It's research masquerading as an engineering response to regulation.\n",
