ks, this is viable.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The core promise is decoupling compliance from development — this is exactly what protects prototyping speed. If engineers don't have to modify model code to add logging, they can iterate faster.\n- A standardized framework means new high-risk modules can be brought into compliance by configuration rather than custom coding each time. This speeds up bringing new systems to market.\n- The \"modular interceptor\" approach is practical — teams can build their models and plug them in without a compliance team blocking every PR.\n- However, building the middleware itself takes time and resources upfront. But it's a one-time investment that pays off across all modules.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal somewhat undersells the complexity. For Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.), the logging requirements are very specific (reference database identity, match results, human verifier ID). An API gateway interceptor won't naturally capture these — you'd need deeper integration hooks into those specific modules. So \"without altering core model logic\" might be too strong a claim for all cases.\n- That said, the framework could define an interface/contract that module developers implement for their domain-specific events while the middleware handles the generic transport, storage, and format compliance. This is still viable but requires more than just gateway-level interception.\n\nOverall: The architectural direction is sound and aligns well with protecting prototyping velocity. It's technically feasible as a framework (with the caveat that some high-risk modules will need deeper hooks beyond gateway level). From my perspective, this is a strong proposal because it directly addresses the tension between compliance overhead and development speed. I'd approve it but note that the \"without altering core model logic\" claim needs nuancing for Annex III 1(a) systems — thou