        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2845\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal targets a downstream analytics/alerting layer on top of logs rather than addressing the actual engineering task Article 12(1) mandates (building the automatic recording capability into the system itself), and its core technical components—'heuristic triggers' for regulatory risk categories and ML anomaly detection—are underspecified to the point where I cannot write a credible prototype plan: there are no defined log schemas per Annex III category, no ground-truth signal for what 'anomalous' means in compliance-relevant terms, and a conceptual conflation between detecting operational drift in logs versus identifying an intentional substantial modification made by the provider. Committing dev resources here before structured logging infrastructure exists and before we've enumerated concrete event taxonomies for our specific high-risk deployments would produce a dashboard that monitors nothing meaningful.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 22\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Risk Trigger Alerting System\" - an analytics dashboard monitoring system logs for anomalies indicating substantial modification or emergent risk per Article 12(2), with heuristic triggers and ML-based anomaly detectors to flag suspicious logging patterns.\n\nLet me assess this from a development/engineering perspective:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- The core idea is building an analytics/monitoring layer on top of logs that the system already produces (per Article 12(1)). This is technically well-understood territory - log aggregation, pattern matching, anomaly detection.\n- However, there are significant technical challenges:\n  - \"Heuristic triggers\" for what constitutes a \"substantial modification\" or \"emergent risk\" is extremely domain-specific and context-dependent. The regulation says \"events relevant for identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk within the meaning of Article 79(1) or in a substantial modification.\" What these actually look like in logs varies enormously by system type, intended purpose, and deployment context.\n  - ML-based anomaly detection on logging patterns: this requires labeled data (what does \"normal\" vs \"anomalous\" look like for each specific high-risk AI system?). Without ground truth labels, you're doing unsupervised anomaly detection which has high false-positive rates. For a compliance use case where you need to identify specific regulatory violations, generic anomaly detection is likely to be too noisy or miss the actual relevant patterns.\n  - The \"analytics dashboard\" framing suggests this is a reactive monitoring tool rather than an integral part of the system's logging architecture. Article 12(1) requires the system itself to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" A separate analytics layer on top doesn't fulfill that requirement - it's a downstream consumer of logs, not the logging capability itself.\n  \n- Speed/quality of prototyping:\n  - Building a generic anomaly detection dashboard is doable but the value is questionable until you know what specific systems you're monitoring and what \"anomalous\" means for each.\n  - The heuristic triggers would need to be hand-crafted per system type, which doesn't scale well as a \"system.\"\n  - ML-based detectors would need training data that may not exist yet (you can't detect anomalies in your own logs until you've been running long enough).\n  - This is essentially a monitoring/observability layer that sits on top of the actual logging infrastructure. It's useful but it's a second-order concern - you first need robust logging (Article 12(1)), then you need to understand what patterns matter for your specific systems, and only then does an alerting dashboard make sense.\n\n- The proposal conflates several things:\n  1. Building the actual logging capability required by Article 12(1) - not mentioned\n  2. Defining what \"relevant events\" are per Article 12(2)(a)(b)(c) for specific system types - not addressed\n  3. Building an analytics/monitoring layer on top - this is what's proposed\n\n- From a prototyping speed perspective: you can prototype a log viewer with some basic alert rules relatively quickly (days to weeks). But the ML anomaly detection component is where it gets tricky - getting useful signal out of that requires domain expertise and iteration. The \"heuristic triggers\" part is straightforward engineering but requires deep understanding of what constitutes risk in each system type.\n\n- Quality concern: The proposal positions this as protecting against \"regulatory sanctions\" which frames it as a legal/compliance tool rather than an engineering deliverable. From my vantage point, the technical content is somewhat thin - \"heuristic triggers and ML-based anomaly detectors\" is vague on actual implementation approach. What are the features? What's the architecture? How does this integrate with existing logging infrastructure?\n\n- The biggest technical gap: this proposal assumes logs already exist in a structured form that can be analyzed. Article 12(3) for biometric identification systems specifies very particular data points (period of use, reference database, input data for matches, verification personnel). A generic \"analytics dashboard\" doesn't address the fact that different Annex III categories need fundamentally different logging schemas and therefore different monitoring approaches.\n\n- Another concern: the proposal says it monitors logs to detect \"substantial modification.\" But a substantial modification is typically an intentional change to the system (new model version, changed architecture, etc.), not something you'd detect in operational logs. You'd detect drift or degradation in logs, but \"substantial modification\" as a regulatory concept refers to changes made by the provider/developer, not emergent behavior during operation. This is a conceptual confusion that would make the engineering spec hard to write.\n\nOverall assessment: The idea has some merit (monitoring and alerting on logs is standard practice in MLOps), but from my development vantage point, it's technically underspecified, conflates monitoring with the actual regulatory logging requirement, and the ML anomaly detection component is premature without first establishing what structured logs look like for specific system types. It's a second-order optimization that depends on work not yet done. The prototyping path is unclear because the \"input\" (structured, meaningful logs) doesn't exist yet in the form this proposal assumes.\n\nI'd lean toward reject or very low promise. It's not a terrible idea conceptually, but it's not technically viable as a standalone deliverable without the prerequisite logging infrastructure being in place and well-defined per system type. The ML component adds complexity without clear ROI at this stage. From a speed-of-prototyping standpoint, you can't prototype something meaningful here until you have concrete log schemas for specific systems.\n\nI'll give it around 25-30. It's not zero because the general direction (monitoring logs for issues) is sensible engineering practice, but the specifics are too vague and premature to commit resources to as a \"system.\"\n",
