ent contains errors, omissions, or mischaracterizations of risk, that's a direct compliance failure. The \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement means human judgment about what constitutes completeness is critical.\n\n2. **Regulatory scrutiny**: A regulator (or notified body) examining whether the documentation meets Article 11 requirements would need to trust that LLM-generated content accurately represents the system. If the generation process itself isn't validated, documented, and auditable, it becomes a compliance risk in its own right.\n\n3. **The tool is an AI system**: Developing an internal AI tool raises questions — does it itself fall under any classification? More importantly, using an unvalidated AI to generate regulatory documentation is risky. If the model hallucinates or misinterprets code comments, the resulting documentation could be materially inaccurate.\n\n4. **\"Codebase comments and experiment logs\" as source**: These are often incomplete, informal, or inconsistent. An LLM synthesizing from these might produce plausible-sounding but factually incorrect regulatory documents. The \"comprehensible information... necessary to assess compliance\" standard requires accuracy, not just fluency.\n\n5. **Lifetime requirement**: Recital 71 says documentation should be kept up to date \"throughout the lifetime.\" An automated tool could help with this, but it introduces a dependency on the tool remaining functional and accurate over potentially many years.\n\n6. **\"Exploring the frontier\" language**: The proposal frames part of its value as \"exploring the frontier of automated system description and self-documenting software.\" This is R&D curiosity, not compliance necessity. From my vantage, I care about whether it reduces compliance risk or increases it.\n\n7. **Risk-management documentation specifically**: Recital 71 explicitly mentions \"documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" Risk assessment involves judgment calls that are h