/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- From a prototyping standpoint, the standard approach for Article 12 compliance is well-understood: structured event logging (JSON logs, append-only stores, etc.) with the specific fields mandated by paragraphs 2 and 3. You can prototype that in days to weeks. It's boring but it works.\n- This proposal would take months or years of research before you even have a working prototype, and there's no guarantee the \"semantic compression\" will produce genuinely interpretable output. The risk of spending significant engineering resources on this with uncertain technical payoff is high.\n- Even if you did develop such a system, integrating it into production high-risk AI systems while maintaining performance SLAs would be a major engineering challenge (real-time latency for semantic compression, storage costs, etc.).\n\n**Fit to the regulation:**\n- Article 12 says \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" It doesn't mandate \"semantic\" or \"causal\" logging. The minimum requirements in paragraph 3 are specific and concrete. A well-designed structured event logger with the mandated fields would satisfy the letter and spirit of the regulation.\n- The proposal's framing of \"pushing the knowledge frontier by turning mandatory record-keeping into a tool for deep scientific insight\" is a nice aspirational goal but doesn't change the engineering reality that there's a simpler, more viable path to compliance.\n\n**My decision:** From my vantage point (technical viability and prototyping speed/quality), this proposal fails on both counts. The technical approach relies on unsolved research problems (real-time causal reasoning extraction, reliable semantic compression of latent spaces into interpretable form). The prototyping timeline is unbounded because we don't have a clear engineering path — it's fundamentally a research question, not an engineering one. Meanwhile, the regulatory requirement can be met with well-understood structured logging techniques that are prototype-able in weeks. I would reject this from my function.\n\nPromise score: This is interesting as a long-term research direction (interpretability is valuable), but from my specific vantage point of \"can we build this, when will it work, and does it actually solve the regulatory problem better than boring structured logging?\" — the answer is largely no. I'd give it maybe 15-20. It's not a terrible idea in abstract, but it's not viable for near-term prototyping or compliance delivery. Let me settle on 15.\n",
