        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2841 is about an \"Immutable Data Lineage and Versioning System\" that maps model predictions back to specific versions of reference databases, using content-addressable storage with cryptographic hashing.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Content-addressable storage (CAS) is a well-established pattern (git, IPFS, Docker layers). It's technically mature and not experimental.\n- Cryptographic hashing for linking input data to database states is straightforward and reliable.\n- The concept of versioning reference databases and tracking which version was used during inference is a standard practice in ML ops (data versioning via DVC, LakeFS, Delta Lake, etc.).\n- This is technically sound and feasible. The engineering complexity is moderate - it's not a research problem, it's an implementation problem with known solutions.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- CAS layers can be prototyped quickly if you use existing libraries/tools (e.g., git internals, IPFS go-lib, or even simple hash-based blob stores).\n- However, integrating this into the inference pipeline requires careful design to add minimal latency. The \"immutable\" requirement means write-once storage, which is architecturally clean but adds operational complexity (storage growth, cleanup policies).\n- For prototyping: you could build a working prototype in weeks - hash the DB state at each update, store the hash alongside prediction records. This is very doable.\n- The \"debug model drift and data contamination\" angle is a practical engineering benefit that goes beyond compliance - this makes it more than a checkbox item.\n\n**Scope relative to Article 12:**\n- Article 12(3)(b) specifically requires logging \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" This proposal directly addresses that.\n- Article 12(3)(c) requires logging \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" - this is also covered.\n- The content-addressable approach elegantly handles both: you store the hash of the DB state and the hash of the matched input, creating an immutable link.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Immutable\" storage at scale can get expensive. Storage growth needs to be considered for prototyping - but for a prototype, this is manageable.\n- The proposal says it \"directly satisfies Article 12(3)\" which is specifically for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification/verification). If the lab's system isn't in that category, this specific implementation might be over-engineered or misdirected. However, the general pattern of data lineage versioning would still apply to 12(2)(a)-(c).\n- From a prototyping speed standpoint: this is a well-defined engineering task with clear success criteria. No research risk. The main risk is integration complexity into existing pipelines.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is technically viable, uses proven patterns, has clear scope, and can be prototyped at reasonable speed. It addresses specific regulatory requirements while providing genuine engineering value (debugging drift/contamination). From a dev engineer's perspective, this is a clean, well-scoped technical problem with known solutions. I'd approve it.\n\nPromise score: This is solid but not revolutionary - it's \"do the right thing with established tech.\" The prototyping path is clear, the risk is low, and the value is real. I'd put it around 72-78. It's a good use of engineering resources but doesn't have the \"wow\" factor of something novel or strategically transformative. It's solidly above average for an implementation project.\n\nLet me settle on 74 - technically sound, well-scoped, quick to prototype, directly addresses regulatory text, adds real d