m my vantage point:**\n- \"Diverse projects\" - will a single API truly capture all relevant events for Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically? Article 12(3) has specific minimum requirements (reference database against which input data is checked, etc.) that may not be generic. A universal layer might miss project-specific fields.\n- However, the proposal says it's a \"standardized software library\" - this could have hooks for project-specific extensions while maintaining the core mandatory fields. The text doesn't explicitly address the reference database requirement in 12(3)(b), which is specific to biometric/identity verification systems.\n- But as a compliance matter, having a base layer that captures the universal minimum (timestamps, input data, user IDs) and then requiring project-specific extensions for Annex III point 1(a) systems would be appropriate. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention this limitation, but it also doesn't claim to cover everything - it says \"mandatory timestamps, input data, and user identifiers by default.\"\n- From a pure compliance perspective, this is a strong foundational infrastructure investment. The risk of NOT having automated logging is severe: manual or ad-hoc logging creates gaps that regulators will catch during audits, and the institution could face enforcement actions (fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, but also significant penalties for non-compliance with high-risk system requirements).\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- Without a systematic approach, each new project is a potential compliance gap. At scale, this becomes unmanageable. A universal layer scales compliance with growth.\n- If the institution gets hit with a regulatory investigation, having a single, auditable telemetry architecture dramatically reduces defense costs and demonstrates good faith.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- Demonstrates to clients and partners that the institution takes AI governance seriously through engineering, not just policy documents.\n- If this layer is well-documented, it can be referenced in technical documentation (Annex IV) as evidence of compliance.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It addresses the core obligation of Article 12(1) and partially addresses 12(3). The \"by default\" aspect eliminates human error risk. The main gap I see is that it may not fully cover all of Article 12(3)'s specific requirements (particularly 12(3)(b) - reference database), but as a foundational layer with project-specific extensions, it's the right architecture.\n\nI'll approve this. Promise score: It's very strong for compliance - maybe 85-90. The slight deduction is because the proposal as stated doesn't explicitly address the full scope of Article 12(3)(b) (reference database) and could be seen as incomplete without project-specific extensions being mandated. But the core infrastructure investment is exactly what's needed.\n\nI'll go with 85.\n",
