ance.\n- The scope seems appropriately narrow - it focuses on specific Article 12(3) obligations rather than being a sprawling, unfocused initiative.\n- One concern: \"real-time\" flagging in the R&D cycle. As a compliance officer, I want continuous assurance, not just point-in-time checks. A validation suite that runs as part of CI/CD or ongoing monitoring would be ideal. The proposal says \"scans system logs against EU AI Act requirements and flags gaps in real-time\" which suggests continuous operation - good.\n- From institutional survival: if we deploy a high-risk system without proper logging, we face penalties (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for certain violations under Article 99), market withdrawal orders, and reputational damage. Catching gaps early is critical.\n- From regulatory compliance perspective: this tool directly supports our ability to demonstrate compliance with Article 12 during regulatory inspections or audits. Having automated evidence that we validated logging capabilities strengthens our position.\n- From market reputation: being known as an institution that proactively validates its AI systems against EU requirements before deployment is a strong competitive signal to enterprise clients and regulators.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- \"The tools team will create\" - this implies internal resource commitment, which is what we're approving. That's fine, it's the proposal's implementation path.\n- The scope seems reasonable and bounded - it's not asking for a full compliance management system, just a diagnostic/validation tool focused on logging requirements.\n- It specifically targets Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification), which is where Article 12(3) applies. This shows regulatory precision.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses specific regulatory obligations, provides continuous assurance rather than one-time checks, catches issues early in the development lifecycle (redu