fy presence of fields but doesn't prove the logs are meaningful or that the system's behavior in edge cases is captured. However, this is a framing issue, not a fundamental flaw in the concept.\n2. The proposal focuses on log *content* validation (do the right fields exist?) rather than log *integrity* and *retention* over the system's lifetime. Article 12 says \"over the lifetime of the system\" - a test suite that validates at deployment time doesn't address drift, system updates, or long-term retention challenges. But this could be part of an iterative testing process.\n3. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) are very detailed - the proposal would need to specifically address those four sub-points (a)-(d). The proposal says \"Article 12 criteria\" which could encompass this.\n4. The proposal is about verification/testing, not about the actual implementation of logging capabilities. It's a compliance *assurance* tool rather than a compliance *implementation* tool. But from my vantage point as Compliance Officer, I need tools to verify that engineering has done their job correctly - so this is highly relevant.\n5. \"Synthetic test cases\" - good approach because you can't always rely on real operational data for testing, and synthetic cases allow controlled triggering of specific event types without needing actual risk events.\n\n**From my professional vantage point (Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n\nThis is a highly relevant proposal. The EU AI Act's enforcement mechanism includes the ability of market surveillance authorities to request technical documentation and logs. If we cannot demonstrate that our systems properly log the required events, we face:\n- Fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for non-compliance with high-risk system requirements)\n- Withdrawal from market\n- Reputational damage in the AI industry\n\n