d be extended.\n\n**Recital 71:** Emphasizes comprehensible information, traceability, technical documentation kept up to date, automatic recording of events by means of logs over the duration of the lifetime.\n- The immutable database supports \"kept up to date\" and audit trail requirements. The centralized approach helps with traceability across projects.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n1. Directly addresses Article 12(1) — automatic recording requirement. This is non-negotiable regulatory language (\"shall technically allow\").\n2. Centralized immutable database supports audit trails and regulator access (Article 78, 79 post-market obligations).\n3. Standardization across projects reduces compliance risk from inconsistent logging practices — a major institutional survival concern. If different teams log differently, we're exposed.\n4. Removing manual burden from researchers increases actual adoption — if researchers bypass the system because it's burdensome, we have gaps in our logs, which is a direct compliance violation.\n5. \"Over the lifetime of the system\" — a centralized persistent database supports this temporal requirement better than per-project ephemeral logging.\n\nConcerns:\n1. The proposal is somewhat generic. It says \"event timestamps and system states\" but doesn't explicitly map to the specific event types required by 12(2)(a)-(c) or the biometric-specific requirements in 12(3). As a compliance officer, I need assurance that the middleware captures the RIGHT events, not just ANY events.\n2. \"Lightweight Python library\" — is this robust enough for all architectures? What about non-Python ML pipelines? The proposal says \"all high-risk prototypes\" but limits itself to Python integration into \"core ML pipeline.\"\n3. No mention of log retention periods (which must align with the system's lifetime plus regulatory requirements).\n4. No mention of access controls, data protection (GDPR interplay), or who can access/modify 