        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2077\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core idea—stamping each inference event with the version identifier of the reference database in use—is technically viable using mature data-versioning tooling (DVC, LakeFS, Delta Lake) and is prototypable within a sprint by wrapping the DB access layer. However, the proposal's specification of *per-event* cryptographic immutable snapshots of an entire biometric reference store introduces unnecessary engineering friction: hashing or snapshotting a large template database at every match event will not scale to real-time inference rates, and Article 12(3)(b) only requires identifying 'the reference database against which input data has been checked,' which a version/commit identifier plus periodic full-state snapshots satisfies without the per-event cryptographic overhead. A scoped-down prototype (version stamp per event + daily immutable snapshot) would be faster to build and equally compliant.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 72\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2077 is about an \"Automated Dataset Versioning and Lineage System\" that would:\n- Track which specific version of a reference database was used during inference/training runs\n- Integrate data versioning tools into the deployment pipeline\n- Create immutable snapshots cryptographically tied to each logged event ID\n- Claims to directly satisfy Article 12(3)(b)\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Data versioning is a well-established field (DVC, LakeFS, Delta Lake, etc.). The underlying technology exists and is mature.\n- Cryptographic hashing of snapshots tied to event IDs is straightforward engineering — hash the dataset snapshot at ingestion time, store the hash alongside the log entry. This is technically sound and not novel in a way that introduces risk.\n- \"Immutable snapshots\" — this requires storage infrastructure but is standard practice (S3 with versioning, or object stores with WORM policies).\n- Integration into deployment pipeline: This is where it gets more complex. You need hooks at every point where the model reads reference data to capture which version was active. For a system that queries an external database (as implied by Article 12(3)(b) — \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\"), you'd need to capture the state/version of that database at query time. This is more nuanced than simple training-data versioning because in biometric identification systems, the reference database may be continuously updated (new enrollments, deletions).\n- The \"cryptographically tied\" language adds a layer — computing a hash of an entire large reference database at every inference event would be prohibitively expensive if done naively. You'd need incremental hashing or Merkle-tree-style approaches to make this performant. This is doable but not trivial.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The core concept (tag which DB version was active during a query) is prototypable quickly — you can wrap the database access layer with a version stamp.\n- However, \"cryptographically tied immutable snapshots\" for potentially large biometric reference databases at every inference event introduces significant engineering complexity. You don't want to snapshot an entire biometric template store on every match event. A more practical approach would be to record a version identifier (e.g., a commit hash of the DB state) rather than a full cryptographic snapshot per event.\n- The proposal as stated conflates two things: (1) recording which version was used (simple, fast to prototype), and (2) creating immutable snapshots tied to each event ID (more expensive, more complex). For prototyping purposes, you could achieve compliance with approach (1) alone — the \"reference database\" is identified by its version/commit, not necessarily a full cryptographic snapshot per event.\n\n**Regulatory fit:**\n- Article 12(3)(b) says: \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" This is asking you to log WHICH database was used — i.e., identify it. A version identifier (name, commit hash, timestamp of state) would satisfy this. The proposal's \"cryptographic\" framing goes beyond what the regulation requires but isn't harmful per se.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), the reference database is critical and this logging makes sense.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea is technically viable and addresses a real compliance gap. The technology exists. However, the proposal as written over-specifies the implementation (\"cryptographically tied immutable snapshots\") in a way that could slow prototyping significantly without clear regulatory benefit — the regulation asks for identification of the reference database, not cryptographic proof of its exact state at each event. A lighter-weight version (version stamping + periodic full snapshots) would be faster to prototype and still compliant.\n\nFrom my vantage point: The idea is sound, technically feasible with existing tools, and addresses a genuine requirement. But the specific implementation path described (per-event cryptographic snapshots of large databases) introduces unnecessary prototyping friction. I'd approve the concept but note that the implementation needs scoping down for practical prototyping speed.\n\nDecision: Approve — it's technically viable, uses mature tooling, addresses a real Article 12(3)(b) requirement, and is prototypable in reasonable time even if the \"cryptographic snapshot per event\" framing should be relaxed to \"version identifier per event with periodic full snapshots.\"\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72. The core concept is solid and uses mature technology, but the specific implementation as described has a performance/scalability concern for large reference databases at high inference rates that would slow prototyping. It's not a show-stopper (you can scope it down) but it's not a \"drop-in\" solution either.\n",
