f existing R&D infrastructure**: The proposal explicitly builds on \"experiment tracking tools\" that an R&D lab would already have (or be in the process of adopting). It's not proposing a from-scratch system; it's adding a documentation-generation layer to infrastructure we're already investing in. The \"template-driven documentation generator\" triggered by model merges is architecturally lightweight relative to, say, building an entirely new logging framework. This is efficient use of what we have.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Every successful model merge\" as the trigger: this could generate a lot of intermediate documentation that's noisy. But from a resource standpoint, automated generation has near-zero marginal cost, so even if there's some noise, it's not a significant drain compared to manual updating.\n- Scope: It addresses Recital 71 (technical documentation) but doesn't directly address Article 12(1)'s automatic logging of events or Article 12(2)/(3)'s specific logging capabilities. However, the proposal positions itself as addressing the \"living regulatory document\" aspect, which is complementary to but distinct from the runtime logging requirements. It's not trying to solve everything—just the documentation sync problem—and that scoping is appropriate for a single implementation effort.\n- The \"template-driven\" approach means someone needs to design and maintain templates that map to EU AI Act requirements. This is a one-time (and occasionally updated) cost, not an ongoing research tax.\n\n**Decision**: This clears my bar comfortably. It's a focused, infrastructure-level improvement that reduces recurring compliance overhead by leveraging existing tools, directly supports the lab's ability to iterate quickly on high-risk AI systems without sacrificing regulatory compliance, and is proportionate in scope. I'd approve it.\n\n**Promise score**: From my resource-allocation vantage point, this is highly attractive. It's not a moonshot; it's a practical integration that removes a known bottleneck using tools we likely already have or are building. The ROI on research throughput is clear and recurring. I'd give this around 82-85. It's strong but not a 100 because the \"template-driven\" aspect will require ongoing maintenance as regulations evolve, and there's some risk in the merge-trigger logic creating documentation sprawl that still needs curation. But those are manageable risks.\n\nI'll go with 83.\n",
