atch-specific logging required for biometric-adjacent systems (Annex III 1(a)).\n4. \"Minimizing engineering overhead for individual research teams\" — this is a strong selling point from an efficiency standpoint. Research teams can focus on science rather than compliance plumbing.\n5. The cost: building and maintaining this middleware requires dedicated engineering resources. But if we have multiple high-risk AI projects, the amortized cost per project drops significantly. This is efficient use of infrastructure.\n\nFrom my vantage point (resource allocation, funding constraints, effective use of R&D infrastructure):\n- This is a force-multiplier investment. If we have N high-risk projects, building one shared logging library is far more efficient than N separate implementations.\n- It protects existing R&D infrastructure by standardizing the compliance layer, so future projects can plug in quickly.\n- The main risk is that it becomes a single point of failure or a bottleneck for innovation if the API is too rigid. But \"modular\" mitigates this somewhat.\n- From a funding perspective, this is a one-time (plus maintenance) infrastructure investment that scales across the lab's portfolio. It's efficient capital allocation.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a resource-efficient approach to compliance that leverages shared infrastructure. The modular design addresses flexibility concerns. The main caveat is ensuring it doesn't become over-engineered or too rigid, but as stated, \"modular middleware\" with API hooks seems appropriately scoped.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: I'd say around 78-82. It's a strong efficiency play from my perspective — shared infrastructure, reduced per-project overhead, scalable. But I'm not at 90+ because the \"immutable database\" commitment is a bit heavy, and the generality of what's being logged (timestamps, input hashes, user IDs) doesn't fully cover all of Article 12(3)'s specific requirements without further specification. There's