frastructure is being built. The ROI for speed of prototyping depends heavily on how well it integrates with existing workflows vs. adding overhead to every training run.\n- The \"scientific asset\" and \"intellectual merit\" framing suggests this proposal was motivated more by academic credibility than by practical engineering needs. That's not a disqualifier per se, but it's worth noting the value proposition isn't strongly aligned with prototyping speed.\n\n**Alignment with Article 12 specifically:**\n- Article 12 is primarily about LOGGING - automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system in operation (post-deployment). The article talks about \"period of each use,\" \"reference database against which input data has been checked,\" \"input data for which search led to a match,\" and \"identification of natural persons involved in verification.\"\n- This proposal is about TRAINING provenance - linking datasets, hyperparameters, and model weights. While Recital 71 mentions \"training, testing and validation processes used\" as part of technical documentation, Article 12's operative requirements are about runtime/operational logging, not training lineage.\n- There IS overlap: the recital says technical documentation should include info on \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes.\" A provenance graph could contribute to that documentation requirement. But it's more aligned with Article 11 (technical documentation) than Article 12 (record-keeping/logging).\n\n**My assessment as a Dev Engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed:**\n- The technical concept is sound and feasible, but the scope (\"integrated system,\" \"graph-based ledger\") suggests a large build. For prototyping speed, this could be over-engineered - we might need simpler solutions (e.g., MLflow + hash verification scripts) that get us 80% of the way there in a fraction of the time.\n- The cryptographic hashing aspect adds complexity with ma