sment:\n- This is where I'm most critical. A \"unified framework\" that researchers must integrate into their pipelines adds overhead to the prototyping cycle. Every time a researcher changes their model architecture or data pipeline, they now need to ensure the logging middleware still works correctly.\n- The proposal says \"minimizing operational burden on scientific staff\" but in practice, integrating with a mandatory institutional framework often INCREASES friction during rapid iteration phases.\n- However, if done well (lightweight, plugin-based, truly modular), it could reduce long-term burden by automating what would otherwise be manual compliance work.\n- The risk is that building this \"institution-wide\" framework becomes itself a massive project that delays actual research prototyping while the infrastructure team builds and debugs it.\n\nTechnical viability:\n- The core components (timestamped event logging, input/output capture, database reference tracking) are all technically standard and well-understood.\n- The challenge is the \"unified across all pipelines\" part. This requires deep knowledge of every pipeline's architecture, which changes constantly in R&D.\n- The proposal doesn't address how it handles evolving research code - if a model goes from v1 to v2 with completely different input formats, does the logging framework break? How is backward compatibility maintained?\n- \"Automated\" capture without manual intervention is achievable for well-defined production systems but much harder for experimental/research code where schemas are in flux.\n\nFrom my development engineering perspective:\n- The technical components are viable (logging, event capture, API design) - this isn't science fiction.\n- But the scope (\"institution-wide,\" \"all pipelines\") makes this a very large undertaking with unclear ROI timeline.\n- The tension between rigid institutional infrastructure and agile research prototyping is real and unaddressed in the proposal.\n- 