acts from existing experiment tracking (MLflow/W&B), meaning it leverages infrastructure already in place, reducing implementation friction.\n- Triggering updates on model versioning events ensures temporal alignment between the system state and its documentation — critical for traceability under Article 12(2)(a) about identifying situations that may present risk.\n- Automating this reduces human error in documentation (missed fields, inconsistent formatting), which is a compliance failure mode.\n- It addresses \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\" mentioned in Recital 71 by pulling hyperparameters, dataset versions, model architecture.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n- The proposal focuses on extracting technical metadata (hyperparameters, dataset versions, architecture). Recital 71 requires MORE than that: \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system... documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" A tool that only pulls from MLflow/W&B may miss qualitative elements like risk management documentation, known limitations descriptions, capability statements.\n- The proposal says it produces a \"compliant format\" but doesn't specify how compliance with specific Annex requirements is verified. Compliance isn't just having data — it's having the right data in the right structure with appropriate depth.\n- It addresses documentation but Article 12(1) specifically requires the SYSTEM to technically allow automatic recording of events (logs). This tool is about documentation generation, not logging capability per se. Though Recital 71 ties them together.\n- The \"custom template engine\" raises a question: who maintains and validates that the templates actually map to all regulatory requirements as they evolve? If the EU publishes implementing acts or harmonised standards that refine what's needed, the template must be updated. This is an ongoing compliance maintenance burden.\n- It says \"compliant format\" — but complianc