enerating a static dossier from dev metadata.\n\nWait, let me re-read more carefully. Recital 71 says: \"This requires keeping records and the availability of technical documentation, containing information which is necessary to assess the compliance...\" and \"high-risk AI systems should technically allow for the automatic recording of events, by means of logs.\" So there are two things: (1) technical documentation kept up to date, and (2) automatic logging capability. Article 12 itself focuses on the logging capability (paragraphs 1-3). The technical documentation requirement is actually in Article 11 (Annex IV).\n\nSo this proposal is somewhat misaligned with Article 12 specifically - it's more of an Article 11 / Annex IV solution (generating the technical dossier) rather than implementing the runtime logging capability that Art. 12 mandates. However, the recital does tie them together, and the \"record-keeping\" in the title encompasses both.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The proposal claims it \"eliminates the tedious manual writing of documentation which typically slows down the final stages of prototyping.\" This is a real pain point I recognize. In our workflow, we do spend time at the end of a prototype cycle manually assembling documentation. Automating this would genuinely speed up iteration.\n- However, from a prototyping perspective, the question is: does building THIS pipeline actually help us prototype faster? The pipeline itself requires engineering investment (setting up the aggregation, template design, PDF generation). For early prototypes, this might be overkill. But for systems that are heading toward high-risk deployment, having this automated would reduce cycle time on compliance documentation.\n- The risk: if we build a rigid PDF-generation pipeline too early in our prototyping process, it could actually slow us down because we'd need to maintain the pipeline alongside iterating on the AI system itself. But as a tool for the \"final