ource project, responding to community issues, refining standards with other EU institutions — these are ongoing resource commitments that go well beyond what Article 12 requires of them.\n- The proposal frames this as expanding \"scientific impact\" and positioning the lab as a \"primary leader in regulatory-technical standardization.\" This is ambition-driven framing rather than efficiency-driven. The lab's core mission is developing AI systems, not becoming a standards body.\n- Collaboration with \"other EU institutions to refine these standards\" is open-ended and potentially very resource-intensive. Standards development across multiple institutions involves coordination costs, alignment on scope, governance decisions — all of which consume senior engineer and PM time.\n- The lab's scarce implementation resources should be directed toward making their OWN systems compliant first. Building a universal standard for the \"broader research community\" is a scope expansion that risks diluting focus from core compliance work.\n- There's no mention of how this connects to existing infrastructure or whether it leverages what the lab already has. It reads as a new project with its own deliverables (spec, reference implementation, GitHub repo, multi-institution collaboration).\n- The funding constraint concern: maintaining an open-source standard is a long-tail commitment. Who maintains it after initial release? What happens when Article 12 interpretations evolve? This creates ongoing obligations that may not fit within the lab's budget cycle.\n\n**Balancing:**\nThe core insight from my vantage point: if the lab must build logging for its own high-risk systems anyway, structuring that work in a clean, reusable way (schema + API) is good engineering practice and low marginal cost. However, the proposal as written goes well beyond \"build our logging cleanly\" into \"become a standards body for the EU research community.\" That expansion — open-source release, multi-institution collaboration, positioning as \"primary leader in regulatory-technical standardization\" — represents a significant scope creep that commits scarce resources to an external-facing mission that isn't the lab's core mandate.\n\nThe proposal conflates two things: (1) building good internal logging infrastructure for compliance (which we should do anyway), and (2) creating a public standards project with ongoing community maintenance (which is a different kind of commitment). As written, it commits to both simultaneously without clear resourcing.\n\nI think this falls just below my approval bar from a resource-allocation standpoint. The ambition is real but the scope as stated — open-source, multi-institution collaboration, reference implementation for \"the broader research community\" — demands ongoing dedicated resources that compete with core R&D work. A lab can absolutely build clean internal logging; what's being proposed here is becoming a standards organization on the side.\n\nHowever, I should note: if reframed as \"develop our Article 12 compliance logging in a well-documented, API-first way so it's internally reusable and potentially shareable,\" that would be much more palatable from an efficiency standpoint. But as written, it's broader than that.\n\nMy decision: reject, but with a moderate promise score because the underlying engineering principle (build logging cleanly, document it well) is sound — it's the scope expansion to open-source standards leadership that crosses my resource-allocation threshold.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 40-50. The idea has merit at its core (structured, documented logging), but the full scope as proposed is too resource-intensive relative to what we actually need for compliance, and the \"standards body\" ambition doesn't align with efficient use of our R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 42.\n",
