d maintain this?\n- The proposal doesn't mention cost estimates, timeline, or whether existing infrastructure (e.g., cloud providers' logging services) could be leveraged instead of building from scratch.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- \"Integrates directly with our existing ML frameworks\" - good, it builds on what we have rather than replacing it.\n- But \"centralized backend\" might duplicate or conflict with existing infrastructure (e.g., if we already have a logging/monitoring stack).\n- The abstraction layer approach is sound in principle but the implementation complexity could be high depending on how heterogeneous our ML frameworks are.\n\n**Article 12 compliance adequacy:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events - this addresses that.\n- Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities for specific purposes (risk identification, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring) - the SDK \"automatically captures system events, timestamps, and input hashes\" but doesn't explicitly address the event categories in 2(a), 2(b), 2(c).\n- Article 12(3) is specifically for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics/identification) requiring specific data points. A generic SDK might not capture those specific elements without project-specific configuration.\n- The proposal mentions \"input hashes\" but Article 12(3)(c) requires the actual input data for which a search led to a match, not just hashes.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe direction is right - a unified approach to compliance logging is more resource-efficient than per-project solutions. However, from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource allocation and infrastructure efficiency:\n\n1. The proposal is somewhat over-specified for the early stage. \"Asynchronous API calls,\" \"centralized backend,\" \"abstraction layer\" - these are architectural decisions that should come after scoping how many systems need this and what the actual compliance surface looks like.\n