vs. many), but the implementation specifics concern me from an infrastructure cost perspective and there's some risk of over-engineering before we've fully scoped the compliance surface.\n\nActually, let me reconsider my threshold. The instruction says \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it.\" This is a real commitment. Is this the best use of our engineering resources? If we have 3-5 high-risk systems, yes, a shared SDK is more efficient than 3-5 separate implementations. But if we have only 1-2, maybe not yet.\n\nThe proposal doesn't specify how many projects it covers or what the current state is. It assumes \"all high-risk AI projects\" as a given. I'll approve because the unified approach is genuinely more resource-efficient than alternatives, and it leverages existing ML frameworks (good infrastructure reuse), but my promise score will reflect the uncertainty about scale and the risk of premature architectural commitment.\n\nI'll go with approve at around 62-65. The direction is right, the efficiency argument holds for a lab with multiple high-risk systems, but there are concerns about implementation scope and whether lighter-weight approaches might achieve the same compliance outcome with less upfront investment.\n",
