out full PKI digital signatures. However, it's not wrong — just potentially over-engineered for an initial prototype.\n\nDecision: I'm leaning toward \"approve\" because the technical direction is sound and directly addresses a regulatory requirement. The core functionality is prototypably simple even if the full vision (digital signatures, indelibility) takes longer. It's a legitimate engineering task with clear scope. But the promise score should reflect that the \"indelible digital signature\" framing adds unnecessary complexity to initial prototyping.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My domain is technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping. The proposal as written:\n- Is technically feasible (nothing here is cutting-edge or uncertain)\n- Has a clear regulatory driver (Article 12(3)(d))\n- But over-scopes for initial prototyping by including PKI/digital signatures/indelibility when basic authenticated logging would satisfy the requirement\n\nThe technical approach isn't wrong, just potentially heavy. I'll approve it because it's technically sound and addresses a real need, but give it a moderate promise score reflecting that the \"digital signature + indelible\" framing could slow initial prototyping without adding much compliance value over standard audit logging with SSO authentication.\n\nPromise: around 62-68. It's viable and needed, but the implementation approach as described is heavier than necessary for a first prototype.\n",
