t system) from just Git/MLflow/dataset manifests is an underestimation. Also, the trigger mechanism is too narrow for lifetime tracking.\n\nHowever, as a starting point and prototyping target, it's solid. The technical approach is sound, uses existing tools, and addresses a real pain point (researchers spending time writing static reports). A prototype could be built quickly to validate whether the auto-generated output meets even 60-70% of what's needed, which would be valuable.\n\nI'll lean toward approve because:\n1. It's technically viable with existing infrastructure\n2. Fast to prototype (structured data → template-based document generation)\n3. Addresses a genuine regulatory need (up-to-date documentation per Recital 71)\n4. Low dependency on building new capture systems\n5. The narrow trigger is a fixable implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw\n\nBut I won't give it the highest score because:\n- Scope is somewhat underestimated for full compliance\n- \"Readable regulatory reports\" from raw logs has quality risks\n- Doesn't clearly address Art 12(3) specific requirements (reference databases, input data matches, verification personnel IDs)\n\nI'd say around 72-78 promise. Let me settle on 75. It's a good prototyping target with clear technical feasibility, but the gap between \"parses metadata into reports\" and \"fully compliant Art 12 documentation\" is real and could slow things down.\n",
