hniques to a new context rather than pushing the knowledge frontier significantly. I'd give it moderate promise - it's not nothing, but it's not cutting-edge science either. The framing \"turns a compliance burden into a novel scientific contribution\" is a bit of a stretch unless the project produces genuinely new theoretical results.\n\nI'll rate this around 50-58. It has some intellectual merit in identifying the right intersection problem, but the specific techniques mentioned (DP on logs, hashing) are well-trodden and don't clearly push the frontier. I'll approve it as it does identify a genuine research gap at the intersection of privacy theory and AI governance, but with moderate confidence in its frontier-pushing potential.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My bar is: knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact. \n\n- Knowledge frontier: Moderate. The specific combination of DP + hashing for regulatory logs hasn't been deeply explored in the context of EU AI Act compliance specifically, but the underlying math isn't new.\n- Intellectual merit: Low-to-moderate as framed. \"Apply X and Y to Z\" without articulating what's genuinely open or hard is a red flag for pure research merit.\n- Broader impact: Moderate at best. A paper on \"we applied DP to AI compliance logs\" would be publishable but not transformative.\n\nI think I'll reject this. The proposal as stated doesn't demonstrate sufficient intellectual depth or frontier-pushing potential. It's more of an engineering application than a research contribution. The framing of turning \"compliance burden into novel scientific contribution\" is aspirational rather than grounded in specific open problems. A stronger version would identify the fundamental impossibility boundaries (e.g., what level of traceability is achievable under DP constraints) or develop new cryptographic primitives for this purpose.\n\nActually, I'll approve but with a lower score. The problem space IS genuinely at an interesting intersection, and there ARE real open questions about how much privacy you can impose on compliance logs while still satisfying regulatory traceability requirements. That's a legitimate research question. But the proposal as stated doesn't clearly articulate what's novel beyond \"apply DP and hashing.\" I'll approve with a score around 52-55.\n\nLet me settle: I'll reject it. From my pure research vantage point, this reads as an engineering project dressed up as science. The techniques are known, the application is straightforward (or in some cases conceptually mismatched - DP on individual log entries), and the \"novel scientific contribution\" claim isn't supported by identifying specific open problems or theoretical gaps. A researcher would want to see: what's the specific mathematical question? What's the impossibility result you're aiming for? What new primitive are you developing? None of that is here.\n\nFinal decision: reject, score around 42-48.\n",
