        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2473 is about \"Efficient Observability for Large Scale AI Models\" - investigating methods to log high-dimensional system states without prohibitive computational or storage overhead, using sketching algorithms and compressed sensing.\n\nLet me evaluate this from my vantage point:\n\n**Technical viability concerns:**\n- Sketching algorithms (Count-Min sketches, HyperLogLog, etc.) and compressed sensing are well-studied areas in computer science. They have known properties - they're lossy by design. The EU AI Act requires that logs enable \"identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk\" and facilitating post-market monitoring. If you compress the data, you lose fidelity. You might miss the very edge cases that matter for compliance.\n- For Article 12(3) specifically (biometric identification systems), the requirements are quite concrete: start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, natural persons involved in verification. These are relatively low-dimensional metadata fields. Sketching/compressed sensing is overkill and potentially counterproductive here - you don't need to compress \"start time = 2025-01-15T14:32:01Z\" or \"database = Eurodac v2.3\".\n- For the broader logging requirements in Article 12(1)-(2), the question is whether model activations really need to be logged at full fidelity. But here's the key issue: if you use lossy compression, you can't guarantee that a specific event would have been captured. This creates a compliance gap.\n- The \"scientific contribution\" framing is attractive but doesn't address the core engineering problem: we need a system that reliably captures compliance-relevant events with sufficient fidelity for audit, at scale. That's more of an infrastructure/architecture problem than a research problem.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping concerns:**\n- This is framed as a \"research project\" - experiments, benchmarking. As a development engineer focused on shipping prototypes that meet regulatory requirements, I need working solutions NOW (the AI Act has phased implementation timelines). A research exploration with open-ended outcomes doesn't give me something to build into our systems by the compliance deadline.\n- Sketching and compressed sensing have known limitations: they're designed for approximate counting/estimation, not for reconstructing specific events when needed. If a regulator asks \"what happened at timestamp X?\", you need that exact record, not an approximation.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what \"high-dimensional system states\" means concretely for our systems. Without concrete use cases tied to the Article 12 requirements, this is abstract research rather than targeted engineering.\n\n**Where it has merit:**\n- There IS a real technical challenge in logging at scale for large models (inference logs can be enormous). Storage and I/O overhead are genuine concerns.\n- The idea of benchmarking against standard logging has some value - understanding the actual cost profile helps inform architecture decisions.\n- If we could use lossy methods for \"background\" telemetry while maintaining exact records for compliance-critical events, that hybrid approach could be valuable.\n\n**My overall assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed:\n- The proposal is too research-oriented and abstract. It doesn't map clearly to specific Article 12 requirements we need to implement.\n- Lossy compression (sketching, compressed sensing) fundamentally conflicts with the traceability guarantee the Act requires. You can't \"approximately log\" a compliance event.\n- However, the underlying problem (logging overhead at scale) is real and worth solving - just not necessarily through this specific research lens.\n- For prototyping purposes, I'd rather have a concrete logging architecture that meets Article 12 requirements with acceptable performance characteristics, validated against our actual workloads, rather than an open-ended exploration of compression techniques.\n\nI think this falls into a \"reject\" for me but with moderate promise. The problem space is valid, the approach is questionable for compliance purposes, and the research framing doesn't give me a shippable prototype path. Maybe a 35-40 range - I acknowledge the technical challenge is real, but the specific approach (lossy compression of compliance-critical data) and the research framing make this not actionable for my function's needs.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"maintain a traceable history\" - so they're aware it needs to be traceable. And they say \"benchmark these against standard logging.\" There's a possibility that the outcome is \"standard logging is fine, here's why\" or \"here's where you can optimize without losing compliance fidelity.\" That's actually useful engineering information.\n\nBut still, as a research project with no clear deliverable tied to our implementation timeline, and given that the lossy nature of sketching/compressed sensing creates fundamental tension with audit requirements... I'll reject it but give it moderate promise for the valid problem identification.\n\nI'll go with: reject, promise 35.\n",
