nt recording during system operation. The proposal focuses more on the documentation generation side.\n- The \"trigger upon deployment\" approach may not be sufficient for continuous operational logging required by Art 12(1).\n- It doesn't explicitly address the specific data points required under Art 12(3) for Annex III systems.\n\nBut overall, this is a high-value proposal that addresses a core compliance obligation with an automated solution that reduces human error and ensures timeliness. The Git-based approach provides good traceability. Even if it needs refinement to fully cover all of Article 12's sub-requirements, the foundational architecture described here is exactly what a compliance officer would want to see.\n\n**Decision:** I'd approve this. It directly addresses a core regulatory obligation (automatic recording/logging over lifetime) and does so through automation that reduces compliance risk. The alignment with Recital 71 is strong. Even though it could be more specific about Art 12(2)(a)-(c) and Art 12(3) specifics, the foundational system described is critical infrastructure for compliance.\n\n**Promise score:** I'd give this around 82-85. It's a strong proposal that addresses core requirements, but it conflates some documentation (Art 11) with logging (Art 12), and the deployment-trigger mechanism may not fully satisfy continuous operational logging requirements. From a pure compliance survival standpoint, having automated, always-current technical records is extremely valuable. But it's not a complete solution for Article 12 as written - it needs to be supplemented with runtime event logging capabilities specifically.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
