g requirement - a necessary but not the most complex part of the AI Act compliance picture.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal explicitly mentions \"ensuring our existing infrastructure remains highly efficient\" - it's positioned as enhancing what we have rather than replacing it.\n- A script-based validator is low-cost to develop and maintain, especially if we already generate logs in some form for our AI systems during testing.\n- The \"real-time during prototype testing phase\" aspect means it integrates into our existing development workflow rather than creating a separate parallel process.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Real-time\" scanning might be slightly overstated for a \"script-based validator that periodically scans.\" There's a tension between \"periodic\" and \"real-time\" in the description, but this is likely just imprecise language meaning \"as logs are generated during testing.\"\n- The scope seems appropriately narrow - focused on Article 12 specifically (timestamps, reference database entries) rather than trying to be an all-encompassing compliance suite.\n- It's a prototype-phase tool primarily, so it has clear applicability and doesn't overcommit resources for production deployment that may not yet be needed.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, cost-effective proposal that addresses a specific regulatory requirement (Article 12) using minimal new infrastructure (scripts on existing logs). The efficiency argument is strong: catching missing timestamps or reference database entries during prototype testing is dramatically cheaper than discovering them during audit or post-market monitoring. It doesn't compete with scientific ambition for resources - it's the kind of lightweight compliance scaffolding that lets us focus our actual R&D budget on system development rather than regulatory remediation.\n\nThe main question is whether we already have something like this. If we're generating logs for testing