nly trigger full Article 12 logging upon entering the deployment tier.\" But Article 12(1) states \"High-risk AI systems SHALL technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" The obligation attaches to the system being high-risk, not to when it enters a \"deployment tier\" as defined internally. If the system is already high-risk (by its intended purpose), the logging requirement applies from the moment it exists as a high-risk system - not just when it's deployed. There's a risk that the lab could be in violation if they're running tests of what should be classified as high-risk systems without full logging, simply because they haven't been \"approved\" into deployment tier yet.\n- However, there IS a legitimate distinction: basic research (Article 2(6) exclusion) vs. development of a high-risk system. The Act does exclude \"scientific research and development activity.\" But the boundary can be fuzzy, especially in an R&D institution where \"research\" might actually produce deployable systems.\n- A quarterly cadence is too slow for a lab where projects evolve rapidly. Monthly or even continuous monitoring would be safer from a compliance standpoint.\n- The proposal doesn't mention who makes the classification decision, what criteria are used beyond \"intended use case and EU AI Act criteria,\" or what happens if there's disagreement about whether something is high-risk.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- This is a good governance mechanism in principle but has timing gaps that could create compliance exposure.\n- The quarterly review is a liability - if an enforcement action occurred and we showed we only reviewed quarterly, that looks like negligence rather than reasonable diligence.\n- However, having SOME formal process to classify projects is better than ad-hoc decisions, so the underlying concept supports institutional survival.\n\n**From regulatory compliance perspective:**\n- The core risk: 