ion management integration, and change detection. However, if the team has proper versioning and deployment pipelines, this could be monitored.\n- \"Aggregate and visualize anomalies from all deployed systems\" - ELK is well-suited for this. Kibana dashboards are standard.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Leveraging existing infrastructure (ELK) means the team can prototype quickly without spinning up new systems. This is a major plus for speed.\n- The scope is reasonably bounded: parse logs → detect anomalies → visualize → alert. Each piece is a well-understood engineering task.\n- However, \"all deployed systems\" implies multi-system aggregation, which adds complexity but ELK handles this natively with indices and index patterns.\n- The main prototyping risk is the mapping between Article 79(1) risk categories and actual log signals. This requires close collaboration with compliance/legal to define what constitutes a detectable \"risk situation\" in terms of system behavior. But as a prototype, you can start with a subset.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal conflates two things: the logging capability required by Article 12 (which is about the AI system itself being able to log events) and the monitoring/visualization layer that consumes those logs. The proposal focuses on the consumption side (dashboard, alerts). This is fine - it's complementary to the logging requirement rather than a substitute for it.\n- \"Real-time\" might be an overstatement of what's needed initially; near-real-time with minute-level latency would likely suffice and be easier to prototype. But this is a minor point.\n- The proposal doesn't specify how logs from different high-risk systems (with different architectures) get normalized into the ELK pipeline. This is a real engineering challenge but not a showstopper for prototyping.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is technically viable and well-suited to rapid prototyping because it builds on existing infrastructure. The core components are standard data engineering tasks (ingest, transform, visualize, alert). The main risk area is the domain-specific logic for identifying Article 79(1) risk situations, but that's a scoping problem more than a viability problem - you can prototype with a subset of detectable signals and iterate.\n\nThe fact that it uses \"existing ELK stack infrastructure\" is the key differentiator for speed. No new infra to provision, no vendor lock-in concerns, familiar tooling for the team. This should allow a working prototype in weeks rather than months.\n\nI'd rate this as a solid, technically feasible proposal with good prototyping prospects. It's not 100/100 because there are non-trivial domain-mapping challenges (translating legal risk categories into detectable log patterns) and the \"substantial modification detection\" piece is underspecified, but it clears my bar comfortably. I'd put it around 72-78.\n\nLet me settle on a score: The existing infrastructure leverages are strong, the pattern is well-known, the scope is bounded enough for fast iteration. The main risks (domain mapping, multi-system normalization) are manageable and don't block prototyping. I'll go with 75.\n",
