d (YAML/JSON frontmatter) and committed alongside model artifacts. This is a reasonable engineering pattern.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Building this pipeline is moderate effort. The doc generation tooling part (templates, CI integration) might be 2-4 weeks for a prototype.\n- However, the proposal overstates what it solves. The actual hard part of Article 12 compliance from an engineering standpoint is:\n  - Designing and implementing the event logging schema (what events to capture)\n  - Building the runtime logging infrastructure that captures those events automatically across the system's lifetime\n  - Ensuring log retention, integrity, and queryability\n  - For Annex III point 1(a): capturing specific data points (use periods, reference databases, input data matches, person identification)\n- The doc-as-code pipeline is a nice-to-have for the *presentation* layer of compliance but doesn't address the core technical challenge of automatic event recording.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal positions documentation generation as THE solution to Article 12, when it's really a supporting tool for one aspect (technical documentation currency) while the primary obligation is runtime logging infrastructure.\n- \"Without manual drafting\" is optimistic — compliance documentation needs human sign-off and domain expertise to describe capabilities/limitations accurately.\n- The promise of reducing \"administrative overhead\" is valid but the engineering investment here might be better spent on the actual logging pipeline first.\n\n**Verdict:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is a reasonable engineering tool (doc-as-code) that addresses a real need (keeping technical documentation current as models evolve), but it's somewhat misaligned with the core obligation of Article 12 which is about automatic event recording at runtime. It's a supporting infrastructure piece, not the primary compliance mechanism. The technical approach is sound and prototypable quickly, but it