ervised anomaly detection on logs is well-trodden territory in DevOps/SRE (AIOps, log analysis tools like ELK, Splunk ML, etc.). The \"novel scientific contribution\" angle is questionable unless there's something genuinely new about the domain-specific features of AI system compliance logs versus generic system logs.\n- The ambition level seems moderate — it's not a moonshot, but it's also not trivially easy. It requires domain expertise in both AI safety (what constitutes a \"risk trigger\" under Article 79(1)) and data science.\n- Funding constraint: if the lab is already stretching to meet compliance deadlines for Article 12 implementation, spinning up a parallel research project that depends on having sufficient historical log data creates a timing dependency. You need months of logs before you can meaningfully train any monitor.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The critical dependency: this project requires the lab to first fully implement Article 12 logging (Article 12(1) mandates automatic recording over the lifetime). Until that's operational and has accumulated enough data, this research project is idle.\n- If the lab already has ML pipeline infrastructure, GPU compute for training anomaly detectors, and data engineering teams, then the marginal cost of this project is relatively low.\n- The \"reference database\" and structured nature of Article 12(3) logs (for Annex III point 1(a) systems) actually makes the data more amenable to analysis — it's structured, not free-text.\n\n**My overall assessment:**\nThe idea has genuine merit in that it extracts research value from a mandatory compliance investment. The efficiency argument is strong: you're paying for logging infrastructure anyway, and mining it adds analytical capability without new data collection costs. However, the \"novel scientific contribution\" framing is inflated — anomaly detection on logs is established methodology. The real value is operational (early warning of drif