ints perspective, this is cheap to build (a few engineer-days for templates) but saves potentially significant costs later when you'd have to add logging retroactively or deal with non-compliant prototypes that can't be deployed.\n- It protects scientific ambition by reducing friction: researchers start with the right structure and don't get blocked mid-project realizing they need to add logging infrastructure.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n- \"All new high-risk experiments\" — this could become a rigid gatekeeper if not properly maintained. If the boilerplate becomes stale or overly prescriptive, it could slow down genuinely novel research that doesn't fit the template neatly.\n- The identity management module for human verification (Article 12(3)(d)) is specific to Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics). For other high-risk categories, some of these modules might be unnecessary overhead in the scaffold. But since it says \"modular templates,\" this could be handled by including/excluding modules per project type.\n- There's a maintenance cost: as regulations evolve or as the lab's infrastructure changes, the templates need updating. But this is a minor ongoing cost compared to the one-time savings.\n\nOverall assessment from my vantage point: This is a highly efficient use of limited resources. It's a small, well-scoped investment that multiplies across all future projects. It aligns with effective use of existing infrastructure (Cookiecutter is standard tooling, not novel R&D). It reduces the risk of expensive late-stage compliance fixes. It doesn't overreach — it's scaffolding, not a full system. The main risk is maintenance and rigidity, but those are manageable.\n\nI'd rate this fairly highly from my perspective. It's exactly the kind of \"small investment, large compounding return\" proposal that an efficiency-focused director should welcome. It doesn't require significant funding — it's engineering time, not lab equipment or data purchases. It enables scientific amb