n- Effective use of existing infrastructure: Integrates into existing model serving frameworks rather than requiring new infrastructure. The centralized database is the main new component but it serves all systems.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from a resource-efficiency standpoint. It's a build-once-use-many approach that leverages existing infrastructure, eliminates per-system compliance costs, and frees researcher time. The main cost is the initial engineering effort to build the middleware and the ongoing maintenance of the centralized database, but these are amortized across all high-risk systems. The \"compliance by design\" framing also reduces long-term regulatory risk which protects funding continuity.\n\nI would approve this. It's well-scoped, efficient, and directly addresses a real compliance requirement while maximizing reuse of existing infrastructure. Promise score: I'd put this around 82-85. It's strong but not perfect — the immutable centralized database is a non-trivial commitment, and \"intercept all inference calls\" could have performance implications that need validation. But from a resource-allocation perspective, it's clearly superior to per-system approaches.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
