utput (draft dossiers for human review) keeps it practical — not a moonshot, but a productivity tool.\n\nArguments AGAINST (reject):\n- \"R&D project\" framing: Is this actually R&D or is it an engineering/tooling task? If it's presented as R&D, it might consume research-grade resources (senior ML researchers, compute budgets for training/fine-tuning) on what could be a more modest software engineering effort. There's a risk of over-engineering.\n- \"Small team\" — how small? Even 2-3 people for 6-12 months is significant when we have competing scientific priorities. The ROI depends heavily on how many high-risk systems we actually deploy per year. If it's only 2-3 per year, the manual cost might be lower than building and maintaining this tool.\n- LLM-based agents for regulatory documentation carry accuracy/compliance risk. A hallucinated or mischaracterized description in a compliance dossier could be worse than no auto-generation. This adds QA overhead that might erode the time savings.\n- The \"research opportunity\" framing is somewhat of a stretch. Mapping code artifacts to regulatory fields is more of an NLP/information-extraction problem than a fundamental research contribution. Calling it \"an innovation in AI explainability and transparency\" overstates what's happening — we're formatting existing information, not making the system more explainable per se.\n- Maintenance burden: as our systems evolve, the mapping between artifacts and regulatory fields changes. The tool needs ongoing upkeep.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound from a resource-efficiency standpoint — if we have recurring documentation obligations across multiple systems, automating the draft generation saves real engineering hours. Leveraging existing infrastructure (code repos, training logs) rather than building new pipelines aligns with my domain concern. The \"small team\" and \"draft for human review\" framing shows appropriate scope awareness.\n\nHowever, I'm somewhat wary o