ent. It addresses the logging component but not the full record-keeping obligation.\n\n**Assessment from compliance perspective:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses Art. 12(1) - automatic recording of events\n- Standardized approach reduces risk of gaps across projects (important for institutional survival and consistent compliance)\n- Using existing tools (ELK stack) is practical and cost-effective, reducing implementation burden on scarce resources\n- \"Integrated into base AI templates\" ensures new systems inherit logging from day one, which supports the \"lifetime\" requirement\n- Prevents teams from reinventing - this reduces compliance drift, a real risk in R&D institutions\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- Doesn't explicitly address Art. 12(3) minimum requirements for biometric systems (reference database, specific input data matching records, verification personnel identification)\n- Doesn't fully address the broader technical documentation requirement from Recital 71\n- \"Critical event logs\" is somewhat vague - needs to be mapped to the specific events in Art. 12(2)(a)-(c)\n- ELK stack is fine for structured logging but may need additional tooling for the specific data requirements of biometric systems\n\nOverall: This is a solid, practical proposal that addresses the core mechanical requirement of Article 12(1). It's a foundational infrastructure piece. From a compliance officer's perspective, having a unified framework is better than ad-hoc solutions because it ensures consistent traceability and reduces the risk of some project being non-compliant due to team oversight. The use of existing tools keeps costs manageable. However, it needs to be scoped carefully to ensure it covers all sub-requirements, especially Art. 12(3) for biometric systems.\n\nThis is clearly worth approving - it's a direct, practical response to a core regulatory obligation, and the standardization approach protects institutional compliance posture. The promise score should be high but not perfect because it needs refinement to cover all specific sub-requirements (especially Art. 12(3) fields for biometrics).\n\nI'd say: approve, with a promise around 78-82. It's a strong foundational move that directly serves compliance, but as stated it's somewhat generic and would need scoping work to ensure full coverage of all Article 12 sub-requirements.\n",
