eering.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping concerns:**\n- A \"dedicated research project\" with a \"specialist team\" exploring architectures is the opposite of fast prototyping. This is an open-ended R&D exploration when the regulatory requirements are fairly specific and implementable.\n- The framing as \"publishable scientific contribution\" suggests the deliverable might be a paper rather than working, shippable code that satisfies Article 12.\n- There's no clear scope, timeline, or concrete prototype target mentioned. It's an initiative without defined acceptance criteria tied to the regulatory text.\n\n**What would actually work from my perspective:**\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), the logging requirements are specific: start/end times, reference database ID, input data matched, verifier identity. This is implementable with standard structured logging, possibly with some schema design and storage considerations. A focused engineering sprint could prototype this in weeks, not a multi-year research initiative.\n- The \"low-overhead\" concern is valid but solvable with existing techniques (async writing, sampling for non-critical events, tiered storage) without needing novel compression architectures.\n- Performance degradation from logging is a well-studied problem in systems engineering; it doesn't require \"advanced AI techniques.\"\n\n**Verdict:** This proposal over-engineers a straightforward compliance requirement into an open-ended research project with unclear deliverables and no clear path to shippable, working prototypes that satisfy Article 12. The framing as scientific discovery and prestige publication is misaligned with the actual engineering task. From my perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed, this would waste resources exploring novelty where solid engineering suffices.\n\nHowever, I should note: there IS a legitimate kernel here — logging performance overhead in production AI systems (especially large 