ice.\n\nScore 3.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common\n\nAutomated provenance pipeline is fairly common in ML MLOps. Might be interesting but not unusual. Probably 2 = interesting. Could be 2.\n\nEvidence: Auto-generating regulatory reports from experiment tracking is a known MLOps practice applied to EU AI Act documentation.\n\nScore 2.\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Automating documentation from existing repositories complies with legal requirements and does not offend sensibilities.\n\nScore 4.\n\nimplementability: degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible\nAutomated toolchain linking version control and experiment tracking to formatted reports requires significant integration effort. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nEvidence: Implementing a version-control integrated metadata extraction and report formatting system requires moderate engineering effort but uses existing tools.\n\nScore 3.\n\nReturn rating echo id P2562.\n\nNeed format: for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give ... first and then ... Probably plain text lines.\n\nI'll produce structured list.\n\n",
